2025-07-29 18:10:46 - src.vue_api_endpoints - INFO - JSON data received: {'text': '\n--- Page 1 ---\n \nNO. \nIN THE SUPREME COURT OF THE STATE OF WASHINGTON \nIN RE THE PERSONAL RESTRAINT PETITION OF \nROBERT AYERST \nPetitioner. \nON PETITION FOR REVIEW FROM DIVISION THREE OF THE \nCOURT OF APPEALS #369650-III \nAsotin Cause No. 16-1-00150-7 \nScott D. Gallina, (Former) Judge \nBRIEF OF AMICUS CURIAE THE DEFENDER INITIATIVE \nROBERT C. BORUCHOWITZ WSBA 4563 \nAttorney for AMICUS \n901 12th Avenue \nP.O. Box 222000 \nSeattle, Washington 98122 \n206-398-4151\nPRV41136SC\nFILED \nSUPREME COURT \nSTATE OF WASHINGTON \n71212021 11:31 AM \nBY SUSAN L. CARLSON \nCLERK \n99941-4\n\n--- Page 2 ---\ni \nTable of Contents \nTable of Authorities   \n \n \n \n \n \nii \nI. Interest of Amicus The Defender Initiative  \n \n \n1 \nII. Introduction                                                                                     3 \nIII. Statement of the Case                                                                     6 \nIV. Argument  \n \n \n \n \n \n \n 9 \nA. Summary  \n \n \n \n \n \n 9 \nB. The Appointed Counsel Did Not Comply with the Required \nCourt rules on Indigent Defense Representation              10 \nC. The Lack of Regulatory Review Resulted in Not Discovering \nPast Deficiencies in the Lawyer’s Public Defense Practice 13 \nD. It appears that Mr. Van Idour violated the misdemeanor of \npracticing law without a license.                                       14 \nE. This Court’s Precedent Requires Reversal \n \n  17 \nF. Conclusion \n \n \n \n \n \n  19 \nV. Appendix                                    \n \n \n \n \nA \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n--- Page 3 ---\nii \nTable of Authorities \nCases \nCity of Seattle v. Ratliff, 100 Wash.2d 212 (1983) ...................... 5,17,18,19 \nDavison v. State, 196 Wn.2d 285, 297 (2020), as amended on denial of \nreconsideration (Oct. 20, 2020) .................................................... 4,5,12 \nState v. Hunt, 75 Wn. App. 795, 803, 880 P.2d 96, 100 (1994), citing \nDauphin Cty. Bar Ass\'n v. Mazzacaro, 465 Pa. 545, 551, 351 A.2d 229, \n232 (1976).......................................................................................... 14 \nHurrell-Harring v. State ........................................................................... 2 \nMatter of Ayerst, 486 P.3d 943 (Wash. Ct. App. 2021) ................ 3,4,5,8,17 \nMatter of Simmons, 190 Wn.2d 374, 387 (2018). ..................................... 6 \nMt. Vernon v. Weston, 68 Wn. App. 411(1992) ........................................ 2 \nSolina v. United States, 709 F.2d 160, 167 (2d Cir. 1983), ..................... 17 \nState v. A.N.J., 168 Wn.2d 91 (2010) .................................................. 4,10 \nState v. Ayerst, 8 Wn. App. 2d 1028 (2019) (unpublished opinion) ........... 8 \nState v. Yishmael, 195 Wn.2d 155, 177 (2020) ............................... 4,14,15 \nStrickland v. Washington, 466 U.S. 668, 688 (1984) ........................... 3,12 \nStatutes \nRCW 2.38.180(1)(b) .............................................................................. 16 \nRCW 2.48.180(2)(a) .............................................................................. 15 \nRCW 2.48.180(3). .................................................................................. 14 \nOther Authorities \nAnswer to Disciplinary Board, No.19#00008, filed June 6, 2019 ........ 9, 16 \nEmail from Asotin County Clerk, June 18, 2021 .................................... 12 \nFormal Complaint, Disciplinary Board of the Washington Supreme Court, \nIn re Robert Jerry Van Idour, Proceeding No. 19#00008, April 19, \n2019. .............................................................................................. 6,8,9 \nNLADA,“The Guarantee of Counsel: Advocacy & Due Process in Idaho’s \nTrial Courts,” ..................................................................................... 13 \nRules \nAPR 3 (c) ................................................................................................. 7 \nAPR 3(c) ................................................................................................ 16 \nAPR 8 .................................................................................................... 18 \nAPR 8(c). ............................................................................................... 16 \nAPR 9(D)(2) .......................................................................................... 18 \n ..................................................................................................................  \nCr.R. 3.1 Stds ................................................................................. 3,11,12 \nCriminal Rule on Standards for Indigent Defense ..................................... 9 \n\n--- Page 4 ---\niii \nCrR 3.1(d)(4) ......................................................................................... 12 \nRPC 5.5 (c)(1). ....................................................................................... 16 \nStandard 14.1 ................................................................................... 11, 12 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n--- Page 5 ---\n1 \nI. \nInterest of Amicus The Defender Initiative \nThe Defender Initiative began in 2008 and is a law school-based \nproject aimed at providing better representation for people accused of \ncrimes and facing loss of their liberty in criminal, juvenile, and other court \nproceedings and in the process increasing fairness in and respect for the \ncourts. The Initiative is part of the Fred T. Korematsu Center for Law and \nEquality at Seattle University School of Law, whose mission is to advance \njustice and equality through a unified vision that combines research, \nadvocacy, and education. The first major projects of the Initiative included \na report on misdemeanor practice in conjunction with the National \nAssociation of Criminal Defense Lawyers entitled “Minor Crimes, \nMassive Waste”, and a project funded by the Foundation to Promote Open \nSociety to increase access to counsel in misdemeanor courts in several \nstates.  For the past several years, the Initiative has worked with The Sixth \nAmendment Center on evaluations of public defense in several states, \nincluding current projects in Illinois and New Hampshire.  This work is \nfunded by a grant from the United States Department of Justice. \nThe Defender Initiative has a deep interest and significant \nexperience in right to counsel issues.  The Director of the Defender \nInitiative, Robert C. Boruchowitz, has worked on defender standards \nnationally and in Washington State for more than 30 years. He is an \n\n--- Page 6 ---\n2 \nemeritus member of the Washington State Bar Council on Public Defense, \nfor which he chairs the Standards Committee.  He has written and spoken \nwidely about the right to counsel, and was Director of The Defender \nAssociation in Seattle for 28 years and President of the Washington \nDefender Association for 20 years before joining the Seattle University \nSchool of Law faculty in January 2007.  He is a Professor from Practice \nand has taught Criminal Procedure-Adjudicative, a Right to Counsel \nseminar, and the Youth Advocacy Clinic.  He led a committee that \ndeveloped the American Council of Chief Defenders Statement on \nCaseloads and Workloads in 2007.   In 2003, he was a Soros Senior \nFellow working on issues related to right to counsel in misdemeanor \ncourts.  He was amicus counsel in City of Mt. Vernon v. Weston, 68 Wn. \nApp. 411(1992), the first published Washington appellate court opinion to \nrefer to defender standards. \nProfessor Boruchowitz has been an expert witness in systemic \nlitigation concerning effective public defense representation in \nWashington, New York, and Louisiana. The New York Supreme Court \nAppellate Division found that he qualifies as an expert “on the operation \nof indigent defense systems and the evaluation of such systems in light of \nprevailing professional standards.” Hurrell-Harring v. State, 119 A.D.3d \n1052, 1053, 990 N.Y.S.2d 286, 288 (N.Y. App. Div. 2014). \n\n--- Page 7 ---\n3 \nThe amicus incorporates by reference the full statement of interest \nin the Motion to File an Amicus Brief herein. \n \nThe Defender Initiative does not, in this brief or otherwise, \nrepresent the official views of Seattle University or its School of Law. \nII. \nIntroduction. \n \nMr. Ayerst was convicted at a trial in which he was represented by \na lawyer who was not admitted to the Washington Bar.  Matter of Ayerst, \n__Wn.App.2d__,486 P.3d 943 (Wash. Ct. App. 2021).  The Court of \nAppeals erred when it rejected his claim that the failure to provide \nlicensed counsel was structural error.  While the Court cited Strickland v. \nWashington, 466 U.S. 668, 688 (1984), it misapprehended the teaching of \nthat case concerning prevailing professional norms and its application in \nWashington.  Prevailing professional norms in Washington require \ncompliance with Cr.R. 3.1 Stds.  The unlicensed lawyer, Robert Van \nIdour, did not comply with the rule. \n \nThe Court of Appeals acknowledged that state and federal \nconstitutional protections to the right to counsel are undermined when trial \ncounsel is engaged in criminal activity. Matter of Ayerst, 486 P.3d at 945. \nBut it found that Mr. Ayerst had not provided sufficient proof of the \ncriminal conduct of his trial lawyer, speculating the lawyer, who appeared \non behalf of more than 100 accused persons in nine months, might have \n\n--- Page 8 ---\n4 \nbeen confused. (Id. at 947.)  This analysis ignored the fact that unlawful \npractice of law is a strict liability crime. State v. Yishmael, 195 Wn.2d 155, \n177 (2020). \nThe Court of Appeals ignored the teaching of State v. A.N.J., 168 \nWn.2d 91 (2010), and the history of standards adopted by this Court as \nexplained in Davison v. State, 196 Wn.2d 285, 297 (2020).   \nThe Court of Appeals found no evidence that the lawyer \nintentionally failed to comply with the Admission to Practice Rules. In \nfact, it appears that the unlicensed lawyer violated the misdemeanor of \nunlawful practice of law, which is a strict liability crime. While confusion \nmight be a defense in a bar discipline case, it does not overcome a strict \nliability criminal prosecution. The logical inference from the Court’s \nanalysis is that if the lawyer had purposely evaded Washington’s licensing \nrequirements, he would have had a conflict of interest that interfered with \nrepresentation of Mr. Ayerst.  Matter of Ayerst, 486 P.3d at 947.  \nEven though he had applied for admission to the Washington Bar, \nMr. Van Idour’s application was never finally approved. The Court \nspeculated that Mr. Van Idour could have been licensed under APR 8. But \nwhile the Court of Appeals wrote that the unlicensed lawyer was \n“supervised” by a Washington lawyer, which is required under APR 8, in \nfact the pleadings in Mr. Ayerst’s case were signed only by Mr. Van \n\n--- Page 9 ---\n5 \nIdour, without a bar number, and no Washington attorney was “of record” \nas required by APR 8.1  The rule requires that the Washington attorney be \nthe lawyer of record and responsible for the conduct of the matter. \nThe Court of Appeals also misapprehended the holding of City of \nSeattle v. Ratliff, 100 Wash.2d 212 (1983).  The Court acknowledged, \nciting Ratliff, that “because Mr. Ayerst\'s attorney was not licensed in \nWashington, he did not have ‘counsel’ as that term is defined for \nconstitutional purposes.”  Matter of Ayerst, 486 P.3d at 946.  But the Court \nveered into an analysis of federal cases about lawyers licensed in other \nstates, and did not adequately address the Ratliff holding. \n \nThis Court has responded to the well-documented problems in \npublic defense in the state by adopting court rules that require certain \nstandards to be met and certificates of compliance to be filed.  Davison, \n196 Wn.2d at 298–99.  But these rules did not protect Mr. Ayerst because \nhis unlicensed lawyer in this case did not comply with the standards or \ntimely submit certificates of compliance. \n                                               \n1 “In any such matter, litigation, or administrative proceeding, the applicant shall be \nassociated with an active lawyer member of the Bar, who shall be the lawyer of record \nand responsible for the conduct of the matter, litigation, or administrative proceeding.” \nAPR 8 (c )(2) \n \n \n\n--- Page 10 ---\n6 \n \nWhile Mr. Van Idour was admitted in Idaho, he did not comply \nwith Washington’s rules and Mr. Ayerst was deprived of a lawyer who \nmet the Standards required to represent indigent clients.   \nThis Court has made clear that the bar admission process is not a \nmeaningless formality. “The court’s ultimate responsibility in matters \nrelating to admission of attorneys is to guard the public and its confidence \nin the judicial system.” Matter of Simmons, 190 Wn.2d 374, 387 (2018).  \n \nThis Court should grant the Personal Restraint Petition. Mr. \nAyerst’s lawyer was not admitted to the bar, he likely committed unlawful \npractice of law, and the APR’s purpose of protection was thwarted.  This \nCourt’s aim of protecting public defense clients by implementing the court \nrule on standards also was thwarted. \nIII. \nStatement of the Case. \nOn or about January 16, 2017, Mr. Van Idour submitted an \napplication for admission to the state bar, pursuant to APR 3(c). 2 \nApproximately a month earlier, he had sought a public defense contract in \nAsotin County.3 On January 29, 2017, Mr. Van Idour signed an indigent \ndefense contract with Asotin County.4  The contract required him to be \n                                               \n2  Formal Complaint, Disciplinary Board of the Washington Supreme Court, In re Robert \nJerry Van Idour, Proceeding No. 19#00008, April 19, 2019. \n3 Id. \n4 Formal Complaint, In re Robert Jerry Van Idour, supra. \n\n--- Page 11 ---\n7 \nadmitted to the Washington bar. 5 Mr. Van Idour was never admitted to \npractice in Washington. 6 \n \nOn March 28, 2017,  the trial court appointed Mr. Van Idour to \nrepresent Robert Ayerst. CP 36.   \n On October 6, 2017, former Asotin County Superior Court Judge \nScott Galina signed an Order for Limited Admission to Practice that \npurported to admit Mr. Van Idour provisionally to provide indigent \ndefense services in Asotin County.  The order was dated “Nunc Pro Tunc \nto February 1, 2017.”7 \nOn or about October 26, 2017, the WSBA advised Mr. Van Idour \nthat his application for admission under APR 3 (c) was approved, \ncontingent on completing additional steps.8   \nMr. Ayerst was convicted by a jury of attempted burglary in the \nsecond degree, malicious mischief in the second degree, and bail jumping \non November 17, 2017. CP 98.   \n                                               \n5 Mr. Van Idour is now an inactive member of the Idaho State Bar. Idaho Attorney Roster \nSearch, https://isb.idaho.gov/licensing-mcle/attorney-roster-search/, last checked June 19, \n2021. Mr. Van Idour had been licensed to practice law in Idaho for approximately 30 \nyears. \n6 Complaint, supra. \n7 Id. \n8 Complaint, supra. \n\n--- Page 12 ---\n8 \nOn or about November 27, 2017, Mr. Van Idour was notified that \nhis application under APR 8(c) was denied.9 10 \n On December 18, 2017, Mr. Ayerst was sentenced to 55.5 months \nin prison. Matter of Ayerst, supra; State v. Ayerst, 8 Wn. App. 2d \n1028 (2019) (unpublished opinion).   \nOn April 19, 2019, the WSBA filed a formal complaint against Mr. \nVan Idour, alleging that he was not authorized to practice law in \nWashington as an out-of-state attorney.11. Mr. Van Idour appeared for \nmore than100 accused persons under his Asotin contract.12 \nApproximately three months after his contract began, Mr. Van \nIdour submitted an application for limited admission to the WSBA under \nAPR 8 (c), 13 which permits attorneys working in public defense and who \nare eligible to apply for admission under APR 3, upon application and \napproval, to practice law in Washington but with two important conditions \nthat Mr. Van Idour did not meet.  Under APR 8 (c), that lawyer must be \nassociated with an active member of the Washington bar “who shall be the \nlawyer of record and responsible for the conduct of the matter”. The \n                                               \n9 Complaint, supra. \n10 Id.  As documented in the email in the appendix below, Mr. Van Idour on October 30, \n2017, told the Asotin County superior court administrator that he did not have a bar \nnumber and he had to answer an 80-question open book exam. \n11 Complaint, Disciplinary Board of the Washington Supreme Court, In re Robert Jerry \nVan Idour, Proceeding No. 19#00008, April 19, 2019. \n12 Id. \n13 Id. \n\n--- Page 13 ---\n9 \napplicant must apply for and take the first available bar examination after \nthe date the applicant was granted authorization to practice under the rule \nor have filed an application for admission by motion of Uniform Bar \nExam score transfer.  APR 8 (c).  \nMr. Van Idour admitted to the Washington State Bar (WSBA) that \nhe was not a member of the WSBA but said whether he was admitted to \nthe practice of law “and/or did not gain admission during the term of the \ncontract is a question of law which” he was not required to admit or \ndeny.14 He asserted that “the court repeatedly appointed” him “to represent \nspecific indigent defendants” 15and that based on that he “believed this \nwas a proper admission to practice for those matters.”16 He claimed that he \nhad been admitted “on a pro hac vice basis”.17 Mr. Van Idour was never \nadmitted to practice law in Washington under any rule.18   \nIV. \nArgument \n \nA. \nSummary \n \n \nThe Admission to Practice Rules and the Criminal Rule on \nStandards for Indigent Defense require that counsel be admitted and that \n                                               \n14 Answer to Disciplinary Board, No.19#00008, filed June 6, 2019. \n15 The judge who appointed him is no longer on the bench and is facing multiple felony \ncharges. See, “Gallina Asking for Another Delay to Trial”, Lewiston Tribune,  March 3, \n2021, at https://lmtribune.com/northwest/gallina-asking-for-another-delay-to-\ntrial/article_9e29af2e-0fee-58da-82bb-a915ffbe8669.html.  \n16 Answer, supra. \n17 Id. \n18 Complaint, supra. \n\n--- Page 14 ---\n10 \ncounsel file quarterly certificates of compliance. The appointed counsel \nwas never admitted and filed only one certificate of compliance in ten \nmonths, after Mr. Ayerst’s trial. His attempt to be admitted pro hac vice \nmonths after his appointment to represent Mr. Ayerst did not comply with \nthe rules and was rejected by the State Bar. To permit a conviction under \nsuch circumstances to stand would drastically undermine the APR and the \nStandards, which are designed to protect the public. \nB. \nThe Appointed Counsel Did Not Comply With the \nRequired Court Rules on Indigent Defense Representation \n \nIn reversing a juvenile conviction because of ineffective assistance \nof counsel, this Court discussed the statute that requires each county to \nadopt public defense standards. State v. A.N.J., 168 Wn.2d 91, 113 (2010). \nThe Court rejected the argument that the WSBA standards had been \nincorporated into the statutes, noting that the law in 2004 when A.N.J. \npled guilty provided that the bar association endorsed standards “may \nserve as guidelines to local legislative authorities in adopting standards.”  \nThe Court said it was not adopting the standards, but they could be \nconsidered in determining effective assistance of counsel.  A.N.J., 168 \nWn.2d 91, 110.  But in 2005, the statute was changed to read “should \nserve as guidelines to local legislative authorities in adopting standards.” \nRCW 10.101.030. \n\n--- Page 15 ---\n11 \nAnd effective October 1, 2012, the Court adopted standards and a \nrequired “Certification of Appointed Counsel of Compliance with \nStandards.” CrR 3.1 Stds. The certification states in part: “I meet the \nminimum basic professional qualifications in Standard 14.1.”  Standard \n14.1 provides in part that attorneys providing defense services shall \n“Satisfy the minimum requirements for practicing law in Washington as \ndetermined by the Washington Supreme Court;” \nAs then Chief Justice Madsen wrote in July 2012, \nwe have learned that in areas of our state, the promise of \naccess to effective assistance of counsel promised by our \nconstitution has not been met and that we needed to take \nnew measures to fully enact the rights and protections due \nto those who enter the criminal justice system.19 \n \nShe added that the changes regarding certification and caseload guidelines \n“are necessary to address documented ongoing flaws in indigent defense \nprograms throughout the state.’20 \nWhen Mr. Van Idour represented Mr. Ayerst without complying \nwith the Standards and the court rule, he thwarted this Court’s efforts to \nprotect Mr. Ayerst’s right to counsel. Reversing the convictions in this \ncase will make it clear that the Court intends to enforce the Standards it \nhas adopted. \n                                               \n19 Chief Justice Barbara Madsen, “Enacting Standards for public defenders is a difficult \nbut necessary balancing act”, Full Court Press, July 2012. \n20 Id. \n\n--- Page 16 ---\n12 \nThe “prevailing professional norms” referenced in Strickland, \nsupra, in Washington are guided by the Standards. \nThis Court in Davison discussed the Standards and emphasized the \ncourt rule that provides: \nBefore appointing a lawyer for an indigent person, or at the \nfirst appearance of the lawyer in the case, the court shall \nrequire the lawyer to certify to the court that he or she \ncomplies with the applicable Standards for Indigent \nDefense Services. CrR 3.1(d)(4).    \n \nDavison v. State, 196 Wn.2d 285, 298–99. \n \nThere is nothing in the record to show that the trial judge complied \nwith this court rule.  But it is unlikely, as Mr. Van Idour did not comply \nwith CrR3.1 STDS that requires a quarterly certification of compliance \nwith the standards for public defense adopted by the Supreme Court.  \nFirst, he only filed one certification during 2017 in Asotin County, on \nDecember 1, 2017, on which he circled “3rd” before “Calendar Quarter”.21  \nSecond, he certified that he met the minimum basic professional \nqualifications in Standard 14.1, but that was not true, as 14.1 provides: \nStandard 14.1. In order to assure that indigent accused receive the \neffective assistance of counsel to which they are constitutionally \nentitled, attorneys providing defense services shall meet the \nfollowing minimum professional qualifications: A. Satisfy the \nminimum requirements for practicing law in Washington as \ndetermined by the Washington Supreme Court; … \n \n                                               \n21 See email from Asotin County Clerk, June 18, 2021, and copy of the Certification, in \nAppendix. \n\n--- Page 17 ---\n13 \nThird, he left blank the first line on the certification regarding what \npercentage of his total practice was devoted to indigent defense cases. And, \nas with every other pleading he filed in this case, he left the WSBA# blank, \nas he did not have one. \nC. \nThe Lack of Regulatory Review Resulted in Not \nDiscovering Past Deficiencies in the Lawyer’s Public Defense \nPractice \n \nWithout some regulatory review of a bar applicant, past indicators \nof problems are overlooked. In 2010, The National Legal Aid and \nDefender Association (NLADA) wrote a critical evaluation of public \ndefense in Idaho, and highlighted Nez Perce County as an example of the \nproblems.22  Mr. Van Idour’s firm had the county’s public defense \ncontract, both for direct representation and for overseeing the conflict \nlawyers. The  report found “near unanimous agreement” among the \ncounty’s judges “that there is not a single thing about the public defense \nsystem in their county that they believe is worth replicating anywhere \nelse.”23  \nIn a paragraph that underscores the importance of the Washington  \nStandards designed to protect defender clients, NLADA reported: \n                                               \n22 NLADA, “The Guarantee of Counsel: Advocacy & Due Process in Idaho’s Trial \nCourts,” \nhttp://www.righttocounselproject.org/sites/default/files/id_guaranteeofcounseljseri01-\n2010_report.pdf, p.10. \n23 Id. \n\n--- Page 18 ---\n14 \nThe district and magistrate judges all with one voice expressed \nconcern that public defense clients do not receive high quality \nrepresentation. Their biggest complaint is they do not believe the \npublic defenders meet with their clients in advance of appearing in \ncourt, but instead they believe these public defenders meet with \ntheir clients primarily only at the courthouse. Though most \ncriminal justice system stakeholders agree Fitzgerald and Van \nIdour “have a wealth of experience,” the big complaint is their lack \nof day-to-day communication with their clients. 24 \n \nIn that context, Mr. Ayerst’s letter to the trial judge May 28, 2017, \nprovides additional support for holding that the failure to have a licensed \nWashington attorney requires reversal of the conviction.  Asking that he \nbe released or the charges against him be dismissed, Mr. Ayerst stated, “I \nwrote Mr. Van Idour for almost two week[sic] with no answer.”25 \nAs Division II of the Court of Appeals has written, when people \nholding themselves out as lawyers are not adequately “regulated, the \ndangers to the public are manifest....” State v. Hunt, 75 Wn. App. 795, \n803, 880 P.2d 96, 100 (1994), citing Dauphin Cty. Bar Ass\'n v. \nMazzacaro, 465 Pa. 545, 551, 351 A.2d 229, 232 (1976). \nD. \nIt appears that Mr. Van Idour violated the misdemeanor of \npracticing law without a license \n \nPracticing law without a license in Washington is a gross \nmisdemeanor. RCW 2.48.180(3).  It is a strict liability offense. State v. \nYishmael, 195 Wn.2d 155, 177 (2020). No proof of intent is required.  A \n                                               \n24 Id, at 60. \n25 This letter is provided in full in the Appendix. \n\n--- Page 19 ---\n15 \nfactual finding that the defendant’s actions constitute the practice of law \nand that the defendant was never admitted to the WSBA is sufficient to \nconvict. \nIn holding that the crime is a strict liability offense, this Court \nnoted the lack of protection for clients of unlawfully practicing lawyers: \nWhen lawfully practicing attorneys cause harm, malpractice \ninsurance and the victims’ compensation fund can provide some \nrelief for their clients. \n By contrast, the unlawful practice of law often causes harm \nwithout any of the protections for malpractice by lawyers. Because \nthese harms are predictable, the unlawful practice of law is a \ncrime.  \n \nYishmael, 195 Wn.2d at 160. \n \n \nThe court expressed concern for the victims of unlawful lawyer \npractice: “Victims in this case became homeless, were jailed, and lost \nalmost everything they owned.” Yishmael, 195 Wn.2d at160. The Court \nnoted that the statute prohibiting unlawful practice of law “protects the \npublic from the harm of poor legal advice. Society might reasonably \nexpect those unlicensed to practice law should not practice law.”  Id., at \n168 [footnote omitted].   \nRCW 2.48.180(2)(a) provides: “The following constitutes unlawful \npractice of law: (a) A nonlawyer practices law, or holds himself or herself \nout as entitled to practice law;…” \n\n--- Page 20 ---\n16 \nMr. Van Idour meets the definition of “nonlawyer” in RCW \n2.38.180(1)(b): “a person who is not an active member in good standing of \nthe state bar….”  There is no question that Mr. Van Idour was practicing \nlaw by providing legal advice and filing trial motions and legal documents \nand appearing in court on behalf of accused persons.  \nWhile the WSBA case against Mr. Van Idour has not yet been \nresolved, Mr. Van Idour admits in his answer to the WSBA complaint that \nhe had not been admitted to the WSBA pursuant to APR 3(c) or APR 8(c). \nInstead, Mr. Van Idour asserts that he was admitted on a pro hac vice \nbasis. 26 \nWashington permits pro hac vice representation, but Mr. Van Idour \ndid not meet the requirements of the rule: \nA lawyer admitted in another United States jurisdiction, and not \ndisbarred or suspended from practice in any jurisdiction, may \nprovide legal services on a temporary basis in this jurisdiction that: \n(1) are undertaken in association with a lawyer who is admitted to \npractice in this jurisdiction and who actively participates in the \nmatter; \n \nRPC 5.5 (c)(1). [Emphasis added.] \n \nThe Court of Appeals stated that Mr. Van Idour “was working \nunder the supervision of a Washington attorney named Neil Cox.” The \n                                               \n26 Answer to Disciplinary Board, No.19#00008, filed June 6, 2019. \n\n--- Page 21 ---\n17 \nrecord does not support a conclusion that Mr. Cox actively participated in \nthe Ayerst matter.   \n \nMr. Van Idour’s pro hac vice claim is meritless. No pro hac vice \norder had been entered before the purported order in October 2017.    \nThe framing of the rule suggests that pro hac vice admission is per case, \nnot per 100 cases. And it is clear that “temporary” in the rule cannot mean \none year, purportedly representing more than 100 people. \n \nIn effect, Mr. Van Idour was committing a crime the entire time he \nwas attempting to represent Mr. Ayerst.  \nE. \nThis Court’s Precedent Requires Reversal \n \nThe Court of Appeals admits that “because Mr. Ayerst\'s attorney \nwas not licensed in Washington, he did not have ‘counsel’ as that term is \ndefined for constitutional purposes. See City of Seattle v. Ratliff, 100 \nWash.2d 212, 217, 667 P.2d 630 (1983).”  Matter of Ayerst, 486 P.3d at \n946.  But the Court then veered into a review of cases citing Solina v. \nUnited States, 709 F.2d 160, 167 (2d Cir. 1983), involving out of state \nlawyers.  Matter of Ayerst, 486 P.3d 943, 946.  The Court wrote: “In the \ndecades since Solina, courts have generally agreed structural error applies \nto representation by an unlicensed attorney only if the attorney has never \nbeen licensed in any jurisdiction.” Id., at 946.  \n\n--- Page 22 ---\n18 \n  \nThe Court of Appeals misapprehended Ratliff’s holding. It ignored  \nJustice Utter’s clear language that applies here: “Although one who is \nauthorized to practice only under certain conditions, such as a legal intern, \nmay be considered ‘counsel’ for constitutional purposes, this is so only \nwhen he or she complies with those conditions.”  City of Seattle v. Ratliff, \n100 Wn.2d at 218.  Mr. Van Idour did not comply with the conditions that \nmight have allowed him to practice under any of the rules that the Court of \nAppeals speculated could have applied. And he did not comply with the \nconditions that apply to all appointed lawyers representing accused \npersons in Washington—satisfying the minimum requirements to practice \nlaw and certifying quarterly that he complies with certain standards. \n \nMr. Ratliff was represented by a Rule 9 intern who was not \nallowed by the trial court to consult with his supervisor before trial. This \nCourt emphasized that the supervising attorney “must supervise and \nassume professional responsibility for the legal intern\'s work. APR \n9(D)(2).” Ratliff, 100 Wn.2d at 216.  Although the Court of Appeals \nsuggested that Mr. Van Idour was supervised by a Washington attorney, \nthere is no record of that, and the supervision requirement of someone \nseeking to practice under APR 8 was not met. \n \nThis Court held: \n\n--- Page 23 ---\n19 \nThus, in contrast to representation in accordance with governing \nrules, representation by a law student intern who fails to comply \nwith the conditions placed upon his or her practice does constitute \nan absolute denial of the right to counsel which requires reversal. \n Ratliff, 100 Wn.2d at 219. \n \n \nIf one substitutes “unlicensed out of state lawyer” for “law student \nintern” in that holding, it is clear that Mr. Van Idour’s failure to comply \nwith Washington’s court rule on standards constitutes an absolute denial \nof the right to counsel. This finding requires reversal, without a showing \nof prejudice. \nIn language that controls this case, this Court held: “Denial of \nrepresentation by one actually authorized to practice in court constitutes a \ndenial of counsel, not merely ineffective assistance.” Ratliff, 100 Wn.2d at \n220.   \nMr. Ayerst was denied counsel and his conviction should be \nreversed. \nF. \nConclusion. \nThis Court has written: “the right to counsel is of paramount \nimportance to all persons appearing in our courts and must be jealously \nguarded.” Ratliff, supra, 100 Wn.2d 212, 218. In keeping with that \nholding, Mr. Ayerst’s petition for review should be granted and his \nconviction should be reversed. \n \n\n--- Page 24 ---\n20 \nRespectfully submitted, \n \n__________________ \nRobert C. Boruchowitz WSBA #4563. \n901 12th Avenue \nSeattle, Washington 98122 \n206 398-4151 \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n--- Page 25 ---\nA \n \nV. \nAppendix \n \n \n \n \n \nFrom: McKenzie Campbell <MCampbell@co.as0tin.wa.uS> \nDate: June 18, 2021 at 11 :24:49 AM PDT \nTo: "Story, Maddison" <mstory@seattleu.edu> \nSubject: RE: B. Vanldour \nMaddi, \nYes that is correct. \nMcKenzie \nFrom: Story, Maddison (mailto:mstory@seattleu.edu1 \nSent: Friday, June 18, 2021 11 :05 AM \nTo: McKenzie Campbell <MCampbell@co.asotin.wa.us> \nSubject: Re: B. Vanldour \nHello McKenzie, \nThank you for sending these along. \nJust to confirm- this is the only certification Mr. Van ldour filed for the year 2017? \nBest, \nMaddi Story \nOn Jun 18, 2021, at 10:33 AM, McKenzie Campbell <MCampbell@co.asotin.wa.us> wrote: \nHello, \nI have attached the documents we discussed on the phone. \nLet me know if you need anything else. \nMcKenzie A. Campbell \nAsotin County Clerk \nP.O. Box 159 \nAsotin, WA 99402 \nPhone - (509) 243-2081 \nFax - (509) 243-4978 \nmcampbell@co.asotin.wa.us \nThis e-mail and your response are considered a public record and will be subject to disclosure under Washington\'s Public Disclosure Act \n\n--- Page 26 ---\nB \n \n \n \n \n,N:SUPERIOR COURT \n[ I JUVENILE DEPARTMENT \n[ I DISTRICT COURT \nI J MUNICIPAL COURT \nFOR \n[ 1 CITY OF I I COUNTY OF~------\' \nSTATE OF WASIDNGTON \nCERTIFICATION BY: \n[NAME], [WSBA#] l\'\\,iL .. .:-1: j -\\.l«_.,,_ f, )~ \nFOR \n[1,rr ),ND 3 , 4\'"] CALENDAR QUARTER OF [YEAR] \nThe undersigned attorney hereby certifies: \n]No.: _____ \n_ \n] Administrative Filing \nCERTIFICATION \nOF \nAPPOINTED \nCOUNSEL \nOF \nCOMPIJANCE \nWITH \nSTANDARDS REQUlRED BY CRR 3.1 / \nCRRU3.l / JUCR9.2 \n1. \nApproximately __ % of my total practice time is devoted to indigent defense cases. \n2. \nI am familiar with the applicable Standards adopted by the Supreme Court for attorneys \nappointed to represent indigent persons and that: \na. Bask Qualifications: I meet the minimum basic professional qualifications in Standard \n14.1. \nb. Office: I have access to an office that accommodates confidential meetings with clients, \nand I have a postal address and adequate telephone services to ensure prompt response to \nclient contact, in compliance with Standard 5.2. \nc. Investigators: I have investigators available to me and will use investigative services as \nappropriate, in compliance with Standard 6.1. \nd. Caseload: I will comply with Standard 3.2 during representation of the defendant in my \ncases. [Effective October 1, 2013 for felony and juvenile offender caseloads; effective \nJanuary 1, 2015 for misdemeanor caseloads: I should not accept a greater number of cases \n(or a proportional mix of different case types) than specified in Standard 3.4, prorated if the \namount of time spent for indigent defense is less than full time, and taking into account the \ncase counting and weighting system applicable in my jurisdiction.] \ne. Case Specific Qualifications: I am familiar with the specific case qualifications in \nStandard 14.2, Sections B-K and will not. accept appointment in a case as lead counsel \nunless Lme_~t t the ~cations for that case. [Effective October 1( 2013] \nrur1- l14=--\n_ ~-t-\\3:: \nSignature, WSB ~# \nDate \n\n--- Page 27 ---\nC \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \nI \nSUPERIOR COURT OF WASHINGTON \nFOR ASOTIN COUNTY \n·~.-: \nz:n JUN 12\' P 3: 22 \nf>/CKEIIZIE KELLEY \n-· "\'.ffY CLERI< \n. . . : !JU!.-HY. WA · \nSTATE OF WASHINGTON, \nPlaintiff, \nCASE NO. 16-1-00150-7 \nV. \nROBERT AYERST, \nDefendant. \nCORRESPONDENCE. Copy sent to counsel. \n. -\n:, ... ··--- .. \n-Y\'Y\\ \'1 -- -N a. V\'v\'\\....~ - .. , ~ \n\\~ o ".lo\'e.c.T \n_ , A~ e--r-s:T \'. \n••• • \nc •• ~·1vc1. :r: \'::Ta.\\ l(tcl.. \n.+-c.i \n~UV ... i ~ -\n~OVv" -\n- ~ \n\\,-. c,,.\\"\'0. \\{I~~ 5... \n. o__ . \nC.o _; ~ \\ ~ \n\'i-i ""\'\'le S. . . • CA. \\.oJ,. \\ \n:. "\'Ch.\'~ - -w~r.-r..<>-,.,J~ . ·nr..~\\ \n. \nv,L°\'-.~ .. ?-v...~ ·_ \no \' . \n· \n· \\ \nTh · \n. . \n· \n· \' \n.. \n. __:.::...~ - --\n\'{V\\_,~ .. • \' A(P_. I \n-\n\'.7. - .. _ ... ,:i. . .I-\'-~/lv:v\\ -\n-- ---- .J r\'\'-\'I i\'_IJ 1-·· ·_ :t-u - . ---~1-.e.1: . Th ,\'..s .. ;_ -re so.l 0-ta.c . __ qµJ . \n_:_ · ,: 1-<.T .. : . . \\-\\o~ ... ..1.. \n.- u.,o..s. \n-~1,,1.-.,, ; \nlf\':-\\C..<\'".r 1\' ~- ~ \n.. .. 0r-!<l.. \n0....""\'-. \n... l<.),e<!...~ e.. ~- . \nQ.. ~ -\n~ o.~ \n~ __ .. \n\' \n·+·· \n.,\'-~-\n. ·. _._ --\n-~\'-\'} - -