. . . . .. ........ . ... . ... ...... . 16 \nState Supreme Court Cases \nIn re Coats, 173 Wn.2d 123 (2011) .... . .. . ... . . ... .. 6 \nIn re Personal Restraint of Crace, 174 Wn.2d 835 (2012). 7 \nIn re Personal Restraint of Davis, 152 Wn.2d 647 (2004) . 7 \nIn re Personal Restraint of Finstad, 177 Wn.2d 501 (2013) 7 \nMatter of the Disciplinary Proceedings against Ralph G. Turco, \nTacoma Municip/e Court Judge, 137 wn.2d 227 (1999) . 19 \nSherman v. State, 128 Wn.2d 164 (1995) . . . . . . . • . . . . 17 \nState v. Gamble, 168 Wn.2d 161 (2010) . . . • . . . . . . 17-18 \nState v. Solis, 187 Wn.2d 535 (2017) . . . . . ...... .... 17 \nFederal Court of Appeals Cases \nCole v. United States, 162 F.3d 957 (7th Cir. 1998) .. . . . 9 \nii \n\n--- Page 4 ---\nDerringer v. United States, 441 F.2d 1140 (8th Cir. 1971) . 9 \nHardamon v. United States, 319 F.3d 943 (7th Cir. 2003). 8 \nKieser v. New York, 56 F.3d (2nd Cir. 1995) . . . . . . . . . . . 9 \nUnited States v. Costanzo, 740 F.2d 251 (3rd Cir. 1984) . 9 \nUnited States v. Maria-Martinez, 143 F.3d 914 (5th Cir. 1998) \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 8-9 \nUnited States v. Mitchell, 216 F.3d 1126 (D.C. Cir. 2000) . 6 \nUnited States v. Watson, 479 F.3d 607 (8th Cir. 2007) ... 8 \nState Court of Appeals Cases \nCity of Seattle v. Ratliff, 100 Wn.2d 212 (1983) .... passim \nDependency of A.E.T.H., 9 Wn.App.2d 502 (2019) . . ... 19 \nIn re Personal Restraint of Haynes, 100 Wn.App. 366 (2000) \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 18 \nMatter of Ayerst, 17 Wn.App.2d 356 (2021) . . . 6, 9, 16-18 \nState v. Ayerst, 35867-4-111, 2019 WL 1568060 (Div. Ill, Apr. \n11, 2019) ...................... ... .. .. .. ... . . 3, 5 \nState v. Bilal, 77 Wn.App. 720 (1995) . . . . . • • . . . . . . . . 17 \nState v. Edison, 61 Wn.App. 530 (1991) . . . . . . . . . . • . . . 8 \nState v. Medlock, 86 Wn.App. 89 (1997) . . . . . • . • • . . . . . 7 \nStatev. Williams, 15 Wn.App.2d 841 (2021 )(unpublished, see \nGR 14. 1) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 18 \nTatham v. Rogers, 170 Wn.App. 76 (2012) ..... . . . 17-18 \nOther Cases \nFerrara v. Keane, 806 F. Supp. 472 (1992) •••. . .. .. ... 7 \niii \n\n--- Page 5 ---\nIn re Moore, 63 III.App.3d 899 (1978) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 12 \nCheatham v. State, 364 So.2d 83 (Fla.Dist.Ct.App. 1978) \n. ... .... .. ... .. ... .. .... .. ... .. . . .. . .. .... .. . 12 \nPeople v. Felder, 47 N.Y.2d 287 (1979) . ........ .•.. 13 \nPeople v. Masonis, 58 Mich.App 615 (1975) . . ... . .. . . 12 \nState v. Washington v. Scott D. Gallina, Asotin County \nSuperior Court Cause 19-1-00066-02 . . ... . ...... . .. . 5 \nCourt Rules \nAPR8 \npassim \nAPR 9 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 1, 10, 11, 13 \nRAP 13.4 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 6 \nOther Authorities \nConst. amend. VI . . . . .. . ...... . ......... .. . ...... 7 \nConst. art. 1 § 22 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . • . . . 7 \n"Over 1000 New Lawyers Get Licenses Without Taking Bar \nExam," \nBloomburg \nLaw, \nJan. \n4 , \n2021 , \nhttps://news.bloomberglaw.com/business-and-practice/over-\n1-000-new-lawyers-get-licenses-without-taking-bar-exam 14 \niv \n\n--- Page 6 ---\nI. \nSUMMARY OF ISSUES \n1. \nDOES THE COURT OF APPEALS\' DECISION \nCONFLICT WITH THIS COURT\'S DECISION IN \nRA TL/FF, INVOLVE A SIGNIFICANT QUESTION OF \nCONSTITUTIONAL \nLAW, \nOR \nREPRESENT A \nSUBSTANTIAL MATTER OF PUBLIC INTEREST? \n2. \nDOES THE COURT OF APPEAL\'S DECISION TO \nDENY RELIEF BASED ON THE ALLEGATIONS \nAGAINST FORMER JUDGE GALLINA RAISE A \nSIGNIFICANT QUESTION OF CONSTITUTIONAL \nLAW? \nII. \nSUMMARY OF ARGUMENT \n1. \nTHE COURT\'S DECISION DOES NOT CONFLICT \nWITH RATLIFF, WHICH IS SPECIFIC TO APR 9 \nLEGAL INTERNS AND FITS WITHIN THE SAME \nLEGAL FRAMEWORK USED BY THE COURT IN THIS \nCASE, AND, BECAUSE THE DECISION HAS NO \nIMPACT ON THE RIGHT TO COUNSEL IT IS \nNEITHER \nA \nSIGNIFICANT \nQUESTION \nOF \nCONSTITUTIONAL LAW NOR A SUBSTANTIAL \nMATTER OF PUBLIC INTEREST. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n1 \n\n--- Page 7 ---\nTHE COURT\'S DECISION DOES NOT RAISE A \nSIGNIFICANT QUESTION OF LAW UNDER EITHER \nTHE \nSTATE \nOR \nFEDERAL \nCONSTITUTION \nBECAUSE THE COURT CORRECTLY APPLIED \nWELL-ESTABLISHED STATE AND FEDERAL LAW IN \nDETERMINING THAT THE DEFENDANT WAS NOT \nENTITLED \nTO \nRELIEF \nBASED \nON \nTHE \nALLEGATIONS AGAINST JUDGE GALLINA. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n2 \n\n--- Page 8 ---\nIll. \nSTATEMENT OF THE CASE \nThe Defendant was convicted after jury trial of Attempted \nBurglary in the Second Degree, Malicious Mischief in the Second \nDegree, and Bail Jumping (C Felony), from events occurring \nSeptember 6, 2016. See State v. Ayerst, 35867-4-111, 2019 W.L. \n1568060 (Unpublished).1 The underlying facts of the case are largely \nimmaterial and need no discussion herein. \nThe Defendant was initially represented by John Fay, who was \nlater replaced by attorney Robert Van · ldour who represented the \nDefendant through trial. See Bond Order (10/04/16) and Motion and \nOrder Allowing Withdrawal and Substitution, submitted with the \nState\'s Response to Personal Restraint Petition. Mr. Van ldour had \ncontracted with Asotin County and had arranged to practice under \nAPR 8( c ), which authorizes attorneys who are I icensed by other states \nto appear for the purposes of public defense while they apply for a \nformal license to practice in Washington. Declaration of Robert J. \nVan ldour. Mr. Van !dour also applied for formal admission to the \nWashington State Bar by motion under APR 3(c). See Personal \nRestraint Petition Appendix A He was later notified that his \napplication for admission by motion under APR 3(c) was accepted, \n1The State does not cite the unpublished decision for \nprecedent, but rather as establishing the procedural and factual \nbackground of the case. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n3 \n\n--- Page 9 ---\nand that his application for limited admission under APR 8(c) had \nbeen denied. Appendix A Mr. Van ldour was, and still is, a member \nin good standing with the Idaho State Bar Association. Declaration of \nRobert J. Van ldour. \nMore than a year after the Defendant\'s trial and sentencing, \nJudge Gallina was arrested and charged with sexual related felony \nand gross misdemeanor charges for actions unrelated to this trial. \nThose charges are currently pending. See State of Washington v. \nScott D. Gallina, Asotin Superior Court Cause 19-1-00066-02. \nThe Defendant appealed his conviction, and it was affirmed by \nthe Court of Appeals in an unpublished opinion prior to Judge \nGallina\'s arrest. See State v. Ayerst,35867-4-111, 2019 W. L. 1568060. \nThe Defendant then filed this personal restraint petition, alleging that \nhe had been deprived of his right to counsel, relying solely on the fact \nthat Mr. Van ldour was not licensed to practice in Washington. He \nneither argued nor demonstrated that Mr. Van ldour committed any \nact of misfeasance or nonfeasance, nor did he otherwise claim any \nprejudice therefrom. Additionally, the Defendant argued that he was \ndenied a fair trial under the appearance of fairness doctrine based on \nthe criminal charges against former Judge Gallina. \nIn a published decision, the Division Three of the Court of \nAppeals dismissed the Defendant\'s petition, finding that 1) the facts \nset forth in the Defendant\'s petition failed to establish that he should \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n4 \n\n--- Page 10 ---\nbe excused from showing prejudice based on a theory of structural \nerror and the Defendant failed to allege that he was prejudiced by his \nattorney\'s representation, and 2) the Defendant failed to establish \nfacts warranting relief from conviction based on the allegations \nagainst Judge Gallina." see Matter of Ayerst, 17 Wn.App.2d 356 \n(2021 ). The Defendant is now seeking review of the Court of Appeals\' \ndecision. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n5 \n\n--- Page 11 ---\nIV. \nDISCUSSION \nThe decision to review a Court of Appeals decision terminating \nreview is discretionary. RAP 13.4(a). A petition for review will be \naccepted by the Supreme Court only if at least one of four criteria are \nmet. RAP 13.4(b); In re Coats, 173 Wn.2d 123, 132-33 (2011 ). Three \ncriteria relevant to this petition are a) the decision of the Court of \nAppeals is in conflict with a decision by the Supreme Court, b) the \npetition raises a significant question under the State or Federal \nConstitution, and c) the petition involves a substantial public interest \nthat should be determined by the Supreme Court. Id. Because none \nof these criteria are met by either claim, the Defendant\'s petition for \nreview should be denied. \n1. \nTHE COURT OF APPEALS DECISION DOES NOT \nCONFLICT WITH THIS COURT\'S HOLDING IN CITY OF \nSEATTLE V. RA TL/FF BECAUSE THE FACTS OF THE \nDEFENDANT\'S CASE ARE DISTINGUISHABLE IN THAT \nCOUNSEL FOR THE DEFENDANT WAS LICENSED IN AT \nLEAST ONE JURISDICTION. \nGenerally, a defendant challenging his conviction through a \nPersonal Restraint Petition (PRP) must show that the error raised \nimpacted the outcome of the trial. See, e.g., In re Personal Restraint \nof Crace, 174 Wn.2d 835 (2012); In re Pers. Restraint of Finstad, 177 \nWn.2d 501, 506 (2013). If the defendant cannot show that they were \nprejudiced by the error, then their petition is dismissed. Crace, 174 \nWn.2d 847-48. An exception to the prejudice requirement exists \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n6 \n\n--- Page 12 ---\nwhere there is a structural error, i.e. an error which impacts the \nframework of the trial process. Weaver v. Massachusetts, -\nU.S. \n--, 137 S. Ct. 1899 (2017). \nBoth the Sixth Amendment of the United States Constitution \nand article 1, section 22 of the state constitution guarantee the right \nto counsel in a criminal case. Const. amend. VI; Const. art. 1, § 22; \nSee, e.g., In re Personal Restraint of Davis, 152 Wn.2d 647, (2004). \nWith regard to this right to counsel, the state constitution provides no \nmore protection than the Sixth Amendment. State v. Medlock, 86 \nWn.App. 89, 99, review denied, 133 Wn.2d 1012 (1997). Absolute \ndenial of counsel, such as when the "attorney" was not licensed in any \nstate at the time of representation, does not require a showing of \nprejudice.2 See Ferrara v. Keane, 806 F.Supp. 472, 474 (S.D.N.Y. \n1992), aff\'d, 2 F.3d 403 (2d Cir. 1993). However, lesser violations of \nthe right to counsel do not require automatic dismissal. A defendant \nwho is represented by a person who has been licensed to practice law \nin any jurisdiction has not suffered from a per se Sixth Amendment \nviolation. See, e.g.,United States v. Mitchell, 216 F.3d 1126, 1132 \n(D.C. Cir. 2000) ("We decline appellant\'s invitation to extend the per \nse ineffectiveness rule beyond those cases in which a defendant is \n2See, e.g.,Brecht v. Abrahamson, 507 U.S. 619, 629-30 \n(1993), citing Gideon v. Wainwright, 372 U.S. 335 (1963). \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n7 \n\n--- Page 13 ---\nrepresented by a person never properly admitted to any bar.\'); United \nStates v. Maria-Martinez, 143 F.3d 914,917 (5th Cir. 1998) ("the key \nto adequate representation is not technical license to· practice in the \njurisdiction involved, but a credential from some forum demonstrating \nthe specialized knowledge of a lawyer.\'). \nA defendant who was represented by a lawyer whose license \nhas been suspended must demonstrate actual prejudice in order to \nhave her conviction vacated. See, e.g., United States v. Watson, 479 \nF.3d 607, 61 1 (8th Cir. 2007) (suspension of a state license not per \nse ineffective assistance of counsel); Hardamon v. United States, 319 \nF.3d 943, 951 (7th Cir. 2003) (suspension of an attorney does not \namount to per se ineffective assistance); State v. Edison, 61 Wn. App. \n530 (1991) (defendant must demonstrate actual prejudice when \nrepresented by a lawyer who has been licensed but whose license \nwas suspended). \nA defendant who was represented by an out-of-state attorney \nwho has not been admitted pro hac vice must demonstrate actual \nprejudice in order to have her conviction vacated. See, e.g., Cole v. \nUnited States, 162 F.3d 957,959 (7th Cir. 1998); Kieserv. New York, \n56 F .3d 16, 17 (2nd Cir. 1995) (lawyer suspended in New Jersey and \nnot admitted pro hac vice in New York when case was tried); United \nStates v. Costanzo, 740 F.2d 251, 258-59 (3rd Cir. 1984); Derringer \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n8 \n\n--- Page 14 ---\nv. United States, 441 F.2d 1140, 1141 (8th Cir. 1971). \nThese violations are considered less serious because they do \nnot thwart the purposes of the right to counsel. As the Court of \nAppeals explained in Ayerst, \nThe limited allowance for structural error is based on \ntwo considerations. "First; courts are concerned that a \ndefendant have a counselor who has legal training and \ndemonstrated the specialized knowledge and ability of \na lawyer." Maria-Martinez, 143 F.3d 914, 916. This \nconcern was emphasized in the Washington Supreme \nCourt\'s decision in Ratliff, where the defendant was \nrepresented by a law student who operated outside the \nparameters of the admission to practice rules applicable \nto law students. 100 Wash.2d at 217, 667 P.2d 630. \nSecond, there is concern "an undisclosed lack of \ncredentials may create a conflict of interest." \nMaria-Martinez, 143 F.3d at 916. \n17 Wn.App.2d at 361-62. \nUnder these applicable standards, the Court of Appeals \ncorrectly determined that, while the Defendant had been deprived of \ncounsel, the deprivation was not severe enough to constitute a \nstructural error. Ayerst, 17 Wn.App.2d at 362. As the Court noted, Mr. \nVan ldour "was and is an attorney, subject to state licensing \nregulations," and could have qualified to provide indigent defense \nrepresentation in Washington [under APR 8]." Id. at 3. Thus, the \nDefendant was required to demonstrate prejudice and, because he \nfailed to do so, the Court of Appeals dismissed his PRP. Id. \nThe Court of Appeals decision neither conflicts with City of \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n9 \n\n--- Page 15 ---\nSeattle v. Ratliff nor undermines this Court\'s authority to regulate the \npractice of law. 100 Wn.2d 212 (1983). In Ratliff, this Court held that \nrepresentation by a legal intern licensed under APR 9 ("rule 9 intern") \nwho has been prevented from complying with that rule is a per se \nviolation of the right to counsel. 100 Wn.2d at 221. There, the \ndefendant was scheduled to be tried on three charges, but his court-\nappointed counsel did not appear on the morning of trial. Id. at 213. \nUpon questioning the defendant, the court learned that he was being \nrepresented by a rule 9 intern, who had not completed law school and \nwho had never been licensed to practice law in Washington or any \nother jurisdiction, in a different case. Id. at 213-14; see APR 9(8). \nDespite the fact that the rule 9 intern had no involvement with the \nmatters set for trial, the court summoned him and, after allowing the \nhim to speak with the defendant "must a little bit," attempted to \nproceed with the trial. Id. at 214. When the intern objected, the court \nrequired him over objection to proceed, but allowed the defense to \ndelay the presentation of its case until the next business day. Id. at \n214. The court did not allow the intern time to consult his supervising \nattorney prior to trial, as required by APR 9. Id. at 215, 220; see APR \n9(D). \nBy the time that case reached this Court, two separate issues \nhad been raised. The defendant argued that he was denied effective \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n10 \n\n--- Page 16 ---\nassistance of counsel because the intern was given insufficient time \nto prepare his case and was prevented from consulting his \nsupervising attorney as required by APR 9. Id. at 215. In addition, the \nCourt of Appeals, sua sponte, questioned whether representation of \nan indigent defendant by a law student, without the presence of a \nsupervising attorney in court, constituted a per se violation of the right \nto counsel. Id. \nThis Court first addressed . the constitutional challenge to \nrepresentation by rule 9 interns. In doing so, it reminded the legal \ncommunity that it has the constitutionally granted power to determine \nwho may appear before the bar, and that this power is both "well \nestablished" and "involate." Id. at 215. Pursuant to this power, this \nCourt promulgated and adopted the Admission to Practice Rules \nincluding APR 9, which allows "lesser qualified persons to engage in \nlimited practice." Id. at 216. Next, this Court explained that rule 9 has \nseveral requirements, including and supervision by a qualified \nlicensed attorney. Id. Additionally, rule 9 contains specific rules \noutlining the conditions under which an intern may try cases without \na supervisor present. Id. Thus, this Court concluded, \n[a]lthough a legal intern is not an attorney, this court \nhas established safeguards sufficient to assure that \npersons attaining rule 9 status will be competent to \nserve the needs of their clients. In light of this court\'s \nauthority to determine which persons may practice in \nWashington state courts, and in light of the rules this \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n11 \n\n--- Page 17 ---\ncourt has promulgated to assure that persons so \npracticing are qualified, we have held that there is no \nper se impropriety in allowing legal interns to appear in \ncriminal proceedings. \nId. at 216-217. Ultimately, this Court held that when rule 9 is complied \nwith, a criminal defendant receives legal assistance "sufficient to meet \nwith both state and federal constitutions." Id. at 218. \nThis Court then turned to the facts of the defendant\'s case. \nWhereas a law student acting in compliance with rule 9 does qualify \nas counsel for Sixth Amendment purposes, this Court held, \n"representation by a Jaw student intern who fails to comply with the \nconditions placed upon his or her practice does constitute an absolute \ndenial of the right to counsel which requires reversal." Id. at 19 \n(emphasis added), citing Cheatham v. State, 364 So.2d 83, 84 \n(Fla.Dist.Ct.App.1978) (failure to comply with requirements that \nsupervising attorney be present and defendant be informed of student \nstatus); In re Moore, 63 III.App.3d 899, 904 (1978) (failure to inform \ncourt of student status and failure to obtain defendant\'s written \nconsent); cf. People v. Masonis, 58 Mich.App. 615, 619 (1975) (/aw \nstudent representation did not violate right to counsel where \ndefendant was aware of student status, general effective assistance \nof counsel standards were met, and there was no violation of student \npractice rules). Additionally, the Court held that where counsel is \noutright denied, no prejudice need be shown. Id. at 220, citing People \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n12 \n\n--- Page 18 ---\nv. Felder, 47 N.Y.2d 287, 294 (1979) (defendants were denied \neffective assistance of counsel where each defendant was \nrepresented in his underlying criminal action by a person who had not \ncompleted law school and who was not, and had never been, \nadmitted to the Bar of the State or of any other jurisdiction). Because \nthe record demonstrated that the law student representing the \ndefendant was prevented from complying with the requirements of \nAPR 9 which would have otherwise qualified him as counsel, this \nCourt held that there was a denial of the "in the circumstances of this \ncase." Id. at 213. \nThe Court of Appeals decision in this case does not conflict \nwith Ratliff because the holding of Ratliff is restricted to law students \npracticing under APR 9 or, at the very least, representation by \nindividuals who have never completed law school or been licensed as \nan attorney in any jurisdiction. In Ratliff, the question before this Court \nwas whether a rule 9 intern, who had never been admitted to practice \nin any jurisdiction, could ever qualify as counsel for constitutional \npurposes and, if so, the conditions required for doing so. This reading \nis supported by the cases cited by this Court in reaching its \nconclusions, a majority of which discuss the practice of law by student \ninterns in other states. \nFurthermore, the Defendant\'s argument that the Court of \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n13 \n\n--- Page 19 ---\nAppeal\'s decision in his case interferes with this Court\'s ability to \nregulate the practice of law in Washington state is misplaced. Ratliff\'s \ndiscussion of this Court\'s power to regulate the practice of law is \noriented towards this Court\'s constitutional power to decide when an \nindividual may affirmatively be admitted to practice in Washington \nstate. It does not necessarily follow that every individual represented \nby counsel acting out of compliance with the APRs is per se denied \ncounsel. Absent the requirements of rule 9, a legal intern is \ncomparable to a lay person because they have never been licensed \nto practice law in any jurisdiction and, in many cases, have not yet \ncompleted their legal studies. By contrast, an attorney admissible pro \nhac vice under APR 8, while not licensed in Washington state, has \ncompleted their legal studies and qualified as an attorney in at least \none jurisdiction. See APR B(b ). 3 Even if that attorney were to later fail \nto comply with the requirements of APR 8, he would still have legal \ntraining and specialized knowledge necessary to ensure a fair trial. \nBecause the holding of Ratliff is so restricted, it fits within the \nsame legal framework applied in the Defendant\'s case. As discussed \nabove, courts draw a distinction between deprivation of counsel \n3The irony is inescapable that this issue has been raised in \nan era where this Court suspended the bar exam and admitted \nover 500 law school graduates under diploma privilege. See \nhttps://news. bloom berg law .com/business-and-practice/over-1-000-\nnew-lawyers-get-l icenses-without-taking-bar-exam. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n14 \n\n--- Page 20 ---\nclaims where the defendant\'s representative is not qualified to \npractice law in any jurisdiction, and those where the representative is \nqualified to practice in at least one jurisdiction, but not where the \nrepresentation occurred. In Ratliff, the otherwise unlicensed law \nstudent intern was prevented from complying with the requirements \nof rule 9, and therefore was not qualified to practice in any jurisdiction. \nThus, this Court appropriately declared the law student\'s \nrepresentation a per se violation of the right to counsel. In the \nPetitoner\'s matter, the Court of Appeals correctly identified the \ndistinguishing fact that Mr. Van ldour, at the time of his \nrepresentation, was qualified to practice in at least one jurisdiction \nand appropriately required the Defendant to demonstrate prejudice. \n2. \nTHE COURT OF APPEALS\' CORRECTLY CONCLUDED. \nUNDER WELL-ESTABLISHED FEDERAL AND STATE \nSTANDARDS, THAT MR. A YERST\'S DUE PROCESS RIGHT \nTO A FAIR TRIAL WAS NOT VIOLATED. \nThe Court of Appeals applied the correct standard in \ndetermining that the Defendant failed to introduce evidence that the \ncharges against Judge Gallina created an unconstitutional risk of bias. \nDue Process requires that a judge recuse him or herself when \n"the probability of actual bias on the part of the judge or \ndecisionmaker is too high to be constitutionally tolerable. Rippo v. \nBaker, -- U.S. --, --, 137 S.Ct. 905, 907 (2017); see \nWilliams v. Pennsylvania, 579 U.S.--,--, 136 S.Ct. 1899, 1905 \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n15 \n\n--- Page 21 ---\n(2016). A defendant alleging a due process violation, need not \ndemonstrate actual bias, but may argue that the facts of his or her \ncase created an "unconsitutional risk of bias." Williams, 136 S.Ct. at \n1905. This test is objective, and asks whether the judge is laboring \nunder a conflict of interest. See, e.g., Williams, 136 S.Ct. At 1905-06 \nUudge refused to recuse himself from case where he had previously \nauthorized State to seek the death penalty as a district attorney}; \nAetna Life Ins. Co. v. Lavoie; 475 U.S. 813 (2006) Uustice casting the \ndeciding vote was in the process of suing an insurance company \nunder a novel theory analogous to successful party\'s case). \nIn the Defendant\'s case, the Court of Appeals acknowledged \nthat "due process can require a trial judge\'s recusal even when the \njudge has not manifested any \'actual bias."\' Ayerst, 17 Wn.App.2d at \n364. The Court correctly identified the test as an objective one \nbased on "whether, under the circumstances of a particular case, \nthere is \'an unconstitutional potential for bias.\'" Id., citing Williams, \n16 S.Ct. at 1905. Later in its analysis, the Court explained that the \nDefendant was alleging a Due Process violation based on an \nunconstitutional risk of bias rather than actual bias and analyzed \nhis claim accordingly. Ayerst, 17 Wn.App.2d at 365. After doing so, \nit determined that the Defendant\'s claim failed "for want of factual \nsupport." Id. This is not the same as insisting that the Defendant \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n16 \n\n--- Page 22 ---\nshow actual bias, as he claims in his brief. See Brief of Petitioner at \n15. Rather, after reviewing all properly raised claims and the record \nbefore it, the Court simply concluded that the Defendant failed to \nsufficiently demonstrate even a risk of unconstitutional bias.4 \nMoreover, the Court of Appeals correctly found that the \nPetitioner failed to allege facts sufficient to find that he was denied \nthe right to a fair trial under the appearance of fairness doctrine. \nUnder the appearance of fairness doctrine, a judicial proceeding is \nvalid if "a reasonably prudent, disinterested observer would \nconclude that the parties received a fair, impartial, and neutral \nhearing." State v. Solis, 187 Wn.2d 535,540 (2017); see a/so State \nv. Gamble, 168 Wn.2d 161, 187 (2010); State v. Bilal, 77 Wn.App. \n720, 722 (1995). This test is objective and assumes that "a \nreasonable person knows and understands all the relevant facts." \nTatham v. Rogers, 170 Wn.App. 76, 96 {2012), quoting Sherman v. \nState, 128 Wn.2d 164,206 (1995) (internal citations and quotations \nomitted). The trial court is presumed to perform its functions \n"regularly and properly without bias or prejudice." Thatham, 170 \nWn.App. at 96. Therefore, the party asserting an appearance of \n4 At oral argument, the Defendant claimed for the first time \nthat Judge Gallina and Mr. Van ldour were in collusion. The Court \nfound that the claim was both unsupported and improperly raised \nand refused to consider it. Ayerst, 17 Wn.App.2d at 365, n.2. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n17 \n\n--- Page 23 ---\nfairness violation must produce sufficient evidence of a judge\'s \nactual or potential bias. Id., citing In re Personal Restraint of \nHaynes, 100 Wn.App. 366,377 n. 23 (2000); Gamble, 168 Wn.2d \nat 187. Mere speculation is not enough. Tatham, 170 Wn.App. at \n96, citing Haynes, 100 Wn.App. 366 (2000). \nApplying these standards, the Court of Appeals found that \nthe Defendant failed to establish sufficient facts to show that the \nappearance of fairness doctrine was violated. Ayerst, 17 \nWn.App.2d at 365. Additionally, the Court explained that even if \nallegations against Judge Gallina were proven true, "the judge\'s \nalleged misconduct would not provide grounds for relief from \nconviction unless tied to the specific circumstances of the \ndefendant\'s case." Id. at 363-64. In doing so, the Court referred to \nits analysis in State v. Williams, where it concluded that even if the \ncharges against Judge Gallina were true, there would be no basis \nfor an objective observer to expect him to favor the State in that \ncase. 15 Wn.App.2d 841 , 850 (2021) (unpublished, see GR 14.1) \n(appealing convictions for felony violation of a no-contact order, \nwitness tampering, and assault). \nIn his brief, the Defendant argues that "[c]ases involving \nviolation of the CJC\'s and the appearance of fairness in non-\ncriminal cases have held behavior far less egregious than in \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n18 \n\n--- Page 24 ---\nAyerst\'s case to violate the appearance," citing to Matter of the \nDisciplinary Proceedings against Ralph G. Turco, Tacoma \nMuniciple Court Judge. 137 Wn.2d 227, 239 (1999) and \nDependency of A.E.T.H .• 9 Wn.App.2d 502, 517-18 (2019). Both \ncases are readily distinguishable. See Turco, 137 Wn.2d 227 \n( concern expressed over appearance of fairness in domestic \nviolence cases where judge was accused of assaulting wife at a \npublic event); A.E.T.H., 9 Wn.App.2d 502 (violation of appearance \nof fairness where court employees committed misconduct over \ncourse of a termination case). \nHere, while the allegations against Judge Gallina, if true, \nseriously impugn his character, they fail to demonstrate what his \npartiality in the Defendant\'s case would be. Unlike in Turco, there \nare no similarities between the crimes for which the Defendant was \nconvicted (Burglary, Malicious Mischief, and Bc1il Jumping) and the \ncrimes of which Judge Gallina stands accused. Similarly, Judge \nGallina\'s alleged misconduct was not directly tied to the \nDefendant\'s case, as was the VGAL programs in A.E.T.H. \nV. \nCONCLUSION \nThe Court of Appeals decision in the Defendant\'s case does \nnot conflict with this Court\'s decision in Ratliff, nor does it raise a \nsignificant question of constitutional law or an issue of substantial \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n19 \n\n--- Page 25 ---\npublic interest. Similarly, the Court of Appeals applied well-\nestablished state and federal law to reach its conclusion that the \nDefendant was not entitled to relief based on the allegations \nagainst Judge Gallina, and thus the decision does not raise a \nsignificant question of state or federal constitutional law. Because \nno basis exists for review of either claim the State respectfully \nrequests that the Defendant\'s Petition for Review be denied. \nDated this ~ \nday of August, 2021 . \nRespectfully submitted, \nEK. YOUNG, \nttorney for Respo \nent \nDeputy Prosecuting Attorney for Asotin County \nP.O. Box 220 \nAsotin, Washington 99402 \n(509) 243-2061 \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n20 \n\n--- Page 26 ---\nSUPREME COURT OF THE STATE OF \nWASHINGTON \nTHE STATE OF WASHINGTON, \nRespondent, \nV. \nROBERT L. AYERST, \nAppellant. \nNo: 999414 \nDECLARATION OF SERVICE \nDECLARATION \nOn August 6, 2021 I electronically mailed, through the portal, a copy of the MOTION TO ENLARGE \nTIME IN WHICH TO FILE RESPONSE in this matter to: \nLISE ELLNER \nliseellnerlaw@comcast.net \nI declare under penalty of perjury under the laws of the State of Washington the foregoing statement \nis true and correct. \n~ \n. \n-\n~~ :;-) \nSigned at Asotin, Washington on August 6, 2021. \n/Y \n1 ~ 1 g \n. \nEN~ ILLER\n-\nDECLARATION \nOF SERVICE \nOffice Assistant \nPage 1 of 1 \nBenjamin C. Nichols, Prosecuting Attorney \nP. 0. Box 220, Asotin, WA 99402 \n(509) 243-2061 \n\n--- Page 27 ---\nASOTIN COUNTY PROSECUTOR\'S OFFICE\nAugust 06, 2021 - 10:29 AM\nTransmittal Information\nFiled with Court:\nSupreme Court\nAppellate Court Case Number:   99941-4\nAppellate Court Case Title:\nPersonal Restraint Petition of Robert L. Ayerst\nSuperior Court Case Number:\n16-1-00150-7\nThe following documents have been uploaded:\n999414_Motion_20210806102602SC761760_7315.pdf \n    This File Contains: \n     Motion 1 - Extend Time to File \n     The Original File Name was SKMBT_36321080610320.pdf\nA copy of the uploaded files will be sent to:\nLiseellnerlaw@comcast.net\nbnichols@co.asotin.wa.us\nboruchor@seattleu.edu\njaimehawk@hotmail.com\njhawk@aclu-wa.org\njmidgley@aclu-wa.org\npleadings@aclu-wa.org\nrcboru@aol.com\ntalner@aclu-wa.org\nComments:\nSender Name: Sharlene Tiller - Email: stiller@co.asotin.wa.us \n    Filing on Behalf of: Curtis Lane Liedkie - Email: cliedkie@co.asotin.wa.us (Alternate Email: )\nAddress: \n135 2nd Street\nP.O. Box 220 \nAsotin, WA, 99402 \nPhone: (509) 243-2061\nNote: The Filing Id is 20210806102602SC761760\n', 'source': 'text_input'}
