2025-07-29 18:12:18 - src.vue_api_endpoints - INFO - JSON data received: {'text': "\n--- Page 1 ---\nNO. 36965-0-III \n IN THE COURT OF APPEALS OF THE STATE OF WASHINGTON \nDIVISION THREE \nIN RE THE PERSONAL RESTRAINT OF \nROBERT AYERST, \nPetitioner. \nON APPEAL FROM THE SUPERIOR COURT OF THE \nSTATE OF WASHINGTON FOR ASOTIN COUNTY \nAsotin Cause No. 16-1-00150-7 \nThe Honorable Scott D. Gallina, Judge \nSUPPLEMENTAL BRIEF OF PETITIONER \nLISE ELLNER, WSBA No. 20955 \nSPENCER BABBITT, WSBA No. 51076 \nAttorneys for Petitioner \nLAW OFFICES OF LISE ELLNER \nPost Office Box 2711 \nVashon, WA 98070 \n(206) 930-1090\nFILED \nCourt of Appeals \nDivision Ill \nState of Washington \n512712020 9:58 AM \nNo. 99941-4\n\n--- Page 2 ---\ni \n \nTABLE OF CONTENTS \n \nPage \n \nA. \nSUPPLEMENTAL ISSUES....................................................1 \n \nB. \nSTATEMENT OF THE CASE................................................1 \n \nC. \nARGUMENT..........................................................................5 \n \n1. \nMR. AYERST SHOULD BE GRANTED RELIEF \nBECAUSE THE TRIAL COURT’S ABILITY TO \nFULFILL \nITS \nJUDICIAL \nROLE \nCANNOT \nBE \nGUARANTEED BY \nA \nJUDGE \nACCUSED OF \nRAPING HIS STAFF \n \n \n....................................................................................5 \n \n \n \na. \nMr. Ayerst’s is entitled to relief because the trial \n \n \n \ncourt’s impartiality can reasonably be  \n \n \n \n \nquestioned due to multiple violations of   \n \n \n \nWashington’s Code of Judicial Conduct..........6 \n \n2. \nJUDGE GALLINA’S ROLE AS PRESIDING JUDGE \nDURING MR. AYERST’S TRIAL VIOLATES THE \nAPPEARANCE OF FAIRNESS DOCTRINE \n \n \n....................................................................................8 \n \n3. \nAYERST WAS DENIED HIS RIGHT TO EFFECTIVE \nASSISTANCE OF COUNSEL WHEN HIS TRIAL \nCOUNSEL WAS NOT LICENSED TO PRACTICE \nLAW IN WASHINGTON AND NEVER COMPLETED \nTHE REQUIREMENTS OF APR 8 \n \n \n..................................................................................11 \n \nD. \nCONCLUSION.....................................................................15 \n \nAppendix A: WSBA Letter  \nAppendix B: Email Exchange \n \n \n\n--- Page 3 ---\nii \nTABLE OF AUTHORITIES \n \nPage \nWASHINGTON CASES \n \nCity of Seattle v. Ratliff,  \n100 Wn.2d 212, 667 P.2d 630 (1983) ........................................... 12 \n \nIn re Coats, \n173 Wn.2d 123, 267 P.3d 324 (2011) ............................................. 5 \n \nIn re Monschke,  \n160 Wn. App. 479, 251 P.3d 884 (2010)......................................... 5 \n \nIn re Swenson,  \n158 Wn. App. 812, 244 P.3d 959 (2010)..................................... 6, 8 \n \nMatter of Sandoval,  \n189 Wn.2d 811, 408 P.3d 675 (2018) ............................................. 5 \n \nState of Washington v. Scott D. Gallina,  \nAsotin County Superior Court Case 19-1-00066-02 ....................... 3 \n \nState v. Ayerst,  \n8 Wn. App. 2d 1028 (2019) (Unpublished).................................. 1, 3 \n \nState v. Chambers,  \n176 Wn.2d 573, 293 P.3d 1185 (2013) ........................................... 9 \n \nState v. Dominguez,  \n81 Wn. App. 325, 914 P.2d 141 (1996)........................................... 8 \n \nState v. Finch,  \n181 Wn. App. 387, 326 P.3d 148 (2014)................................... 9, 10 \n \nState v. Gamble,  \n168 Wn.2d 161, 187, 225 P.3d 973 (2010) ..................................... 9 \n \nState v. Johnson,  \n179 Wn.2d 534, 315 P.3d 1090 (2014), cert. denied, 135 S.Ct. 139,  \n190 L.Ed.2d 105 (2014) ............................................. 11, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 \n\n--- Page 4 ---\niii \nTABLE OF AUTHORITIES \n \nPage \nWASHINGTON CASES, continued \n \nState v. Ra,  \n144 Wn. App. 688, 175 P.3d 609 (2008)................................... 9, 10 \n \nState v. Roberts,  \n142 Wn.2d 471, 14 P.3d 713 (2000) ............................................. 12 \nFEDERAL CASES \n \nBrewer v. Williams,  \n430 U.S. 387, 398, 97 S.Ct. 1232, 51 L.Ed.2d 424 (1977) ........... 12 \n \nChapman v. California,  \n386 U.S. 18, 87 S.Ct. 824, 17 L.Ed.2d 705 (1967) ................. 12, 15 \n \nGideon v. Wainwright,  \n372 U.S. 335, 83 S.Ct. 792, 9 L.Ed.2d 799 (1963) ........... 11, 13, 15 \n \nHarrison v. United States,  \n387 F.2d 2013 (1967), reversed on other grounds, 392 U.S. 219, 88 \nS.Ct. 2008, 20 L.Ed.2d 1047 (1968) ....................................... 13, 14 \n \nHolloway v. Arkansas,  \n435 U.S. 475, 98 S.Ct. 1173, 55 L.Ed.2d 426 (1978) ................... 12 \n \nJohnson v. Zerbst,  \n304 U.S. 458 (1938) ......................................................... 13, 14, 15 \n \nSolina v. United States,  \n709 F.2d 160 (1983) ....................................................12, 13, 14, 15 \n \nU.S. v. Bryant,  \n136 S.Ct. 1954 (2016)................................................................... 11 \n \nUnited States v. Mouzin,  \n785 F.2d 682 (9th Cir. 1986) .......................................................... 14 \n \n \n\n--- Page 5 ---\niv \nTABLE OF AUTHORITIES \n \nPage \nFEDERAL CASES, continued \n \nUnited States v. Turnbull, \n 888 F.2d 636 (9th Cir. 1989) ......................................................... 12 \n \nUnited States v. Wright,  \n568 F.2d 142 (9th Cir 1978) ........................................................... 13 \n \nWeaver v. Massachusetts,  \n___ U.S. ___, 137 S.Ct. 1899, 198 L.Ed.2d 420 (2017) ............... 15 \n \nWheat v. United States,  \n486 U.S. 153, 108 S.Ct. 1692, 100 L.Ed.2d 140 (1988) ............... 12 \nRULES, STATUTES, AND OTHERS \n \nCode of Judicial Conduct RULE 1.1 ............................................... 6 \n \nCode of Judicial Conduct RULE 1.2 ............................................... 6 \n \nCode of Judicial Conduct RULE 2.11.......................................... 8, 9 \n \nCode of Judicial Conduct RULE 2.3 ............................................... 7 \n \nRAP 16.4 .............................................................................. 5, 8, 11 \n \nU.S. Const. Amend. VI .................................................11, 12, 13, 14 \n \nU.S. Const. Amend. XIV ......................................................... 11, 12 \n \nWash. Const. art. I, § 22 ........................................................... 6, 12 \n \nWashington State Bar APR 8 ................................................ 1, 2, 11 \n \n \n \n \n\n--- Page 6 ---\n \n- 1 - \nA. \nSUPPLEMENTAL ISSUES \n1. \nIs Mr. Ayerst entitled to relief when the impartiality of \nthe judge presiding at his trial can reasonably be questioned \nfollowing credible allegations that the judge committed rape \nand violated multiple provisions of the Washington Code of \nJudicial Conduct during his time as a judge in Asotin \nCounty? \n2.  \nWas Mr. Ayerst denied effective assistance of counsel \nwhen his trial counsel was not licensed to practice law in \nWashington at the time of Mr. Ayerst’s trial and never \ncompleted the requirements for admission to the Washington \nState Bar under APR 8(c)? \nB. \nSTATEMENT OF THE CASE \n \nA jury convicted Robert Ayerst of attempted burglary in the \nsecond degree, malicious mischief in the second degree, and bail \njumping based on an incident that occurred on September 6, 2016. \nSee State v. Ayerst, 8 Wn. App. 2d 1028 (2019) (Unpublished). The \nsubstantive facts related to the incident are not at issue in this \nPersonal Restraint Petition (PRP). \n \nMr. Ayerst’s trial counsel was Robert Van Idour. Personal \n\n--- Page 7 ---\n \n- 2 - \nRestraint Petition of Robert Ayerst. Mr. Van Idour is not licensed to \npractice law in Washington but is licensed to practice in Idaho and \nis in good standing with the Idaho State Bar Association. \nDeclaration of Robert Van Idour. At the time of Mr. Ayerst’s trial, Mr. \nVan Idour was contracted to provide public defense services in \nWashington pursuant to APR 8(c), but a search of the Washington \nState Association (WSBA) lawyer directory revealed that he never \nacquired a license to practice in Washington as of April 2020. \nDeclaration of Robert Van Idour. \nLise Ellner, counsel on appeal, contacted Asotin County and \ndisciplinary counsel for the WSBA in regard to this issue. On April \n19, 2019, disciplinary counsel for the bar provided Ms. Ellner with a \nletter and formal complaint confirming that Mr. Van Idour was never \nlicensed or authorized to practice law in Washington State. See \nAppendix A (attached hereto and incorporated by reference herein). \nMs. Ellner contacted the Washington State Office of Public \nDefense to seek additional information regarding Mr. Van Idour’s \nstatus when obtaining an indigent defense contract in Asotin \nCounty. See Appendix B (attached hereto and incorporated by \nreference herein). \n\n--- Page 8 ---\n \n- 3 - \nThe email exchanges indicate that Asotin County incorrectly \nasserted that Mr. Van Idour was under the supervision of Neil Fox \nworking pro hac vice, when according to disciplinary counsel for the \nWSBA, Mr. Van Idour was not authorized to work pro hac vice. See \nAppendices A, B. \nThe presiding judge at Mr. Ayerst’s trial was Asotin County \nSuperior Court Judge Scott Galina. Judge Gallina was aware of this \nfact and issued an order that purported to permit Mr. Van Idour to \npractice in Washington without a Washington State license. See \nAppendix A. \n \nJudge Gallina was arrested roughly a year after Mr. Ayerst’s \ntrial and charged with second degree rape, indecent liberties, and \nassault in the third degree with sexual motivation based on \nallegations of sexual misconduct made by multiple members of his \njudicial staff. See State of Washington v. Scott D. Gallina, Asotin \nCounty Superior Court Case 19-1-00066-02. \n \nDivision Three of the Washington State Court of Appeals \naffirmed Mr. Ayerst’s convictions and sentence following a direct \nappeal. State v. Ayerst, 8 Wn. App. 2d 1028. Mr. Ayerst later filed a \nPRP requesting relief on several grounds. Personal Restraint \n\n--- Page 9 ---\n \n- 4 - \nPetition of Robert Ayerst. Mr. Ayerst alleged that Judge Gallina’s \narrest and prosecution are evidence of bias that affected the \noutcome of his trial, that he received ineffective assistance of \ncounsel because Mr. Van Idour was never licensed to practice law \nin Washington, that the testifying detective perjured himself by \ndenying the existence of a recorded interview, and that the \nprosecutor had improper contact with prospective jurors before voir \ndire. Personal Restraint Petition of Robert Ayerst. The state \nresponded to the claims in Mr. Ayerst’s PRP and this court \nappointed Mr. Ayerst counsel to provide supplemental briefing. \nOrder Appointing Counsel (4/28/2020). \n\n--- Page 10 ---\n \n- 5 - \nC. \nARGUMENT \n1. \nMR. AYERST SHOULD BE GRANTED \nRELIEF \nBECAUSE \nTHE \nTRIAL \nCOURT’S ABILITY TO FULFILL ITS \nJUDICIAL \nROLE \nCANNOT \nBE \nGUARANTEED \nBY \nA \nJUDGE \nACCUSED OF RAPING HIS STAFF \n \nCriminal defendants currently under restraint may seek \ncollateral relief by way of a PRP following an unsuccessful direct \nappeal. RAP 16.4; In re Coats, 173 Wn.2d 123, 140, 267 P.3d 324 \n(2011). In a PRP, appellate courts may grant a defendant relief \nwhere: \nThe conviction was obtained . . .  in violation of the \nConstitution of the United States or the Constitution or laws \nof the State of Washington; or . . .  \n \nOther grounds exist for a collateral attack on a judgment in a \ncriminal proceeding . . . instituted by the state or local \ngovernment . . . \n \nRAP 16.4(c)(2, 5). The petitioner must prove the existence of an \nerror by a preponderance of the evidence. In re Monschke, 160 \nWn. App. 479, 490, 251 P.3d 884 (2010). \nWhen the alleged error is constitutional in nature, the \npetitioner must show that it caused actual and substantial prejudice \nto be entitled to relief. Matter of Sandoval, 189 Wn.2d 811, 821, \n408 P.3d 675 (2018) (citing Coats, 173 Wn.2d at 132). Criminal \n\n--- Page 11 ---\n \n- 6 - \ndefendants in Washington have a due process right to a fair trial \nbefore an impartial judge. In re Swenson, 158 Wn. App. 812, 818, \n244 P.3d 959 (2010) (citing Wash. Const. art. I, § 22). \na. \nMr. Ayerst’s is entitled to relief because the trial \ncourt’s \nimpartiality \ncan \nreasonably \nbe \nquestioned due to multiple violations of \nWashington’s Code of Judicial Conduct \n \nMr. Ayerst suffered actual and substantial prejudice because \nhis trial was held before a judge who could not ensure due process \nwas satisfied where the judge was arrested and charged with rape \nagainst members of his own staff. The allegations alone call into \nquestion Gallina’s ability to fulfill his judicial obligations which at a \nminimum require compliance with the law. Code of Judicial Conduct \n(CJC) RULE 1.1 provides in part: \nCompliance with the Law \nA judge shall comply with the law, including the Code \nof Judicial Conduct. \nId. Furthermore, CJC 1.2 provides: \nPromoting Confidence in the Judiciary \nA judge shall act at all times in a manner that promotes \npublic confidence in the independence, integrity, and \nimpartiality of the judiciary, and shall avoid impropriety and \nthe appearance of impropriety. \n\n--- Page 12 ---\n \n- 7 - \n \nId. Other provisions in the CJC directly address the type of \nallegations Judge Gallina currently faces. CJC 2.3 provides: \n \nBias, Prejudice, and Harassment \n \n(A) A judge shall perform the duties of judicial office, \nincluding administrative duties, without bias or \nprejudice. \n \n(B) A judge shall not, in the performance of \njudicial duties, by words or conduct manifest bias \nor prejudice, or engage in harassment, including \nbut not limited to bias, prejudice, or harassment \nbased upon race, sex, gender, religion, national \norigin, ethnicity, disability, age, sexual orientation, \nmarital status, socioeconomic status, or political \naffiliation, and shall not permit court staff, court \nofficials, or others subject to the judge's direction and \ncontrol to do so. \n \nId. (emphasis added). The comments to this rule explain that:  \n[3] Harassment, as referred to in paragraphs (B) and \n(C), is verbal or physical conduct that denigrates or \nshows hostility or aversion toward a person on bases \nsuch as race, sex, gender, religion, national origin, \nethnicity, disability, age, sexual orientation, marital \nstatus, socioeconomic status, or political affiliation. \n \n[4] Sexual harassment includes but is not limited to \nsexual advances, requests for sexual favors, and \nother verbal or physical conduct of a sexual nature \nthat is unwelcome. \n \nId.  \n\n--- Page 13 ---\n \n- 8 - \nFinally, CJC 2.3 also requires a judge act impartially and fair. \nThis means: \nA judge shall uphold and apply the law and, shall perform all \nduties of judicial office fairly and impartially. \n \nId.  \nThe allegations of rape and sexual assault against Judge \nGallina suggest that he violated multiple provisions of the CJC \nduring his time as an Asotin County Superior Court Judge. This \nalleged pattern of misconduct raises serious concerns about Judge \nGallina’s ability to dispense justice in a fair and impartial manner. \nBecause Judge Gallina’s impartiality can reasonably be questioned, \nMr. Ayerst was denied his due process right to a fair trial. See CJC \n2.11(A). This error entitles him to relief under RAP 16.4(c)(2). \n2. \nJUDGE \nGALLINA’S \nROLE \nAS \nPRESIDING \nJUDGE \nDURING \nMR. \nAYERST’S \nTRIAL \nVIOLATES \nTHE \nAPPEARANCE \nOF \nFAIRNESS \nDOCTRINE \n \nIn addition to abiding by the CJC, judges must also adhere \nto the appearance of fairness doctrine. Swenson, 158 Wn. App. at \n818 (citing State v. Dominguez, 81 Wn. App. 325, 328, 914 P.2d \n141 (1996)). This doctrine requires a judge to disqualify himself \nfrom a proceeding if he is biased against a party or if the judge’s \n\n--- Page 14 ---\n \n- 9 - \nimpartiality could reasonably be questioned. Swenson, 158 Wn. \nApp. at 818 (citing CJC 2.11(A)).  \nWashington courts have consistently held that that the \nappearance of fairness in judicial proceedings is especially \nimportant in the context of criminal proceedings. State v. \nChambers, \n176 \nWn.2d \n573, \n293 \nP.3d \n1185 \n(2013); State v. Finch, 181 \nWn. \nApp. \n387, \n326 P.3d 148 (2014); State v. Gamble, 168 Wn.2d 161, 187, 225 \nP.3d 973 (2010); State v. Ra, 144 Wn. App. 688, 175 P.3d 609 \n(2008).  \nIn Finch, this court held that a trial judge, who was presiding \nover two separate cases involving a minor child, violated the \nappearance of fairness doctrine because the judge was unable to \nseparate the two cases and his role as the impartial judge versus \nthe investigator/prosecutor when he attempted to investigate the \ntruth of the minor child's allegations in the criminal proceeding by \nordering a polygraph in the juvenile proceeding. Id. This Court held \nthat the trial court’s inability to maintain its neutral role and \ninsistence on using the juvenile proceeding to investigate the \nveracity of the allegations in the criminal case violated the \n\n--- Page 15 ---\n \n- 10 - \nappearance of fairness doctrine and ordered remand before a new \njudge. Finch, 181 Wn. App. at 399. \nIn Ra, this Court noted that the trial court's comments on the \ndefendant's \ncharacter \nand \nscolding \nthe \ndefendant \nwere \ninappropriate, and “did not show proper restraint, and should not \nhave been made.” 144 Wn. App. at 705. The Ra court did not, \nhowever, decide whether the appearance of partiality warranted \nreversal, because it had reversed on another issue, but it did order \nthat the new sentencing hearing be held before a different \njudge. Id. Although not dispositive, Ra is nevertheless instructive of \nwhat this Court has previously noted as inappropriate judicial \nbehavior. \nFar worse than in Finch and Ra, here, Judge Gallina is \naccused of raping and sexually assaulting his staff. He was \nremoved from his duties pending trial. These allegations alone go \nto the heart of the notion of fairness and impartiality because a \njudge accused of violent crimes does not appear able to behave in \na lawful and impartial manner. Judge Gallina’s alleged behavior, \nbrought to light by multiple victims, demonstrates his contempt for \nthe law and his colleagues. Regardless of whether he is convicted, \n\n--- Page 16 ---\n \n- 11 - \npending such charges, the appearance of fairness is destroyed \nbecause a reasonably prudent, disinterested observer would be \nunable to conclude that the parties received a fair, impartial, and \nneutral hearing. \nThis court should reverse Mr. Ayerst’s conviction and grant \nhim relief in the form of a new trial. RAP 16.4(a). \n3. \nAYERST WAS DENIED HIS RIGHT TO \nEFFECTIVE \nASSISTANCE \nOF \nCOUNSEL \nWHEN \nHIS \nTRIAL \nCOUNSEL WAS NOT LICENSED TO \nPRACTICE LAW IN WASHINGTON \nAND \nNEVER \nCOMPLETED \nTHE \nREQUIREMENTS OF APR 8 \n \nMr. Ayerst was denied his constitutional right to counsel by \nthe appointment and representation of Mr. Van Idour, who was \nnever licensed to practice law in Washington State. \nThe Sixth Amendment guarantees indigent defendants in \nstate and federal criminal proceedings, appointed counsel in any \ncase in which a term of imprisonment is imposed. U.S. Const. \nAmend. VI, XIV; U.S. v. Bryant, 136 S.Ct. 1954 (2016); Gideon v. \nWainwright, 372 U.S. 335, 83 S.Ct. 792, 9 L.Ed.2d 799 (1963); \nState v. Johnson, 179 Wn.2d 534, 315 P.3d 1090 (2014), cert. \ndenied, 135 S.Ct. 139, 190 L.Ed.2d 105 (2014). \n\n--- Page 17 ---\n \n- 12 - \nArt. I, § 22 (amend 10) provides the equivalent right to \nappointed counsel. State v. Roberts, 142 Wn.2d 471, 515, 14 P.3d \n713 (2000). The purpose of this right is to guarantee all defendants \nthe right to a fair trial. Wheat v. United States, 486 U.S. 153, 158-\n59, 108 S.Ct. 1692, 100 L.Ed.2d 140 (1988). \n“[T]he assistance of counsel is among those ‘constitutional \nrights so basic to a fair trial that their infraction can never be treated \nas harmless error’”. Holloway v. Arkansas, 435 U.S. 475, 489, 98 \nS.Ct. 1173, 55 L.Ed.2d 426 (1978) (citing Chapman v. California, \n386 U.S. 18, 43, 87 S.Ct. 824, 17 L.Ed.2d 705 (1967)). The right to \ncounsel granted by the Sixth and Fourteenth Amendments means \nat least that a person is entitled to the help of a licensed attorney. \nBrewer v. Williams, 430 U.S. 387, 398, 97 S.Ct. 1232, 51 L.Ed.2d \n424 (1977); City of Seattle v. Ratliff, 100 Wn.2d 212, 217, 667 P.2d \n630 (1983)  \n “counsel” \nas \nused \nin \nthe Sixth Amendment and Const. art. 1, § 22 includes \nonly those persons authorized by the courts \nto practice law.  \n \nId; see also United States v. Turnbull, 888 F.2d 636, 638 (9th Cir. \n1989); Solina v. United States, 709 F.2d 160, 167 (1983). \n“Nothing in the language or the history of the sixth \n\n--- Page 18 ---\n \n- 13 - \namendment \nto \nthe \nConstitution” \ncontemplates \npermitting \nrepresentation by persons who are not qualified attorneys because \nunlicensed lay representation does not “further” the objectives of \nthe Sixth Amendment. United States v. Wright, 568 F.2d 142, 143 \n(9th Cir 1978). \n“[The] right of one charged with crime to counsel may not be \ndeemed fundamental and essential to fair trials in some countries, \nbut it is in ours.” Gideon, 372 U.S. at 344. “Reason and reflection \nrequire us to recognize that in our adversary system of criminal \njustice, any person hauled into court, who is too poor to hire a \nlawyer, cannot be assured a fair trial unless counsel is provided for \nhim. This seems to us to be an obvious truth.” Gideon, 372 U.S. at \n344. \nRepresentation by a person not licensed to practice before \nthe court in which he or she appears does not satisfy a defendant’s \nSixth Amendment right to counsel and voids any proceeding in \nwhich the person appears. Solina, 709 U.S. at 167 (citing Harrison \nv. United States, 387 F.2d 2013, 212-13 (1967), reversed on other \ngrounds, 392 U.S. 219, 88 S.Ct. 2008, 20 L.Ed.2d 1047 (1968) \n(citing Johnson v. Zerbst, 304 U.S. 458, 468 (1938))). \n\n--- Page 19 ---\n \n- 14 - \nFor example, in Solina, the defendant was represented by a \nperson who went to law school but never passed the bar, although \nfrom the record he appeared skilled. Solina, 709 F.2d at 160-65. \nThe United States argued that this should issue should be \nconsidered under a harmless error standard. Id. The Court, citing \nZerbst, reversed and remanded for a new trial holding that the state \nwas incorrect to suggest a harmless error standard and \nadmonished that “prosecutors, trial courts, or both, would do well to \nmake sure that persons representing criminal defendants are duly \nlicensed to practice.” Solina, 709 F.2d at 169 fn. 10; Zerbst, 304 \nU.S. at 465-69.  \n“[O]ne never admitted to practice law and therefore who \nnever acquired the threshold qualification to represent a client in [a \nWashington State] court cannot be allowed to do so . . . for \npurposes of the Sixth Amendment.” United States v. Mouzin, 785 \nF.2d 682, 697 (9th Cir. 1986). Other circuits agree with this holding. \nSee, e.g., Solina, 709 F.2d at 168; Harrison, 387 F.2d at 212-14. \nIt is structural error “if an indigent defendant is denied an \nattorney” because the proceeding is then a “fundamentally unfair” \none. Weaver v. Massachusetts, ___ U.S. ___, 137 S.Ct. 1899, \n\n--- Page 20 ---\n \n- 15 - \n1903, 198 L.Ed.2d 420 (2017) (citing Gideon, 372 U.S. at 343–\n345 (emphasis added)). The Court in Weaver explained that \n“structural error”: \nmeans only that the government is not entitled to \ndeprive the defendant of a new trial by showing that \nthe error was “harmless beyond a reasonable \ndoubt.” Chapman, 386 U.S. at 24. \n \nWeaver, 137 S.Ct at 1908.1. \nOnce Mr. Ayerst was appointed counsel by the trial court, he \nwas entitled to representation by a Washington State licensed \nlawyer at all critical stages of his trial. Here, under the state and \nfederal constitutions and the legal precedent cited herein, Mr. \nAyerst was denied his constitutional right to counsel because Mr. \nVan Idour was never a licensed lawyer in Washington State. \nZerbst, 304 U.S. 458; Gideon, 372 U.S. 335; Solina, 709 F.2d 160. \nThe remedy for violation of Mr. Ayerst’s right to counsel \nrequires automatic reversal. Gideon, 372 U.S. at 343-45. \nD. \nCONCLUSION \n \nMr. Ayerst is unlawfully restrained because his conviction \nwas obtained in violation of Washington State law and he was \ndenied his constitutional right to effective assistance of counsel \n \n1 Burden on defendant to show prejudice in certain ineffective assistance of \ncounsel claims involving public trial rights not objected to during trial. \n\n--- Page 21 ---\n \n- 16 - \nduring trial. Mr. Ayerst respectfully requests that this court grant him \nrelief by reversing his convictions and ordering a new trial. \n \nDATED this 27th day of May 2020. \nRespectfully submitted, \n \n \n \n______________________________ \nLISE ELLNER, WSBA No. 20955 \nAttorney for Petitioner  \n \n________\n \nSPENCER BABBITT, WSBA No. 51076 \nAttorney for Petitioner \n \n \nI, Lise Ellner, a person over the age of 18 years of age, served the \nAsotin County Prosecutor’s Office bnichols@co.asotin.wa.us and \ncliedkie@co.asotin.wa.us and Robert Ayerst/DOC#291791, \nWashington State Penitentiary, 1313 North 13th Avenue, Walla \nWalla, WA 99362 a true copy of the document to which this \ncertificate is affixed on May 27, 2020. Service was made by \nelectronically to the prosecutor and Robert Ayerst by depositing in \nthe mails of the United States of America, properly stamped and \naddressed. \n \n_____________________________________________Signature \n \n\n--- Page 22 ---\n \n \nAPPENDIX A \n \n\n--- Page 23 ---\n \n \n \n \n \n\n--- Page 24 ---\n \n \n \n \n \n\n--- Page 25 ---\n \n \n \n \n\n--- Page 26 ---\n \n \n \n \n \n\n--- Page 27 ---\n \n \n \n \n \n\n--- Page 28 ---\n \n \n \n \n \n\n--- Page 29 ---\n \n \nAPPENDIX B \n \n\n--- Page 30 ---\n \n \nEmail exchange \n \nFrom: Katrin Johnson [mailto:katrin.johnson@opd.wa.gov]  \nSent: Tuesday, May 7, 2019 2:51 PM \nTo: liseellnerlaw@comcast.net \nSubject: FW: Clarification - Application for Public Defense \nImprovement Funds \n \nFrom: Chris Kemp [mailto:ckemp@co.asotin.wa.us]  \nSent: Thursday, September 21, 2017 2:08 PM \nTo: Katrin Johnson <katrin.johnson@opd.wa.gov> \nSubject: RE: Clarification - Application for Public Defense \nImprovement Funds \n \nHi Katrin, \n \nMr. Van Idour is under the supervision of Neil Cox, Pro Hac Vice, \nand we are currently waiting on WSBA (see below).  \n \n \nFrom: Barbara Ochota [barbarao@wsba.org] \nSent: Wednesday, September 6, 2017 9:59 AM \nTo: Tammy L. Tenny \nSubject: RE: Status Inquiry \n \nGood morning. \n \nI can only tell you that it is still being reviewed by regulatory service \ncounsel. \n \nBest regards, \n \n[WSBA-Logo-Seal-PMS2188-PNG] \nBarbara Ochota| Admissions Analyst \nWashington State Bar Association | 206 727 8266 \n1325 Fourth Avenue, Suite 600 | Seattle, WA 98101-2539 | • \nbarbarao@wsba.org<mailto:barbarao@wsba.org> \nwww.wsba.org<http://www.wsba.org/> \nThe WSBA is committed to full access and participation by persons \nwith disabilities. If you have questions about accessibility or require \n\n--- Page 31 ---\n \n \naccommodation please contact \nbarbarao@wsba.org<mailto:barbarao@wsba.org>. \n \n \nFrom: Tammy L. Tenny [mailto:TTenny@co.asotin.wa.us] \nSent: Tuesday, September 05, 2017 11:31 AM \nTo: Barbara Ochota \nSubject: Status Inquiry \n \n \nHello Barbara:  I am sending this e-mail regarding status inquiry of \nour contracted defense attorney, Robert VanIdour.  Thank you. \n \nTammy Tenny | Superior Court Administrator Asotin, Columbia & \nGarfield Counties P.O. Box 159, 135 2nd Street, Asotin, WA 99402 \nPh: (509) 243-2082 Fax: (509) 243-2072 \nttenny@co.asotin.wa.us<mailto:ttenny@co.asotin.wa.us> \n \nChris Kemp \nAsotin County  \nCFO/Risk Manager \nckemp@co.asotin.wa.us \n509-243-2078 \n509-243-2089 - Fax \n \nFrom: Katrin Johnson [mailto:katrin.johnson@opd.wa.gov]  \nSent: Wednesday, September 20, 2017 5:09 PM \nTo: Chris Kemp \nSubject: Clarification - Application for Public Defense Improvement \nFunds \n \nMs. Kemp, good morning. \n \nOur office is currently reviewing the applications submitted by \ncounties for 2018 public defense improvement funds, and we are \nseeking one clarification regarding the application submitted by \nAsotin County. \n \nThe application indicates that Robert J. Van Idour currently has a \nnew contract for representation on felony cases. However, based \n\n--- Page 32 ---\n \n \non the Washington State Bar Association’s website, he appears to \nnot be a licensed attorney within Washington. We were curious as \nto what arrangement might be in place for an out-of-state attorney \nto take on a full-time felony contract. \n \nThank you in advance, \n \nKatrin Johnson \nPublic Defense Services Manager \nWashington State Office of Public Defense \nwww.opd.wa.gov \nT: 360.586.3164, ext. 108 \n \n \n \nFrom: Chris Kemp [mailto:ckemp@co.asotin.wa.us]  \nSent: Thursday, September 21, 2017 2:08 PM \nTo: Katrin Johnson <katrin.johnson@opd.wa.gov> \nSubject: RE: Clarification - Application for Public Defense \nImprovement Funds \n \nHi Katrin, \n \nMr. Van Idour is under the supervision of Neil Cox, Pro Hac \nVice, and we are currently waiting on WSBA (see below).  \n \nFrom: Barbara Ochota [barbarao@wsba.org] \nSent: Wednesday, September 6, 2017 9:59 AM \nTo: Tammy L. Tenny \nSubject: RE: Status Inquiry \n \nGood morning. \n \nI can only tell you that it is still being reviewed by regulatory service \ncounsel. \n \nBest regards, \n \n[WSBA-Logo-Seal-PMS2188-PNG] \n\n--- Page 33 ---\n \n \nBarbara Ochota| Admissions Analyst \nWashington State Bar Association | 206 727 8266 \n1325 Fourth Avenue, Suite 600 | Seattle, WA 98101-2539 | • \nbarbarao@wsba.org<mailto:barbarao@wsba.org> \nwww.wsba.org<http://www.wsba.org/> \nThe WSBA is committed to full access and participation by persons \nwith disabilities. If you have questions about accessibility or require \naccommodation please contact \nbarbarao@wsba.org<mailto:barbarao@wsba.org>. \n \n \nFrom: Tammy L. Tenny [mailto:TTenny@co.asotin.wa.us] \nSent: Tuesday, September 05, 2017 11:31 AM \nTo: Barbara Ochota \nSubject: Status Inquiry \n \n \nHello Barbara:  I am sending this e-mail regarding status inquiry of \nour contracted defense attorney, Robert VanIdour.  Thank you. \n \nTammy Tenny | Superior Court Administrator Asotin, Columbia & \nGarfield Counties P.O. Box 159, 135 2nd Street, Asotin, WA 99402 \nPh: (509) 243-2082 Fax: (509) 243-2072 \nttenny@co.asotin.wa.us<mailto:ttenny@co.asotin.wa.us> \n \n \n \nChris Kemp \nAsotin County  \nCFO/Risk Manager \nckemp@co.asotin.wa.us \n509-243-2078 \n509-243-2089 - Fax \n \nFrom: Katrin Johnson [mailto:katrin.johnson@opd.wa.gov]  \nSent: Wednesday, September 20, 2017 5:09 PM \nTo: Chris Kemp \nSubject: Clarification - Application for Public Defense Improvement \nFunds \n \n\n--- Page 34 ---\n \n \nMs. Kemp, good morning. \n \nOur office is currently reviewing the applications submitted by \ncounties for 2018 public defense improvement funds, and we are \nseeking one clarification regarding the application submitted by \nAsotin County. \n \nThe application indicates that Robert J. Van Idour currently \nhas a new contract for representation on felony cases. \nHowever, based on the Washington State Bar Association’s \nwebsite, he appears to not be a licensed attorney within \nWashington. We were curious as to what arrangement might \nbe in place for an out-of-state attorney to take on a full-time \nfelony contract. \n \nThank you in advance, \n \nKatrin Johnson \nPublic Defense Services Manager \nWashington State Office of Public Defense \nwww.opd.wa.gov \nT: 360.586.3164, ext. 108 \n \n \n\n--- Page 35 ---\nLAW OFFICES OF LISE ELLNER\nMay 27, 2020 - 9:58 AM\nTransmittal Information\nFiled with Court:\nCourt of Appeals Division III\nAppellate Court Case Number:   36965-0\nAppellate Court Case Title:\nPersonal Restraint Petition of Robert Lloyd Ayerst\nSuperior Court Case Number:\n16-1-00150-7\nThe following documents have been uploaded:\n369650_Briefs_20200527095548D3131155_5524.pdf \n    This File Contains: \n     Briefs - Petitioners - Modifier: Supplemental \n     The Original File Name was Ayerst Supplemental Brief .pdf\n369650_Other_Filings_20200527095548D3131155_6284.pdf \n    This File Contains: \n     Other Filings - Appearance \n     The Original File Name was Ayerst Notice of Appearance.pdf\nA copy of the uploaded files will be sent to:\nbabbitts@seattleu.edu\nbnichols@co.asotin.wa.us\ncliedkie@co.asotin.wa.us\nComments:\nSender Name: Valerie Greenup - Email: valerie.liseellner@gmail.com \nAddress: \nPO Box 1370 \nYelm, WA, 98597 \nPhone: (360) 451-7328\nNote: The Filing Id is 20200527095548D3131155\n", 'source': 'text_input'}
