2025-07-29 18:12:43 - src.vue_api_endpoints - INFO - JSON data received: {'text': '\n--- Page 1 ---\nFILED \nCourt of Appeals \nDivision Ill \nState of Washington \n6123/2020 1 :44 PM \nNo. 369650-0-111 \nIN THE COURT OF THE APPEALS \nOF THE STATE OF WASHINGTON \nIn Re Personal Restraint Petition of: \nROBERT L. AYERST, \nPetitioner. \nRESPONSE TO SUPPLEMENTAL BRIEF OF PETITIONER \nP. 0. Box220 \nAsotin, Washington 99402 \n(509) 243-2061 \nCURT L. LIEDKIE \nAsotin County Chief Deputy \nProsecuting Attorney \nWSBA#30371 \nNo. 99941-4\n\n--- Page 2 ---\nTABLE OF CONTENTS \nPage \nTABLE OF AUTHORITIES .... ... . .. . .. . , . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . ii \nI. \nSUMMARY OF ISSUES .... , . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 1 \nII. \nSUMMARY OF ARGUMENT . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 1 \nIll. \nSTATEMENT OF THE CASE ............ . .. . .. . . . . 2 \nIV. \nDISCUSSION ......... . . . ..... . .. , . . . . . . . . . . . .. . . 4 \n1. \nPETITIONER HAS FAILED TO DEMONSTRATE \nANY ACTUAL OR POTENTIAL BIAS ON THE PART \nOF JUDGE GALLINA. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 6 \n2. \nPETITIONER HAS FAILED TO DEMONSTRATE HE \nWAS DENIED THE RIGHT TO COUNSEL WHERE \nHE WAS REPRESENTED BY A LICENSED \nATTORNEY IN GOOD STANDING WITH THE \nIDAHO STATE BAR... . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 11 \nV. \nCONCLUSION . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 15 \n\n--- Page 3 ---\nTABLE OF AUTHORITIES \nU. S. Supreme Court Cases \nPeters v. Kiff, 407 U.S. 493, 92 S.Ct. 2163, \n33 L.Ed.2d 83 (1972). . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 6 \nOther Federal Cases \nFerrara v. Keane, 806 F.Supp. 472 (S.D.N.Y. 1992), \naffd, 2 F.3d 403 (2d Cir. 1993) ................. .. .. 13 \nUnited States v. Boney. 977 F.2d 624 (D.C. Cir. 1992).. . 11 \nUnited States v. Bradford, 238 F.2d 395 (2d. Cir. 1956) ... 13 \nUnited States v. Mouzin, 785 F.2d 682 (9th Cir. 1986) .. 12, 14 \nUnited States v. Novak, 903 F.2d 883 (2d. Cir. 1990) .. j 1, 12 \nSolina v. United States, 709 F.2d 160 (2d. Cir. 1983). . . . 12 \nState Supreme Court Cases \nIn re Pers. Restraint Cross, 180 Wn.2d 664, \n327 P.3d 660 (2014) ................... . . .. . .. . . . 4,5 \nIn re Pers. Restraint Davis, 152 Wn.2d 647, \n101 P.3d 1 (2004) ..................... ....... ... 11 \nIn re Pers. Restraint Elmore, 162 Wn.2d 236, \n172 P .3d 335 (2007) .................. •..• . , . . . . . . 6 \nIn re Pers. Restraint Hagler, 97 Wn.2d 818, \n650 P.2d 1103 (1982) . .. . ............. •. .... . .. .. 4,5 \nIn re Pers. Restraint Music, 104 Wn.2d 189, \n704 P.2d 144 (1985) ..... . ............ ............ 6 \nIn re Pers. Restraint Rice, 118 Wn.2d 876, \n828 P.2d 1086 (1992) ... . ... . . . . .. . .. . .. ... . .. . ... 5 \nii \n\n--- Page 4 ---\nState v. Alvarado, 164 Wn.2d 556, \n192 P.3d 345 (2008)(en bane) ..... . . .. . .. . .... . ..... 3 \nState v. Chamberlin, 161 Wn.2d 30, \n162 P.3d 389 (2007). . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . • . . . 9 \nState v. McFarland, 127 Wn.2d 322, \n899 P.2d 1251 (1995). . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 11 \nState Court of Appeals Cases \nIn re Pers. Restraint Monschke, 160 Wn. App. 479, \n251 P.3d 884 (2010) ........... ... . .. . .....•... .. . 5 \nIn re Swenson, 158 Wn.App. 812, \n244 P.3d 959 (2010) ............. . . , .. . ...... ...•. 7,9 \nMatter of Dependency of A.E.T.H., 9 Wn.App.2d 502, \n446 P.3d 667 (2019) .............. ... . . . .. . .. .. ... 6 \nState v. Dominguez, 81 Wn.App. 325, \n914 P.2d 141 (1996) . .. • .•.•. ... .. ..... . .. . .... .. . 7 \nStatutes \nRCW 2.36.070 • . . . .. ... . .. . .... . .. .. . .. . .. .. . .. 10 \nCourt Rules \nCJCRP 6 .... . .. . . . . .. . . . . . .. . .. . ..... .. . .. ..... 6 \nState Constitution Provisions \nConst. art. 4, § 31 . . . . . • . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 6 \nOther State Court Cases \nIn Interest of McFall, A.2d 707, 533 Pa. 24 (1992). , .. . . 7,8 \nIn re Bruno, 101 A.3d 635, 627 Pa. 505 (Pa. 2014 ). . . . . . . . 9 \nPeople v. Felder, 47 N.Y.2d 287, \n391 N.E.2d 1274 (1979) .......................... 12 \niii \n\n--- Page 5 ---\nI. \nISSUES \n1. \nIS PETITIONER ENTITLED TO RELIEF WHERE THE \nRECORD \nDOES \nNOT \nREFLECT, \nNOR HAS \nPETITIONER SHOWN, ANY ACTUAL OR POTENTIAL \nBIAS OF THE PRESIDING JUDGE? \n2. \nIS PETITIONER ENTITLED TO RELIEF WHERE HE \nWAS REPRESENTED BY AN ATTORNEY LICENSED \nIN IDAHO, AND HE HAS NOT DEMONSTRATED ANY \nDEFICIENT REPRESENTATION NOR RESULTING \nPREJUDICE? \nII. \nARGUMENT \n1. \nPETITIONER HAS FAILED TO DEMONSTRATE ANY \nACTUAL OR POTENTIAL BIAS ON THE PART OF \nJUDGE GALLINA. \n2. \nPETITIONER HAS FAILED TO DEMONSTRATE HE \nWAS DENIED THE RIGHT TO COUNSEL WHERE HE \nWAS.REPRESENTED BY A LICENSED ATTORNEY \nIN GOOD STANDING WITH THE IDAHO STATE BAR. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n1 \n\n--- Page 6 ---\nIll. STATEMENT OF THE CASE \nThe State incorporates by reference the facts set forth in its \ninitial "Response to Personal Restraint Petition" and any documents \nattached thereto. It further supplements its response as follows: \nFACTS \nThe Petitioner was charged with Attempted Second Degree \nBurglary, Second Degree Malicious Mischief, and subsequently Bail \nJumping (C felony), stemming from events that occurred on \nSeptember 6, 2016. See State v. Ayerst, 35867-4-111, 2019 WL \n1568060(Div. Ill, Apr. 11, 2019) (unpublished)1• Substantial evidence \nwas presented at trial, specifically, a security camera video which \nplaced Petitioner in a car wash parking lot at 2:30 in the morning. \nAyerst. With him was an unidentified passenger carrying what \nappeared to be a tow strap, which was later found attached to the \nequipment room doors. Ayerst. Petitioner was seen backing his truck \nin the direction of the equipment room doors, and the video showed \nhis headlights l\'dipped" as he pulled away from the door, indicating the \ntruck was experiencing resistance due to being attached to the doors. \nAyerst. Petitioner was later seen by an officer sitting in a parking lot \nacross the street from the car wash, suggesting he was waiting to see \nif he had been observed before returning to remove property from the \n1The State only cites to the unpublished opinion for purposes of establishing the \nprocedural and factual background of the case. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n2 \n\n--- Page 7 ---\nequipment room. Ayerst. This substantial evidence led a jury to \nsubsequently find Petitioner guilty of all three charges. Ayerst. \nAt sentencing, the State requested an exceptional upward \nsentence pursuantto RCW 9.94A.535(2)(c)2, based upon Petitioner\'s \ncommission of multiple crimes and offender score of 22. Ayerst. The \npresiding judge, Judge Scott D. Gallina, rejected the State\'s request, \nand instead imposed a mid-range standard sentence of 55.5 months, \nwell below the high-end sentence range. Ayerst. More than a year \nafter Petitioner\'s trial and sentencing, Judge Gallina was arrested and \ncharged with unrelated sexual felony and gross misdemeanor \ncharges. See State of Washington v. Scott D. Gallina, Asotin Superior \nCourt Cause 19-1-00066-02. \nPetitioner was initially represented by John Fay, who was \nlater replaced by attorney Robert Van ldour who represented the \nPetitioner through trial. Bond Order (10/04/16) and Motion and \nOrder Allowing Withdrawal and Substitution. Mr. Van ldour had \ncontracted with Asotin County and had arranged to practice under \nAPR B(c), which authorizes attorneys who are licensed by other \nstates to appear for the purposes of public defense while they \napply for a formal license to practice in Washington. Declaration of \nRobert J. Van ldour. Mr. Van ldour also applied for formal \n2 The State\'s argument was based upon the commonly known "free crimes" doctrine. See \nState v. Alvarado, 164 Wn.2d 556, 192 P.3d 345 (2008) (en bane). \n· \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n3 \n\n--- Page 8 ---\nadmission to the Washington State Bar by motion under APR 3(c). \nSee Petitioner\'s Appendix A. He was later notified that his \napplication for admission by motion under APR 3(c) was accepted, \nand that his application for limited admission under APR 8(c) had \nbeen denied. Petitioner\'s Appendix A. Mr. Van ldour was, and still \nis, a member in good standing with the Idaho State Bar \nAssociation. Declaration of Robert J. Van ldour. \nThe Petitioner appealed his conviction, arguing, among \nother things, insufficiency of the evidence. See State v. Ayerst. His \nappeal was denied and his conviction affirmed. Petitioner then filed \na Personal Restraint Petition. Petitioner\'s Supplemental Brief was \nsubsequently filed on May 27, 2020, alleging that Petitioner is \nentitled to relief based on Judge Gallina\'s alleged criminal conduct \nand the fact that Mr. Van ldour was not licensed in the State of \nWashington. The Petitioner makes no showing that Mr. Van ldour \ncommitted any act of misfeasance or nonfeasance, nor does he \notherwise claim any resulting prejudice. \nIV. DISCUSSION \nA PRP is not a substitute for a direct appeal. In re Pers. \nRestraint of Hagler, 97 Wn.2d 818, 824, 650 P.2d 1103 (1982). \nCourts are reluctant to overturn a settled judicial decision where the \nPetitioner has had an opportunity for direct appellate review. In re \nPers. Restraint of Cross, 180 Wn.2d 664, 676, 327 P. 3d 660 \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n4 \n\n--- Page 9 ---\n(2014). Therefore, there are limitations on the use of a PRP to \ncollaterally attack a conviction. Hagler, 97 Wn.2d at 824. A \npersonal restraint petition must state with particularity the factual \nallegations which, if proven, would entitle the petitioner to relief. In \nre Pers. Restraint of Rice, 118 Wn.2d 876, 886, 828 P.2d 1086 \n(1992). Factual allegations must be supported by evidence. 118 \nWn.2d at 886. If the record does not support the factual \nallegations, then the petitioner must show through affidavits or \nother forms of corroboration that competent and admissible \nevidence will establish the factual allegations. 118 Wn.2d at 886. \nBald assertions and conclusory allegations are not sufficient. Id. \nTo be entitled to collateral relief through a PRP the \nPetitioner must prove the existence of error by a preponderance of \nthe evidence. In re Pers. Restraint of Monschke, 160 Wn. App. \n479, 490, 251 P. 3d 884 (2010). Assuming error can be shown, he \nis also required to prove prejudice. Cross, 180 Wn.2d at 676. The \nsignificance of the level of prejudice depends on the type of error \nshown. Id. To be entitled to relief for a constitutional error, a \npetitioner must also prove by a preponderance of the evidence that \nthe error caused "actual and substantial prejudice." Id. (Emphasis \nadded). This prejudice "must be determined in light of the totality \nof circumstances," and exists only if the error "so infected \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n5 \n\n--- Page 10 ---\npetitioner\' s entire trial that the resulting conviction violates due \nprocess." In re Pers. Restraint of Music, 104 Wn.2d 189, 191, 704 \nP.2d 144 ( 1985). \nTo be entitled to relief for a nonconstitutional error, a \npetitioner must prove by a preponderance of the evidence that the \nerror caused "a fundamental defect resulting in a complete \nmiscarriage of justice." In re Pers. Restraint of Elmore, 162 \nWn.2d 236,251, 172 P.3d 335 (2007) (emphasis added). \n1. \nPETITIONER HAS FAILED TO DEMONSTRATE ANY \nACTUAL OR POTENTIAL BIAS ON THE PART OF JUDGE \nGALLINA. \nPetitioner first argues that the alleged criminal conduct of \nJudge Gallina violates Washington\'s Code of Judicial Conduct \n(CJC) and therefore calls into question the validity of Petitioner\'s \nconviction. It is true that violations of the CJC could result in \ndisciplinary action against Judge Gallina, including removal from \noffice. Const. art. 4, § 31; CJCRP 6. However, Petitioner has not \ndemonstrated how this alleged misconduct "create[d] the likelihood \nor the appearance of bias" as to violate his due process right to a \nfair trial. Matter of Dependency of A.E.T.H., 9 Wn.App.2d 502, 517, \n446 P.3d 667 (2019), quoting Peters v. Kiff, 407 U.S. 493, 502 \n(1972). \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n6 \n\n--- Page 11 ---\nPetitioner next argues that Judge Gallina\'s alleged criminal \nconduct violated the appearance of fairness doctrine. However, \neven if such criminal conduct occurred, Petitioner has failed to \nshow any resulting actual or potential bias against Petitioner. \nDue Process and the appearance of fairness doctrine \nrequire a judge to disqualify himself if he is biased against a party, \nor the judge\'s impartiality may be reasonably questioned. In re \nSwenson, 158 Wn.App. 812,818,244 P.3d 959 (2010). A judicial \nproceeding satisfies the appearance of fairness doctrine if "a \nreasonably prudent and disinterested observer would conclude [the \ndefendant] obtained a fair, impartial, and neutral trial." State v. \nDominguez, 81 Wn.App. 325, 330, 914 P.2d 141 (1996). Without \nevidence of actual or potential bias, an appearance of fairness \nclaim is meritless. Swenson, 158 Wn.App. at 818. Petitioner has \nprovided no case law that supports the contention that allegations \nof unrelated criminal conduct and subsequent filing of criminal \ncharges necessarily inhibits a trial judge\'s impartiality. \nThe State was only able to find a few cases involving the \ncriminal conduct of judges. In those cases, however, the judges \nhad either an interest in gaining prosecutorial leniency for their own \ncriminal conduct, or a personal or pecuniary interest in the outcome \nof the case. For example, In Interest of McFall, 617 A.2d 707, 711 , \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n7 \n\n--- Page 12 ---\n533 Pa. 24 (1992), several defendant\'s sought new trials based \nupon allegations that the judge continued to preside over criminal \nand juvenile matters while working as an F.B.I. informant. McFall, \nat 32. In McFall, a quid pro quo bargain existed where the judge\'s \ncooperation would be made known to the same authorities who \nappeared before her to prosecute cases against the appellees. \nThere, the Pennsylvania Supreme Court determined that the \nappearances of fairness mandated new trials, not because of the \nunderlying criminal act, but because the judge\'s biases and \nloyalties could reasonably and objectively be questioned. This is \nbecause, to a disinterested observer, the judge would likely be \nviewed as attempting to curry favor with the prosecuting authorities, \nwhich necessarily would have included the jurisdiction over whose \ncase she continued to preside. The Pennsylvania Supreme Court \nstated: \nOne could reasonably conclude that, under the \ncircumstances, Cunningham\'s cooperation with the \nUnited States Attorney\'s office cast her in the role of a \nconfederate of the prosecutors in the appellees\' cases. \nMcFall, 617 A.2d at 713. No such alliance occurred here, where \nthe Judge Gallina was not arrested until more than a year after the \nPetitioner\'s trial and did not preside over any more cases \nthereafter. \nIn another case, a Pennsylvania Juvenile Court Judge \nreceived secret payments from the owners of a facility where \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n8 \n\n--- Page 13 ---\njuveniles were housed, and in those cases, the Court reversed \ndelinquency adjudications. See In re Bruno, 101 A.3d 635, \n671-72, 627 Pa. 505 (2014). However, in that case, the judge \nreceiving bribes had a dear pecuniary interest which clearly \nimpugned the appearance of his impartiality in the decisions \nrendered in those cases. There, it is easy to recognize that the \njudge had a financial interest in sending adjudicated delinquents to \nthe detention facility that was paying him to do so. Here, no such \nprofit motive or other incentive is alleged or apparent. While the \nState neither dismisses nor condones the conduct of Judge \nGallina, there are no indications or reasons to speculate that his \nimpartiality in this case was subject to reasonable question. \nA judge should not only be impartial, but should appear so. \nIn re Swenson, 158 Wn. App. 812,818,244 P.3d 959 (2010). \n"Evidence of a judge\'s actual or potential bias must be shown \nbefore an appearance of fairness claim will succeed." State v. \nChamberlin, 161 Wn.2d 30, 37, 162 P.3d 389 (2007). If true, the \nallegations against Judge Gallina seriously impugn his character. \nHowever, the allegations fail to demonstrate that his partiality would \nbe questioned; i.e. that one side had an advantage going into the \ncase. Standing alone, the fact that a judge is involved in criminal \nactivities would tend to lead an outside observer to believe that a \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n9 \n\n--- Page 14 ---\ndefendant, rather than the State would have the advantage. Here, \nin fact, Judge Gallina\'s refusal to grant the State\'s request to \nimpose an exceptional upward sentence demonstrates leniency \ntoward Petitioner, not bias. Additionally, the judge was not working \nwith authorities or seeking some benefit from the State in exchange \nfor leniency on pending charges, nor did he have any pecuniary or \npersonal interest in the outcome ofthe case. The record does not \nreflect any actual or apparent bias, nor has the Petitioner so \ndemonstrated. \nFurther, Petitioner\'s argument seems to presuppose that \nengaging in criminal conduct necessarily renders a person \nincapable of impartiality in all legal proceedings. If that were the \ncase, felons would be categorically prohibited from sitting in \njudgment of their peers. Washington, however, permits even those \nwho have been convicted of criminal conduct to serve on juries. \nSee RCW 2.36.070. Federal courts have also found that the \ninclusion of felon-jurors does not violate the right to an impartial \njury. \nThe Supreme Court has stressed repeatedly that the \ntouchstone of the guarantee of an impartial jury is a \nprotection against juror bias. A per se rule would be \nappropriate, therefore, only if one could reasonably \nconclude that felons are always biased against one \nparty or the other. But felon status, alone, does not \nnecessarily imply bias. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n10 \n\n--- Page 15 ---\nUnited States v. Boney, 977 F.2d 624, 633 (D.C. Cir. 1992). Even if \nthe allegations against Judge Gallina are true, felonious conduct \nalone does not necessarily imply bias. Because Petitioner has not \ndemonstrated any actual or apparent bias of Judge Gallina towards \nPetitioner, his argument fails. \n2. \nPETITIONER HAS FAILED TO DEMONSTRATE HE WAS \nDENIED THE RIGHT TO COUNSEL WHERE HE WAS \nREPRESENTED BY A LICENSED ATTORNEY IN GOOD \nSTANDING WITH THE IDAHO STATE BAR. \nA criminal defendant unquestionably has the right to \neffective assistance of trial counsel under the Sixth Amendment to \nthe United States Constitution and article I, section 22 of the \nWashington State Constitution. See, e.g., In re Pers. Restraint of \nDavis, 152 Wn.2d 647,672, 101 P.3d 1 (2004). To sustain a claim \nof ineffective assistance of counsel, the Petitioner must \ndemonstrate that (1) counsel\'s representation was deficient and (2) \nthat counsel\'s deficient representation caused prejudice. Id. \n(quoting State v. McFarland, 127 Wn.2d 322, 334-35, 899 P.2d \n1251 (1995)). \nWhere "counsel" has never been duly licensed to practice \nlaw in any jurisdiction, prejudice may be presumed, resulting in a \nper se finding of ineffective assistance of counsel. United States v. \nNovak, 903 F.2d 883, 890 (2d. Cir. 1990). However, this per se rule \nis limited to instances where counsel has failed to ever meet the \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n11 \n\n--- Page 16 ---\nsubstantive requirements to practice law. See Solina v. United \nStates, 709 F.2d 160 (2d Cir. 1983) (counsel repeatedly failed the \nbar exam and was not licensed in any jurisdiction); See also \nPeople v. Felder. 47 N.Y.2d 287, 391 N.E.2d 1274 (1979) (counsel \nhad never attended law school or otherwise satisfied the \nprerequisites for bar admission, and had never been licensed in \nany jurisdiction); See a/so Novak, 903 F.2d 883 {2d. Cir. 1990) \n( counsel had never taken the bar exam and received admission to \nthe bar through fraudulent means). The per se rule is premised on \nthe principle that: \none never admitted to practice law and therefore who \nnever acquired the threshold qualification to represent \na client in court cannot be allowed to do so ... \nUnited States v. Mouzin, 785 F.2d 682, 697 (9th Cir. 1986) \n(emphasis added). Here, Mr. Van ldour had satisfied the \nsubstantive requirements to practice law in another jurisdiction. He \nwas admitted to practice law in Idaho, and had therefore "acquired \nthe threshold qualification to represent a client." Because Mr. Van \nldour was licensed in another state the per se rule does not apply \nand Petitioner must demonstrate deficient performance and \nprejudice. \nWhen an attorney is licensed in a jurisdiction other than the \none in which the proceedings occurred. prejudice must be shown. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n12 \n\n--- Page 17 ---\nFor example, in Ferrara v. Keane, trial counsel was licensed to \npractice in New Jersey, but not in New York where the proceedings \ntook place. 806 F.Supp. 472, 473 (S.D.N.Y. 1992), aff\'d, 2 F.3d 403 \n(2d Cir. 1993). In deciding Petitioner\'s ineffective assistance \ncounsel claim, the court concluded that because counsel was \nadmitted in another jurisdiction for more than 30 years, it could not \nbe said that he failed to meet the "substantive requirements" for the \npractice of law. Id. at 474. Therefore, the per se rule did not apply. \nHere, Mr. Van !dour was similarly licensed to practice law in \nanother jurisdiction, and had done so for 30 years. Even if the \nrequirements of APR 8 were not satisfied, it cannot be said Mr. Van \nldour failed to ever meet the "substantive requirements" to practice \nlaw. \nCourts also consider whether counsel is eligible to practice \nin the court on a limited basis pursuant to court rule. Prejudice must \nbe demonstrated even absent counsel making a formal application \nfor admission to practice in the forum court, where counsel would \nhave been eligible for limited admission. United States v. Bradford, \n238 F.2d 395 (2d. Cir. 1956) (counsel who failed to comply with \ncourt rule for limited admission was not automatically ineffective); \nsee a/so Ferrara v. Keane, 806 F.Supp. at 474 (S.D.N.Y. 1992) \n( even assuming counsel\'s petition to proceed pro hac vice was \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n13 \n\n--- Page 18 ---\nflawed, defendant was required to show prejudice). Although there \nwere irregularities in Mr. Van ldour\'s application process, he was \notherwise eligible for admission to practice in Washington. \nTherefore, prejudice must otherwise be demonstrated. \nEven the disbarment of counsel during trial proceedings is \ninsufficient to trigger the application of the per se rule. United \nStates v. Mouzin, 785 F.2d 682, 698 (9th Cir. 1986). In Mouzin, \ndefense counsel was notified during trial that he had been \ndisbarred by the Ninth Circuit Court of Appeals. Id. at 694-95. \nPursuant to local rule, disbarment in any jurisdiction resulted in \ndisbarment from the trial court. Id. at 695. Counsel, with consent of \nthe trial judge, continued to represent the defendant through trial. \nId. On review, the Ninth Circuit concluded that even an automatic \ndisbarment from the trial court was insufficient to render counsel\'s \nrepresentation ineffective. Id. at 698. During the course of his \nrepresentation of Petitioner, Mr. Van ldour remained a licensed \nattorney in good standing in Idaho. If formal disbarment during trial \ndoes not constitute a per se deprivation of counsel, certainly \nrepresentation by a licensed attorney in good standing also \nrequires an additional showing of prejudice. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n14 \n\n--- Page 19 ---\nPetitioner has failed to demonstrate how Mr. Van ldour\'s \nrepresentation was otherwise deficient or any resulting prejudice. \nTherefore, his claim necessarily fails. \nV. CONCLUSION \nPetitioner has failed to establish that Judge Gallina was \nactually or potentially prejudiced against him. Further, Petitioner \nhas failed to demonstrate that Mr. Van ldour provided deficient \nrepresentation, nor any resulting prejudice. Therefore, the \nRespondent respectfully requests an order dismissing this petition \nbe entered. \n;. \nDated this z_g day of June, 2020. \nCURT L. LIEDKIE, WSBA #30371 \nAttorney for Respondent \nDeputy Prosecuting Attorney for Asotin County \nP.O. Box220 \nAsotin, Washington 99402 \n(509) 243:-2061 \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n15 \n\n--- Page 20 ---\nCOURT OF APPEALS OF THE STATE OF \nWASHINGTON - DIVISION Ill \nIN RE THE PERSONAL RESTRAINT OF \nROBERT L. AYERST, \nPetitioner \nCourt of Appeals No: 36965-0-111 \nDECLARATION OF SERVICE \nDECLARATION \nOn June 23, 2020 I electronically mailed, through the portal, a copy of the RESPONSE TO \nSUPPLEMENTAL BRIEF OF PETITIONER in this matter to: \nLISE ELLNER \nliseellnetlaw@comcast.net \nI declare under penalty of perjury under the laws of the State of Washington the foregoing statement \nIs true and correct. \nSigned at Asotin, Washington on June 23, 2020. \nDECLARATION \nOF SERVICE \nLSAM. WEBBER \nOffice Manager \nPage 1 of 1 \nBenjamin C. Nichols, Prosecuting Attorney \nP. 0 . Box 220, Asotin, WA 99402 \n(509) 243-2061 \n\n--- Page 21 ---\nASOTIN COUNTY PROSECUTOR\'S OFFICE\nJune 23, 2020 - 1:44 PM\nTransmittal Information\nFiled with Court:\nCourt of Appeals Division III\nAppellate Court Case Number:   36965-0\nAppellate Court Case Title:\nPersonal Restraint Petition of Robert Lloyd Ayerst\nSuperior Court Case Number:\n16-1-00150-7\nThe following documents have been uploaded:\n369650_Briefs_Plus_20200623134308D3574643_7955.pdf \n    This File Contains: \n     Affidavit/Declaration - Service \n     Briefs - Respondents - Modifier: Supplemental \n     The Original File Name was Response to Supp Brief.pdf\nA copy of the uploaded files will be sent to:\nLiseellnerlaw@comcast.net\nbabbitts@seattleu.edu\nbnichols@co.asotin.wa.us\nvalerie.liseellner@gmail.com\nComments:\nSender Name: Lisa Webber - Email: lwebber@co.asotin.wa.us \n    Filing on Behalf of: Curtis Lane Liedkie - Email: cliedkie@co.asotin.wa.us (Alternate Email: )\nAddress: \n135 2nd Street\nP.O. Box 220 \nAsotin, WA, 99402 \nPhone: (509) 243-2061\nNote: The Filing Id is 20200623134308D3574643\n', 'source': 'text_input'}
