oval, 189 Wn.2d 811 \n(2018) ...................... ; . . . . . . . . . . 8 \nPetition of Williams, 111 Wn.2d 353 (1988) .... 10 \nState v. A.N.J., 168 Wn.2d 91 (2010) ........ 19 \nState v. Buckman, 190 Wn.2d 51 (2018) ...... . 8 \nState v. Earls, 116 Wn.2d 364 (1991) . . . . . . . . 12 \nii \n\n--- Page 4 ---\nState v. Hager, 171 Wn.2d 151 (2011) . . . . . . . 12 \nState v. Mayer, 184 Wn.2d 548 (2015) ... ... . 12 \nState v. McFarland, 127 Wn.2d 322 (1995) .... 12 \nState v. Moore, 79 Wn.2d 51 (1971) . . . . .. . . . 12 \nFederal Court of Appeals Cases \nCole v. United States, 162 F.3d 957 (7th Cir. 1998) \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 14 \nDerringer v. United States, 441 F.2d 1140 (8th Cir. \n1971) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 15 \nHardamon v. United States, 319 F.3d 943 (7th Cir. \n2003) ............................. . ... 16 \nKieser v. New York, 56 F.3d (2nd Cir. 1995) ... 14 \nSolina v. United States, 709 F.2d 160 (2nd Cir. 1983) \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 13 \nUnited States v. Bradford, 238 F.2d 395 (2nd Cir. \n1956) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 15 \nUnited States v. Costanzo, 740 F.2d 251 (3rd Cir. \n1984) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 14 \nUnited States v. Gismore, 546 F.2d 884 {10th Cir. \n1976) .................... . ............ 13 \nUnited States v. Maria-Martinez, 143 F.3d 914 (5th \nCir. 1998) ............................ . . 14 \nUnited States v. Mitchell, 216 F.3d 1126 {D.C. Cir. \niii \n\n--- Page 5 ---\n2000) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 14 \nUnited States v. Mouzin, 785 F.2d 682 (9th Cir. 1986) \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 16 \nUnited States v. Novak, 903 F.2d 883 (2nd Cir. 1990) \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 12-13 \nUnited States v. Watson, 479 F.3d 607 (8th Cir. 2007) \n. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 16 \nState Court of Appeals Cases \nMatter of Ayerst, 17 Wn.App.2d 356 (2021) . . ... 7 \nMatter of Lewis, 37284-7-111, 2021 WL 1750935 (Div. \n111, May 4, 2021) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 7 \nState v. Ayerst, 35867-4-111, 2019 WL 1568060 (Div. \n111, Apr. 11, 2019) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 6 \nState v. Edison, 61 Wn.App. 530 (1991) . . .. . . 17 \nState v. Flores, 197 Wn.App. 1 (Div. Ill, 2016) .. 19 \nState v. Lewis, 35775-9-111, 2019 W.L. 1568058 (Div. \n111, Apr. 11, 2019) .................. .. . . .. . 6 \nState v. S.M., 100 Wn.App. 401 (2000) . . . .. . . 13 \nOther Cases \nFerrara v. Keane, 806 F. Supp. 472 (1992) .. .. 15 \nPeople v. Felder, 47 N.Y.2d 287 (1979) . .. . . . . . 13 \niv \n\n--- Page 6 ---\nCourt Rules \nAPR 8 .... .... .. . . . .. .. . . .. .. .... 4, 5, 17, 18 \nRAP 16. 7 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 10 \nOther Authorities \nU.S. Const. amend. VI . . .. . . . . . . . . . . . . . passim \nConst. art. 1 §.13 .... . . . . . . . . . . ... .. . ... . . 8 \nConst. art. 1 § 22 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 8, 11 \n"Over 1000 New Lawyers Get Licenses Without \nTaking Bar Exam," Bloomburg Law, Jan. 4, 2021, \nhttps ://news. bloomberglaw. com/busi ness-and-practi \nce/over-1-000-new-lawyers-get-licenses-without-tak \ning-bar-exam . . . . . . .. . . . . . . . . . . . . . • . . . . . . 17 \nV \n\n--- Page 7 ---\nI. \nISSUE PRESENTED \n1. \nWHETHER A PETITIONER WHO WAS \nREPRESENTED BY A PERSON ADMITTED \nTO PRACTICE LAW IN A NEIGHBORING \nSTATE MUST DEMONSTRATED ACTUAL \nPREJUDICE IN A COLLATERAL ATTACK \nBASED \nUPON \nTHE \nOUT-OF-STATE \nATTORNEY\'S \nLICENSING \nSTATUS \nIN \nWASHINGTON. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n1 \n\n--- Page 8 ---\nII. \nINTRODUCTION \nThe Petitioners in this matter raise the issue of \nwhether their right to counsel was violated when they were \nrepresented by Robert Van ldour, an attorney who was \nlicensed in a neighboring state but not Washington. \nBecause this issue is being raised for the first time in a \npost-conviction collateral attack, the Petitioners are required \nto demonstrate actual prejudice rather than a per se \nviolation. Because they fail to do so, their Personal \nRestraint Petitions should be dismissed. \nAdditionally, based on federal precedent, Mr. Van \nldour qualified as counsel for Sixth Amendment purposes \nat the time he represented Petitioners. Washington Courts \nhave held that the Washington State Constitution provides \nthe same protections as the United States Constitution on \nissues related to the right to counsel. Therefore, this Court \nshould find that he also qualified as counsel under \nWashington law. \nFinally, Washington Courts have held that violation of \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n2 \n\n--- Page 9 ---\nthe Rules of Professional Conduct does not establish a per \nse violation of the right to counsel, and that the appropriate \nsanction is investigation and discipline by the Washington \nState Bar Counsel. This Court should stand by that well-\nreasoned precedent here. \nIll. \nSTATEMENT OF THE CASE \nRobert L. Ayerst (Petitioner Ayerst) was convicted \nafter jury trial of Attempted Burglary in the Second Degree, \nMalicious Mischief in the Second Degree, and Bail Jumping \n(C Felony), from events occurring September 6, 2016. See \n35867-4-111, 2019 WL 1568060 (Div. Ill, Apr. 11 , 2019) \n(Unpublished).1 \nThe underlying facts of the case are \nlargely immaterial and need no discussion herein. \nJustin C. Lewis (Petitioner Lewis) was convicted after \njury trial of Assault in the First Degree, Robbery in the First \nDegree, Possession of a Controlled Substance (Heroin), \n1The State does not cite the unpublished decision \nfor precedent, but rather as establishing the procedural \nand factual background of the case. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n3 \n\n--- Page 10 ---\nand Possession of Drug Paraphernalia from events \noccurring April 4, 201 7. See State v. Justin C. Lewis, 8 \nWn.App. 1028 (Div. 111, Apr. 11, 2019) {Unpublished).2 The \nunderlying facts of the case are likewise immaterial and \nwarrant no discussion. \nPetitioner Ayerst was initially represented by John \nFay, who was later replaced by attorney Robert Van ldour \nwho represented him through trial. See Bond Order \n(10/04/16) and Motion and Order Allowing Withdrawal and \nSubstitution, submitted with the State\'s Response to \nPersonal Restraint Petition. \nPetitioner Lewis was \nrepresented by attorney Mr. Van ldour throughout the \nproceeding from its origin. \nBond Order (04/05/17) (see \nattached State\'s Response to Personal Restraint Petition \nand attached documents, attached hereto). \nMr. Van ldour had contracted with Asotin County and \nhad arranged to practice under APR 8(c), which authorizes \n2The State does not cite the unpublished decision \nfor precedent, but rather as establishing the procedural \nand factual background of the case. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n4 \n\n--- Page 11 ---\nattorneys who are licensed by other states to appear for the \npurposes of public defense while they apply for a formal \nlicense to practice in Washington. Declaration of Robert J. \nVan ldour. Mr. Van ldour also applied for formal admission \nto the Washington State Bar by motion under APR 3(c). \nSee Personal Restraint Petition Appendix A. He was later \nnotified that his application for admission by motion under \nAPR 3(c) was accepted, and that his application for limited \nadmission under APR 8(c) had been denied. Appendix A. \nAt the time of his appointment, Mr. Van ldour had been an \nattorney, licensed in the State of Idaho for more than 30 \nyear and was, at that time, 3 a member in good standing with \nthe Idaho State Bar Association. Declaration of Robert J. \nVan ldour. \nPetitioner Ayerst appealed his conviction, and it was \naffirmed by the Court of Appeals in an unpublished opinion. \n3 The Idaho Bar Association Website indicates Mr. \nVan ldour\'s status as "inactive" as of December 15, 2021. \nAccording to the WSBA webside, Mr. Van ldour was \nsuspended by order of the Washington Supreme Court \nfor eighteen months effective September 14, 2021. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n5 \n\n--- Page 12 ---\nSee \nState v. Ayerst,35867-4-11I, 2019 WL 1568060. \nPetitioner Ayerst then filed this personal restraint petition, \nalleging that he had been deprived of his right to counsel, \nrelying solely on the fact that Mr. Van ldour was not \nlicensed to practice in Washington. He has neither identified \nany deviation from the standard of practice for criminal \ndefense lawyers in Washington, nor any prejudice \ntherefrom. \nPetition·er Lewis appealed his conviction after trial, \nand it was affirmed by the Court of Appeals in an \nunpublished opinion. See State v. Lewis, supra. Petitioner \nLewis then filed this personal restraint petition, alleging only \nthat he had been deprived of his right to counsel, relying \nsolely on the fact that Mr. Van ldour was not licensed to \npractice in Washington. Petitioner Lewis neither argued nor \ndemonstrated that Mr. Van ldour committed any act of \nmisfeasance or nonfeasance, nor did he otherwise claim \nany prejudice therefrom. In an unpublished decision, the \nDivision Three of the Court of Appeals dismissed the \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n6 \n\n--- Page 13 ---\nDefendant\'s PRP, finding that 1) Petitioner Lewis failed to \nallege he was prejudiced by his attorney\'s representation \nand 2) the facts set forth in his petition failed to establish \nthat he should be excused from showing prejudice based \non a theory of structural error. Matter of Lewis, 37284-7-111, \n2021 WL 1750935 (Div. 111, May 4, 2021) (unpublished). 4 \nIn a published decision, Division Three of the Court of \nAppeals dismissed Petitioner Ayerst\'s petition, finding that \n1) the facts set forth in his petition failed to establish that he \nshould be excused from showing prejudice based on a \ntheory of structural error and Petitioner Ayerst failed to \nallege that he was \nprejudiced \nby his attorney\'s \nrepresentation. see Matter of Ayerst, 17 Wn.App.2d 356 \n(2021 ). \nPetitioners Ayerst and Lewis both separately sought \nreview by this Court of the Court of Appeals\' decision which \nwas granted, solely on the issue regarding the right to \n4 The State does not cite the unpublished decision \nfor precedent, but rather as establishing the procedural \nand factual background of the case. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n7 \n\n--- Page 14 ---\ncounsel, and the matters consolidated for consideration. \nIV. \nARGUMENT \nA collateral attack is not a substitute for a direct \nappeal. The Washington State Constitution draws a \ndistinction between direct appeals fo criminal convictions \nand habeas corpus actions. Const. art. 1. §§ 13, 22. \nSpecifically, collateral, attacks are generally held to a \nheightened standard to protect the principle of finality. In re \nPers. Restraint of Coggin, 182 Wn.2d 115, 120 (2014). \nAdditionally, in a collateral post-conviction proceeding, the \nPetitioner bears the burden rather than the State. In re \nPers. Restraint of Sandoval, 189 Wn.2d 811 , 821 (2018). \n"[A] personal restraint petitioner must first establish by \npreponderance of the evidence that a constitutional error \nhas resulted in actual and substantial prejudice." In re Pers. \nRestaint of Cross, 180 Wn.2d 664, 671 (2014) (internal \ncitations omitted). \nA constitutional error alone does not generally meet \nthe requisite prejudice. State v. Buckman, 190 Wn.2d 51, \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n8 \n\n--- Page 15 ---\n56 n.1 (2018). Thus, an error creating the possibility of \nprejudice is insufficient-the petition must show that "the \noutcome would more likely than not have been different had \nthe alleged error not occurred." In re Pers. Restraint of \n· Meippen, 193 Wn.2d 310 (2019) (internal citations omitted). \n"Although some errors which result prejudice on direct \nreview will also be per se prejudicial on collateral attack, the \ninterests of finality of litigation demand that a higher \nstandard be satisfied in a collateral proceeding." In the \nMatter of the Personal Restraint of St. Pierre, 118 Wn.2d \n321, 329 (1992). Since the decision in St. Pierre, this Court \nhas applied this higher standard for collateral attacks to \ncourt closures, an otherwise structural error. See In re \nCoggin, 182 Wn.2d 115, 121-22 (2014).5 \nIf a petitioner fails to make the required "prima facie \n5At least one amicus brief cites to In re Crace for \nthe proposition that the higher standard for collateral \nattacks does not apply ineffective assistance of counsel \nclaims. 17 4 Wn.2d 835. However, the holding of In re \nCrace suggests only that a petitioner in a collateral attack \nneed not show additional prejudice beyond the \nrequirements of Strickland v. Washington. Id. at 848. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n9 \n\n--- Page 16 ---\nshowing of actual and substantial" prejudice, the result is \ndismissal of the collateral attack. In re Meippen, 193 Wn.2d \nat 316. Furthermore, in a collateral attack, the burden is on \nthe petitioner to identify and, when available, produce the \nnecessary record to support the claim. A petitioner who \nraises a claim is required to have some factual basis to \nsupport it and point the court to where those facts are being \ndrawn from. In re Pers. Restraint of Cook, 114 Wn.2d 802, \n813-14 (1990); see also RAP 16.7 (a)(2)-(3). Unsupported \nallegations lacking citations to authority or references to the \nrecord or to competent admissible evidence are not \nsufficient to sustain the petitioner\'s burden. In re Cook, 114 \nWn.2d at 813-14. Failure to meet this burde results in \ndismissal of the petition. Petition of Williams, 111 Wn.2d \n353, 364-65 (1998). \nHere, the Petitioners do not argue that they were \nprejudiced by Mr. Van ldour\'s representation. They do not \nidentify any deficiencies in Mr. Van ldour\'s representation \nof them Rather, the Petitioners assert that representation by \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n10 \n\n--- Page 17 ---\nMr. Van !dour constituted a per se violation of their \nconstitutional right to counsel. Petition for Review from \nDivision Three of the Court of Appeals #369650-111 at 7; \nPetition for Review from Division Three of the Court of \nAppeals #37284-7-111 at 2. In doing so, they rely heavily on \nthe holding of City of Seattle vs. Ratliff. 100 Wn.2d 212 \n(1983). However, Ratliff was decided on direct appeal. \nAssuming, arguendo, that the holding of Ratliff applies to \nattorneys licensed outside of Washington State1 that case \nwas decided under a different standard. The Petitioners \nhave raised this issue for the first time as part of a post-\nconviction collateral attack; therefore, a demonstration of \nprejudice is.required. Withoutanyevidenceofprejudice, the \nPersonal Restraint Petitions should be dismissed. \nA criminal defendant unquestionably has the right to \neffective assistance of trial counsel under the Sixth \nAmendment to the United States Constitution and Article I, \nSection 22 of the Washington State Constitution. See, e.g., \nIn re Pers. Restraint of Davis, 152 Wn.2d 6471 672 (2004). \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n11 \n\n--- Page 18 ---\nTo sustain a claim of ineffective assistance of counsel, the \npetitioner \nmust \ndemonstrate \nthat \n(1) \ncounsel\'s \nrepresentation was deficient and (2) that counsel\'s deficient \nrepresentation caused prejudice. Id. ( quoting State v. \nMcFarland, 127 Wn.2d 322, 334-35 (1995)). Here, \nPetitioners claim that their respective Sixth Amendment and \nArticle I, Section 22 rights were violated. This Court has \nroutinely held that Article I, Section 22 is no more protective \nthan the Sixth Amendment and neither Petitioner has \nargued or offered analysis suggesting otherwise. State v. \nMoore, 79 Wn.2d 51 (1971 ); State v. Earls, 116 Wn.2d 364, \n375 (1991); State v. Hager, 171 Wn.2d 151, (2011); State \nv. Mayer, 184 Wn.2d 548, 569 ("The right to counsel under \nour state constitution and the federal constitution is the \nsame.") \nOn direct appeal, courts have found complete \ndeprivation of counsel to have occurred where the \npurported "counsel" has never been duly licensed to \npractice law in any jurisdiction. United States v. Novak, 903 \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n12 \n\n--- Page 19 ---\nF.2d 883, 890 (2d. Cir. 1990). This finding is limited to \ninstances where counsel has failed to ever meet the \nsubstantive requirements to practice law. See Solina v. \nUnited States, 709 F.2d 160 (2d Cir. 1983) (counsel \nrepeatedly failed the bar exam and was not licensed in any \njurisdiction); See also People v. Felder. 47 N.Y.2d 287, 391 \nN.E.2d 1274 (1979) (counsel had never attended law \nschool or otherwise satisfied the prerequisites for bar \nadmission, and had never been licensed in any jurisdiction); \nSee also Novak, 903 F.2d 883 (2d. Cir. 1990) (counsel had \nnever taken the bar exam and received admission to the bar \nthrough fraudulent means). \nConversely, courts have held that the Sixth \nAmendment right to "counsel" is satisfied when a defendant \nis represented by someone who is authorized to practice \nlaw rather than a lay person. See State v. S.M., 100 Wn. \nApp. 401 , 410 (2000) (quoting United States v. Gismore, \n546 F.2d 844, 847 (10th Cir. 1976)). In other words, a \ndefendant who is represented by a person who has been \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n13 \n\n--- Page 20 ---\nlicensed to practice law in any jurisdiction has not suffered \nfrom a per se Sixth Amendment violation. See, e.g.,United \nStates v. Mitchell, 216 F.3d 1126, 1132 (D.C. Cir. 2000) \n(\'We decline appellant\'s invitation to extend the per se \nineffectiveness rule beyond those cases in which a \ndefendant is represented by a person never properly \nadmitted to any bar.\'); United States v. Maria-Martinez, 143 \nF.3d 914, 917 (5th Cir. 1998) (\'\'the key to adequate \nrepresentation is not technical license to practice in the \njurisdiction involved, but a credential from some forum \ndemonstrating the specialized knowledge of a lawyer.\'). \nSpecifically, various federal and state courts have \naddressed cases where the defendant was represented by \nan out-of-state attorney who had not been admitted pro hoc \nvice. See, e.g., Cole v. United States, 162 F.3d 957, 959 \n(7th Cir. 1998); Kieserv. New York, 56 F.3d 16, 17 (2d Cir. \n1995) (lawyer suspended in New Jersey and not admitted \npro hac vice in New York when case was tried); United \nStates v. Costanzo, 740 F.2d 251, 258-59 (3d Cir. 1984); \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n14 \n\n--- Page 21 ---\nDerringer v. United States, 441 F.2d 1140, 1141 (8th Cir. \n1971 ). \nFor instance, in Ferrara v. Keane, trial counsel was \nlicensed to practice in New Jersey, but not in New York \nwhere the proceedings took place. 806 F.Supp. 472, 473 \n(S.D.N.Y. 1992), aff\'d, 2 F.3d 403 (2d Cir. 1993). In \ndeciding Petitioner\'s ineffective assistance counsel claim, \nthe court concluded that because counsel was admitted in \nanother jurisdiction for more than 30 years, it could not be \nsaid that he failed to meet the "substantive requirements" \nfor the practice of law. Id. at 474. Where counsel would \nhave been eligible for limited admission to practice in the \nforum court, but failed to make a formal application, the \nrepresentation provided is not automatically ineffective. \nUnited States v. Bradford, 238 F.2d 395 (2d. Cir. 1956) \n( counsel who failed to comply with court rule for limited \nadmission was not automatically ineffective); see also \nFerrara v. Keane, 806 F.Supp. at 474 (S.D.N.Y. 1992) \n(even assuming counsel\'s petition to proceed pro hac vice \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n15 \n\n--- Page 22 ---\nwas flawed, defendant was required to show prejudice). \nEven the disbarment of \ncounsel during trial \nproceedings is insufficient to find complete deprivation of \ncousel. United States v. Mouzin, 785 F.2d 682,698 (9th Cir. \n1986). In Mouzin, defense counsel was notified during trial \nthat he had been disbarred by the Ninth Circuit Court of \nAppeals. Id. at 694-95. Pursuant to local rule, disbarment in \nany jurisdiction resulted in disbarment from the trial court. \nId. at 695. Counsel, with consent of the trial judge, \ncontinued to represent the defendant through trial. Id. On \nreview, the Ninth Circuit concluded that even an automatic \ndisbarment from the trial court was insufficient to render \ncounsel\'s representation ineffective. Id. at 698. \nSimilarly, representation by a lawyer whose license \nhas been suspended does not constitute a complete \ndeprivation of counsel. See, e.g., United States v. Watson, \n479 F.3d 607, 611 (8th Cir.) (suspension of a state license \nnot per se ineffective assistance of counsel); Hardaman v. \nUnited States, 319 F.3d 943, 951 (7th Cir. 2003) \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n16 \n\n--- Page 23 ---\n(suspension of an attorney does not amount to per se \nineffective assistance); State v. Edison, 61 Wn. App. \n( 1991) ( defendant must demonstrate actual prejudice when \nrepresented by a lawyer who has been licensed but whose \nlicense was suspended). \nHere, Mr. Van ldour was licensed to practice law in \nanother jurisdiction, and had been for 30 years. Even if the \nrequirements of APR 8 were not satisfied, it cannot be said \nMr. Van ldour failed to ever meet the "substantive \nrequirements" to practice law.6 Furthermore, during the \ncourse of his representation of Petitioner, Mr. Van ldour \n· \n6 Interestingly and by contrast, an entire class of bar \napplicants was recently granted admission due to \nCOVID-19, having never demonstrated fitness to practice \nlaw through the usual screening process of the bar \nexamination. Unlike these applicants, Mr. Van ldour had \nsuccessfully passed a bar exam and his three decades \npractice without disciplinary action attest to his fitness, \neven where he failed to fill out all necessary paperwork \nand follow through with his reciprocal application process \nwith the WSBA, for which he was undoubtably otherwise \neligible."Over 1000 New Lawyers Get Licenses Without \nTaking Bar Exam," Bloomburg Law, Jan. 4, 2021, \nhttps://news.bloomberglaw.com/business-and-practice/ov \ner-1-000-new-lawyers-get-licenses-without-taking-bar-exa \nm \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n17 \n\n--- Page 24 ---\nremained a licensed attorney in good standing in Idaho. If \nformal disbarment during trial does not constitute a \ndeprivation of counsel, certainly representation by a \nlicensed attorney in good standing cannot meet the \nrequisite standard. \nFurthermore, Mr. Van ldourwas eligible for reciprocal \nadmission. Moreover, he could have practiced pro hac vice \nin any court in Washington, with permission of that court, \nand he was certainly eligible to provide public defense \nunder APR 8. His only deficiency was his failure to be \nmindful of and properly follow the strictures of that rule. \nWhere suspension or even outright disbarment does not \nviolate the 6th Amendment right to counsel, this deficiency, \nwhile not an insignificant barrier to the lawful and regulated \npractice of law, cannot be said to have amounted to an \nabsolute deprivation of counsel. \nFinally, Petitioners and amici emphasize the fact that \nMr. Van ldour\'s failure to obtain a Washington license \nviolated the Rules of Professional Conduct (RPC), including \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n18 \n\n--- Page 25 ---\nthe Standards for Indigent Defense. However, this Court \nhas determined that, while they may be used to evaluate a \nclaim of ineffective assistance of counsel, the "professional \nstandards do not establish minimum Sixth Amendment \nrequirements." State v. A.N.J, 168 Wn.2d 91, 965-66 \n(2010). Furthermore, Washington Courts have declined to \nfind that defense counsel provided ineffective based solely \non counsel\'s violation of the Standards for Indigent Defense \n(SID). State v. Flores, 197 Wn.App. 1, 15 (Div. Ill, 2016), \nreview denied in 188 Wn.2d 1003. In addressing the lack of \nremedy for violations of the SID, the Court found that "[t]he \nomission suggests that the remedy for violations of the \nstandards rests with the disciplinary process." Id. at 15. The \nCourt then stated that "[w]hether there was a violation of the \nRules of Professional Conduct is a question that only an \nappropriate investigation can answer\'\' and declined to issue \nan opinion on the matter. Id. at 15-16. \nSimilarly to the Appellant in Flores, the Petitioners \nemphasize Mr. Van ldour\'s violation of the RPC and SID in \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n19 \n\n--- Page 26 ---\narguing that his representation was deficient under the Sixth \nAmendment. Amicus The Defender Initiative also suggests \nthat "[rJeversing the convictions in this case will make it \nclear that the court intends to enforce the Standards it \nadopted." However, this Court has already determined that \nan appropriate remedy exists-an investigation by the bar \ncounsel and, if a violation is found to have been committed, \ndisciplinary action by the same. This Court should stand by \nits well-reasoned precedent. \nV. \nCONCLUSION \nWithout any showing of prejudice resulting from Mr. \nVan ldour\'s representation, the Petitioners fail to meet the \nheightened requirements for a post conviction collateral \nattack. Furthermore, Mr. Van ldour\'s representation \nqualified as "counsel" for the purposes of the Sixth \nAmendment and, by extension, the Washington State \nConstitution. Therefore, the Respondent respectfully \nrequest that this Court affirm the Court of Appeals\' decision \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n20 \n\n--- Page 27 ---\nand dismiss the Petitioners Personal Restraint Petitions. \nI hereby certify that this supplemental brief contains 3,355 \nwords and is in compliance with RAP 18.17, \nDated this !J.!.. day of March, 2022. \nRespectfully submitted, \nJAJM·I;_¼. \nOUNG, WSB \nA\'tk1fney for Responde \nDeputy Prosecuting Attorney \nfor Asotin County \nP.O. Box 220 \nAsotin, Washington 99402 \n(509) 243-2061 \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n21 \n\n--- Page 28 ---\nASOTIN COUNTY PROSECUTOR\'S OFFICE\nMarch 04, 2022 - 4:50 PM\nTransmittal Information\nFiled with Court:\nSupreme Court\nAppellate Court Case Number:   99939-2\nAppellate Court Case Title:\nPersonal Restraint Petition of Justin C. Lewis\nSuperior Court Case Number:\n17-1-00053-3\nThe following documents have been uploaded:\n999392_Briefs_20220304164551SC959389_0346.pdf \n    This File Contains: \n     Briefs - Respondents Supplemental \n     The Original File Name was SKMBT_36322030417560.pdf\nA copy of the uploaded files will be sent to:\nLiseellnerlaw@comcast.net\nbnichols@co.asotin.wa.us\nboruchor@seattleu.edu\ncliedkie@co.asotin.wa.us\njaimehawk@hotmail.com\njeffrey.even@atg.wa.gov\njhawk@aclu-wa.org\njmidgley@aclu-wa.org\npleadings@aclu-wa.org\nrcboru@aol.com\nsgoolyef@atg.wa.gov\ntalner@aclu-wa.org\nComments:\nSender Name: Sharlene Tiller - Email: stiller@co.asotin.wa.us \n    Filing on Behalf of: Jaime Kiona Young - Email: JYoung@co.asotin.wa.us (Alternate Email: )\nAddress: \n135 2nd Street\nP.O. Box 220 \nAsotin, WA, 99402 \nPhone: (509) 243-2061\nNote: The Filing Id is 20220304164551SC959389\n\n--- Page 29 ---\nFILED \nSUPREME COURT \nSTATE OF WASHINGTON \n3f7 /2022 8 :28 AM \nBY ERIN L. LENNON \nCLERK \nIN THE SUPREME COURT OF THE STATE OF \nWASHINGTON \nIn Re Personal Restraint Petition of: \nJUSTIN C. LEWIS, ROBERT AYERST, \nPetitioner. \nSupreme Court No: 99939-2 consl. 99939-2 \nCourt of Appeal: 37284-7-111 \nDECLARATION OF SERVICE \nDECLARATION \nOn March 4, 20221 electronically mailed, through the portal, a copy of the SUPPLEMENTAL BRIEF \nOF RESPONDENT-STATE OF WASHINGTON in this matter to: \nLise Ellner \nliseelinerlaw@comcast.net \nI declare under penalty of perjury under the laws of the State of Washington the foregoing statement \nis true and correct. \nSigned at Asotin, Washington on March 7, 2022. \nDECLARATION \nOF SERVICE \nPage 1 of 1 \nBenjamin C. Nichols, Prosecuting Attorney \nP. 0 . Box 220, Asotin, WA 99402 \n{509) 243-2061 \n\n--- Page 30 ---\nASOTIN COUNTY PROSECUTOR\'S OFFICE\nMarch 07, 2022 - 8:28 AM\nTransmittal Information\nFiled with Court:\nSupreme Court\nAppellate Court Case Number:   99939-2\nAppellate Court Case Title:\nPersonal Restraint Petition of Justin C. Lewis\nSuperior Court Case Number:\n17-1-00053-3\nThe following documents have been uploaded:\n999392_Cert_of_Service_20220307082701SC293568_3440.pdf \n    This File Contains: \n     Certificate of Service \n     The Original File Name was Declaration.pdf\nA copy of the uploaded files will be sent to:\nLiseellnerlaw@comcast.net\nbnichols@co.asotin.wa.us\nboruchor@seattleu.edu\ncliedkie@co.asotin.wa.us\njaimehawk@hotmail.com\njeffrey.even@atg.wa.gov\njhawk@aclu-wa.org\njmidgley@aclu-wa.org\npleadings@aclu-wa.org\nrcboru@aol.com\nsgoolyef@atg.wa.gov\ntalner@aclu-wa.org\nComments:\nMs. Tiller assisted on Friday, and forgot to add the Declaration of Service, so it is now attached. Thank you.\nSender Name: Lisa Webber - Email: lwebber@co.asotin.wa.us \n    Filing on Behalf of: Jaime Kiona Young - Email: JYoung@co.asotin.wa.us (Alternate Email: )\nAddress: \n135 2nd Street\nP.O. Box 220 \nAsotin, WA, 99402 \nPhone: (509) 243-2061\nNote: The Filing Id is 20220307082701SC293568\n', 'source': 'text_input'}
