2025-07-29 18:15:00 - src.vue_api_endpoints - INFO - JSON data received: {'text': "\n--- Page 1 ---\nSupreme Court No. 999414; conl. 999392 \n \nTHE SUPREME COURT OF THE STATE OF \nWASHINGTON \n \nIN RE THE PERSONAL RESTRAINT PETITION OF  \n \nROBERT AYERST  \n& \n JUSTIN LEWIS \n \nPetitioners, \n \n \nPETITION FOR REVIEW FROM DIVISION THREE OF \nTHE COURT OF APPEALS #36965-0-III; 35775-9-III \nAsotin Cause No. 16-1-00-150-7; 17-1-00053-02 \n \n \nSUPPLEMENTAL BRIEF OF PETITIONER \n \nLISE ELLNER \nAttorney for Appellant \n \nLAW OFFICES OF LISE ELLNER \nPost Office Box 2711 \nVashon, WA 98070 \n(206) 930-1090 \nWSB #20955 \nFILED \nSUPREME COURT \nSTATE OF WASHINGTON \n211612022 9:18 AM \nBY ERIN L. LENNON \nCLERK \n\n--- Page 2 ---\n \ni \n \nTABLE OF CONTENTS \n \nTABLE OF CONTENTS .................................................... i \nTABLE OF AUTHORITIES .............................................. ii \nTABLE OF AUTHORITIES CONT'D ............................... iii \nTABLE OF AUTHORITIES CONT'D ............................... iv \nA. \nSUPPLEMENTAL ISSUES ...................................... 1 \nB. \nRESTATEMENT OF THE CASE ............................. 1 \nC. \nARGUMENT ............................................................ 3 \n1. PETITIONER WAS DENIED HIS \nCONSTITUTIONAL RIGHT TO COUNSEL. ................ 3 \na. Second Circuit Case, Solina, is not controlling or \nharmful. ................................................................................... 12 \nD. \nCONCLUSION ...................................................... 18 \n \n \n \n\n--- Page 3 ---\n \nii \n \nTABLE OF AUTHORITIES \nWashington Cases \nCity of Seattle v. Ratliff, \n 100 Wn.2d 212, 667 P.2d 630 (1983) ................. passim \n \nFeis v. King County Sheriff’s Dept., \n 165 Wn. App. 525, 267 P.3d 1022 (2011) ................... 13 \n \nHome Ins. Co. of New York v. N. Pac. Ry. Co., \n 18 Wn.2d 798, 140 P.2d 507 (1943) ........................... 12 \n \nIn re Ayerst, \n 17 Wn. App. 2d 356, 486 P.3d 943 (2021) .......... passim \n \nIn re Dependency of A.N.J., \n 168 Wn.2d 91, 225 P.3d 956 (2010) ............. 8, 9, 10, 11 \n \nIn re Lewis, \n 17 Wn. App .2d 1040, 2021 WL 1750935 (2021) passim \n \nIn re Rights to Waters of Stranger Creek, \n 77 Wn.2d 649, 466 P.2d 508 (1970) ............................. 6 \n \nPendergrast v. Matichuk, \n 186 Wn.2d 556, 379 P.3d 96 (2016) ............................. 6 \n \nSolina v. United States, \n 709 F.2d 160, (2nd Cir. 1983) ............................. passim \n \nSpokane County v. State, \n 196 Wn.2d 79, 469 P.3d 1173 (2020) ........................... 4 \n\n--- Page 4 ---\n \niii \n \nTABLE OF AUTHORITIES CONT'D \n \nState v. Flores, \n 197 Wn.App. 2d 1, 386 P.3d 298 (2016) ................. 9, 10 \n \nState v. Martinez, \n 196 Wn.2d 605, 476 P.3d 189 (2020) ........................... 6 \n \nW.G. Clark Constr. Co. v. Pac. Nw. Reg’l Council of \nCarpenters, \n 180 Wn.2d 54, 322 P.3d 1207 (2014) ........................... 6 \nFederal Cases \nChapman v. California, \n 386 U.S. 18,  87 S. Ct. 824, 17 L. Ed. 2d 705 (1967) .. 13 \n \nHolloway v. Arkansas, \n  435 U.S. 475, 489, 98 S. Ct. 1173, 55 L. Ed. 2d 426 \n(1978) ......................................................................... 13 \n \nSolina v. United States, \n 709 F.2d 160, (2nd Cir. 1983) ............................. passim \n \nTurner v. American Bar Ass'n, \n 407 F. Supp. 451 (N.D.Tex.1975) ................................. 3 \n \n \n \n \n \n\n--- Page 5 ---\n \niv \n \nTABLE OF AUTHORITIES CONT'D \nStatutes \nRCW 2.48.150 ............................................................... 14 \nRCW 2.48.170 ............................................................... 14 \nRCW 2.48.180 ............................................................... 14 \nRCW 2.48.190 ............................................................... 14 \nOther Authorities \nStandards for Indigent Defense ............................. passim \nRules \nCrR 3.1 ............................................................ 7, 8, 10, 11 \nConstitutional Provisions \nConst. art. 1, § 22 ............................................................ 3 \nConst. art. 4, § 1 .............................................................. 4 \nU.S. Const. Amend. VI ....................................... 1, 3, 5, 15 \n\n--- Page 6 ---\n \n1 \nA. SUPPLEMENTAL ISSUES \n1. The Sixth Amendment and Article 1, Section 22, \nguarantee the right to counsel in criminal proceedings. \nWhen a person is not licensed or otherwise permitted to \npractice in the court in which he appears, the defendant is \ndenied his constitutional right to counsel. \n2. The Attorney Practice Rules (APR’s), Standards of \nIndigent Defense (SID’s), and Criminal Rules (CrR’s) \nguarantee minimum criteria for the lawful practice of law \ninvolving indigent defendants. \nB. RESTATEMENT OF THE CASE \nThe Court of Appeals Division Three issued an \nopinion in Mr. Ayerst’s and Mr. Lewis’ cases based on \nincomplete and mischaracterized facts. Division Three \ndenied review in large part based on its misunderstanding \nthat the facts set forth in the bar complaint had not been \nproven. Mr. Van Idour stipulated to the facts in the WSBA \n\n--- Page 7 ---\n \n2 \ncomplaint on April 2, 2021, before the Court of Appeals \nissued its opinion in this case.   \nMr. Van Idour stipulated to the fact that he was never \nlicensed or otherwise permitted to practice in Washington, \nand never sought such permission from the WSBA. The \nonly unresolved issue was the punishment this Court would \nimpose. (See amended opinion recognizing formal \ncomplaint filed April 19, 2021, original opinion filed May 4, \n2021 and Amended Opinion issued June 1, 2021). \nThroughout Mr. Ayerst’s and Mr. Lewis’ cases, Mr. \nIdour never signed a single pleading with a bar number and \nMr. Neil Cox never filed a notice of appearance or any other \npleading in Mr. Ayerst’s or Mr. Lewis’ cases. The Court of \nAppeals did not consider these facts. \n \n \n \n \n\n--- Page 8 ---\n \n3 \nC. ARGUMENT \n1. PETITIONER WAS DENIED HIS \nCONSTITUTIONAL RIGHT TO COUNSEL. \nIn 1983, in Ratliff, this Court affirmed what it means \nto be counsel in Washington under the state and federal \nconstitutions. City of Seattle v. Ratliff, 100 Wn.2d 212, 217, \n667 P.2d 630 (1983). Division Three considered, \nacknowledged, and rejected this Court’s decision in Ratliff. \nIn re Ayerst, 17 Wn. App. 2d 356, 360, 486 P.3d 943 \n(2021); In re Lewis, 17 Wn. App .2d 1040, 2021 WL \n1750935 (2021) (Unpublished GR 14.1 not cited for \nprecedential value).  \n“[C]ounsel” as used in the Sixth Amendment \nand Const. art. 1, § 22 includes only those persons \nauthorized by the courts to practice law. Ratliff, 100 Wn.2d \nat 217. See, e.g., Turner v. American Bar Ass'n, 407 F. \nSupp. 451, 472–74 (N.D.Tex.1975). “[C]ounsel”, as used \nin the Sixth Amendment, encompasses only those persons \na court has adjudged “fit to practice by virtue of [their] \n\n--- Page 9 ---\n \n4 \ncharacter and/or training.” Id.1 This Court reviews issues of \nconstitutional law de novo. Spokane County v. State, 196 \nWn.2d 79, 84, 469 P.3d 1173 (2020). \nThis Court alone determines who may or may not \nappear before the bar. Const. art. 4, § 1 vests judicial \npower of the state in the Supreme Court. The formulation \nof rules governing admission to practice is a judicial \nfunction inherent in this constitutionally granted power. \nRatliff, 100 Wn.2d at 215. These rules are rigid and \nformalistic. Id. The “court's power to regulate the practice \nof law in this state is thus not only well established but is \ninviolate as well.” Id.  \nDivision Three in Ayerst and Lewis citing to Ratliff, \nacknowledged that Mr. Van Idour “was not licensed in \nWashington, [and accordingly] he did not have ‘counsel’ as \nthat term is defined for constitutional purposes.” Ayerst, 17 \nWn. App. 2d at 360; Lewis, supra. This should have ended \n \n1 Addressed right to unlicensed individual to act as attorney. \n\n--- Page 10 ---\n \n5 \nall other consideration and resulted in a remand for a new \ntrial with “counsel,” but Division Three did follow Ratliff. \nAyerst, 17 Wn. App. 2d at 360; Lewis, supra. \nIn Ratliff, this Court analyzed both state and federal \nconstitutions to hold that to be authorized to be considered \n“counsel” under the Sixth Amendment and art. 1 section \n22, an attorney or rule 9 must comply with APR’s, make \nan official request, follow proper procedure. and obtain \npermission from this Court though the WSBA. Ratliff, 100 \nWn.2d at 117-18.   \nThis Court’s analysis rested on the premise that all \npersons appearing in court must comply with the applicable \nrules, whether as a legal intern under APR 9, or as an out \nof state attorney seeking permission in Washington under \nAPR 8. The bottom line under each scenario requires that \nwhen the representative fails to comply with the rules, the \nresult is a complete denial of counsel requiring reversal. \nRatliff, 100 Wn.2d at 219. \n\n--- Page 11 ---\n \n6 \nMr. Van Idour never complied with the APR’s, never \nmade an official request, never followed proper procedure \nto obtain permission from this Court though the WSBA, and \nnever took or passed the bar exam. Mr. Van Idour never \nsatisfied this Court’s minimum requirements to be \nconsidered authorized counsel. Id. This is reversible error. \nThis Court will not overturn prior published opinions \nunless “ ‘an established rule is incorrect and harmful,’ ” or \n“the legal underpinnings of the precedent have been \neroded.” State v. Martinez, 196 Wn.2d 605, 612, 476 P.3d \n189 (2020) (quoting Pendergrast v. Matichuk, 186 Wn.2d \n556, 565, 379 P.3d 96 (2016) (quoting In re Rights to \nWaters of Stranger Creek, 77 Wn.2d 649, 653, 466 P.2d \n508 (1970), and citing W.G. Clark Constr. Co. v. Pac. Nw. \nReg’l Council of Carpenters, 180 Wn.2d 54, 66, 322 P.3d \n1207 (2014))).  \n The state cannot show that Ratliff is either incorrect \nand harmful, or that its legal underpinnings under the state \n\n--- Page 12 ---\n \n7 \nand federal constitutions have eroded. This Court has not \nissued a single opinion since Ratliff questioning its \nreasoning in Ratliff, nor has it issued any opinions \nundermining its adherence to the mandatory compliance \nwith the APR’s.  \nBy contrast, this Court approved a trend enhancing \nthe right to counsel protections by adopting the Standards \nfor Indigent Defense (SID’s) as essential and mandatory to \ncomply with the right to counsel under CrR 3.1. On \nFebruary 1, 202,2  this Court formally adopted the \nStandards for Indigent Defense. Preamble to CrR 3.1:  \nThe Washington Supreme Court adopts the \nfollowing Standards to address certain basic \nelements of public defense practice related to \nthe effective assistance of counsel. \n  \n \n2 The preamble to CrR 3.1 was adopted effective October 1, 2012. It \nwas amended effective February 1, 2022. The standards were in \nplace at all times during Mr. Ayerst and Mr. Lewis’s trials, direct \nappeals, and timely personal restraint petitions.    \n \n \n\n--- Page 13 ---\n \n8 \nThe Certification of Appointed Counsel of \nCompliance with Standards Required by CrR \n3.1…[] \nreferences \nspecific \n“Applicable \nStandards.” The \ncourt \nadopts \nadditional \nStandards [SID’s] beyond those required for \ncertification as guidance for public defense \nattorneys in addressing issues identified \nin State v. A.N.J., 168 Wn.2d 91 (2010), \nincluding the suitability of contracts that public \ndefense attorneys may negotiate and sign. \nId. \nSome of the relevant state specific standards set \nforth in SID 14.1 adopted by this Court are as follows:  \nStandard 14.1. In order to assure that indigent \naccused receive the effective assistance of \ncounsel to which they are constitutionally \nentitled, attorneys providing defense services \nshall meet the following minimum professional \nqualifications: \nA. Satisfy the minimum requirements for \npracticing law in Washington as determined by \nthe Washington Supreme Court; and…  \nC. Be familiar with the Washington Rules of \nProfessional Conduct; and…. \n H. Complete seven hours of continuing legal \neducation within each calendar year in courses \nrelating to their public defense practice. \n\n--- Page 14 ---\n \n9 \nSID 14.2 requires: \ni. The minimum requirements set forth in \nSection 1; and \nii. Either: \na. has served two years as a prosecutor; or \nb. has served two years as a public defender; \nor two years in a private criminal practice; and \nThe adoption of these standards is a progression \nfrom In re Dependency of A.N.J., 168 Wn.2d 91, 109-110, \n225 P.3d 956 (2010), where this Court previously \ncontemplated the SID’s in an effective assistance of \ncounsel scenario to hold the SID’s must be consulted \nrather than adopted and applied. State v. Flores, 197 \nWn.App. 2d 1, 14, 386 P.3d 298 (2016) (citing A.N.J., 168 \nWn.2d at 109-110). \nIn Flores, the Court of Appeals examined the SID’s \nwhich are very different from the standards examined in \nA.N.J., but the Court did not understand the distinction.  In \nFlores the Court of Appeals when analyzing an ineffective \nassistance of counsel claim, which is not an issue before \n\n--- Page 15 ---\n \n10 \nthis Court, placed the SIDs in the same category as the \naspirational WDA and WSBA standards. The SIDs should \nhave greater legal effect than WDA and WSBA standards \nas they are rules made by this Court itself rather than \naspirational standards from a bar association group.  \nThe Court in Flores did not analyze or address \nwhether a violation of the SID’s impacts denial of counsel \nclaims in the constitutional sense, or whether such a \nviolation is per se reversible error. Flores, simply regarded \nthe SIDs for guidance treating this Court’s rules in the same \nmanner as the WDA and WSBA standards without \nacknowledging the primacy of the SIDs which are actual \ncourt rules this Court promulgated which must be followed.  \nSince A.N.J. and Flores, this Court determined that \nthe SID’s are mandatory to enhance with specificity, the \nprotections indigent defendants are entitled to receive. \nPreamble CrR 3.1. In Ratliff this Court did not specifically \nconsider appointed versus retained counsel, even though \n\n--- Page 16 ---\n \n11 \nthe Rule 9 was appointed in Ratliff. Instead, this Court held \nthat regardless of who pays, to be considered counsel, a \nperson must meet the minimum qualifications for \nauthorized practice in Washington by satisfying the APR’s. \nRatliff, 100 Wn.2d at 117-18; Preamble to CrR 3.1.  \nConsidering the current cases involve indigent \ndefense, the recency of the adoption of the SID’s cannot \nbe ignored in Mr. Ayerst’s and Mr. Lewis’s cases. Since \nA.N.J., this Court has expressed its alarm at the lack of \nfundamental protections for indigent defendants. Preamble \nto CrR 3.1; A.N.J., 168 Wn.2d at 109-10, 119. Here, not \nonly was Mr. Van Idour appointed to represent Mr. Ayerst \nand Mr. Lewis, he obtained an indigent defense contract in \nviolation of the APR’s and the SID’s to represent an entire \nregion of indigent defendants.  \nThis Court’s awareness and expressed concern for \nstandards of indigent defense practice enhances and plays \nan integral role within the larger overarching requirement \n\n--- Page 17 ---\n \n12 \nthat \nto \nbe \nconsidered \ncounsel \nin \nWashington, \nrepresentatives must be legally authorized to practice and \nmust meet minimum standards. In the present indigent \ndefense cases, under the state and federal constitutions, a \nperson presenting him or herself as authorized counsel for \nindigent defendants, must meet the criteria and standards \nfor practice set forth in the APR’s and the SID’s. Ratliff, 100 \nWn.2d at 115, 117-18; Preamble to CrR 3.1.  \nMr. van Idour did not meet these standards. \na. Second Circuit Case, Solina, is not controlling or \nharmful. \nIn its opinion, Division Three rejected this Court’s \nholding in Ratliff in favor of improperly analyzing the lack of \ncounsel as a hybrid between ineffective assistance of \ncounsel, with a non-binding, Second Circuit case, Solina v. \nUnited States, 709 F.2d 160, 167 (2nd Cir. 1983).  \nFederal decisions are entitled to great weight, but \nthey “are not binding upon the Washington State Supreme \nCourt. Home Ins. Co. of New York v. N. Pac. Ry. Co., 18 \n\n--- Page 18 ---\n \n13 \nWn.2d 798, 808, 140 P.2d 507 (1943); Feis v. King County \nSheriff’s Dept., 165 Wn. App. 525, 547, 267 P.3d 1022 \n(2011).  \nIn Solina, the Second Circuit Court held structural \nerror where Solina was represented by a person who \ngraduated from law school but failed some portion of the \nNew York bar exam. Solina, 709 F.2d at 168-70. The Court \nin Solina held denial of authorized counsel could never be \nharmless error. Solina, 709 F.2d at 168-70 (citing Holloway \nv. Arkansas, supra, 435 U.S. 475, 489, 98 S. Ct. 1173, 55 \nL. Ed. 2d 426 (1978);  Chapman v. California, 386 U.S. 18, \n222-23,  87 S. Ct. 824, 17 L. Ed. 2d 705 (1967); Johnson \nv. Zerbst, 304 U.S. 458, 468, 58 S. Ct. 1019, 82 L. Ed. 1461 \n(1938).  \nDivision Three’s reliance on Solina is misplaced, but \nSolina is neither harmful nor does it provide controlling \nprecedential value. The Division Three cases in Ayerst and \nLewis, are the only opinions in Washington to discuss \n\n--- Page 19 ---\n \n14 \nSolina. In these opinions, Division Three refers to “the \ncharlatan in Solina” as unlike Mr. Van Idour, because Mr. \nVan Idour was licensed to practice law in Idaho, but not in \nWashington, while the person in Solina, graduated from \nlaw school but failed some portion of the bar exam.  Ayerst, \n17 Wn.App.2d at 262; Lewis, supra. The Court seems to \nhave ignored the fact that more egregious than in Solina, \nwith each appearance in Court, Mr. Van Idour committed \ncountless per se crimes under RCW 2.48.150, .170, .180, \n.190. \nThe distinction between a person such as Mr. Van \nIdour who repeatedly commits a crime with each \nappearance and the person in Solina, who failed to pass \nsome portion of the bar exam is meaningless under Ratliff. \n“Unauthorized” means “unauthorized” whether from a \nfailure to pass the bar exam, a failure to meet the \nrequirements for practice or any other reasons. Mr. Van \nIdour, like the representative in Solina, was never \n\n--- Page 20 ---\n \n15 \nauthorized to practice law in Washington and therefore \ncould never be considered counsel.  \nIn Solina, the Court held that the representative’s fear \nof exposure that that he failed the bar created a conflict of \nstructural error magnitude because it was inconsistent with \nthe Sixth Amendment right to conflict free counsel. Solina, \n709 F.2d. at 164-65. Mr. Van Idour like the representative \nin Solina also operated under a similar, but more egregious \nconflict of interest which created structural error in Solina.  \nIn Mr. Lewis and Mr. Ayerst’s cases, Mr. Van Idour’s \nfear of exposure is arguably far greater than in Solina, \nbecause Mr. Van Idour was exposed to incarceration for \neach of the many per se crimes he committed with each \nappearance in court.  \nAnalytically, the conflict of interest is not dissectible: \neither it exists or not and either one is legally permitted to \npractice and hold themselves out as counsel or they are \n\n--- Page 21 ---\n \n16 \nnot. In both cases, Mr. Van Idour and the person in Solina \nwere not counsel. This is structural error.  \nDivision Three attempted to distinguish Solina by \nconsidering that perhaps Mr Van Idour was ignorant of the \nlaw.  \nUnder at least some circumstances, Mr. Van \nIdour could have been qualified to provide \nindigent \ndefense \nrepresentation \nin \nWashington. See APR \n8. \nThe \nWSBA \nallegations against Mr. Van Idour have not \nbeen proven. And even under the facts alleged, \nit is not clear Mr. Van Idour was purposefully \nevading Washington's licensing requirements \nat the time he represented Mr. Ayerst. Mr. Van \nIdour could have been confused. Had that \nbeen the case, he would not have been \noperating under the kind of conflict of interest \nidentified in Solina. \nId.  \nThe Court of Appeals opinion is wrong for three \nreasons. First, based on its reliance on a hypothetical – that \nunder some circumstances, Mr. Van Idour “could have \nbeen qualified” as counsel. Id. “[C]ould” have been is the \n\n--- Page 22 ---\n \n17 \nwrong standard. Under Ratliff, to be considered authorized \ncounsel, one must have permission to practice from this \nCourt, and Mr. Van Idour did not. There are no exceptions, \nhypothetical or otherwise. \nSecond, Division Three created an ignorance of the \nlaw excuse/exception to the illegal practice of law. Divison \nThree held that if, hypothetically, and contrary to the \nevidence presented, Mr. Van Idour was “confused” (Mr. \nVan Idour stipulated to not having permission to practice in \nWashington), then somehow Mr. Ayerst and Mr. Lewis \nwere not denied their constitutional right to counsel. There \nis no ignorance of the law exception to the constitutional \nright to authorized counsel.  \nThird, the Court of Appeals manufactured the “I did \nnot mean it” for Mr. Van Idour to deny Mr. Ayerst and Mr. \nLewis their constitutional right to counsel because Mr. Van \nIdour might not have “purposefully” failed to obtain \npermission to practice in Washington.  \n\n--- Page 23 ---\n \n18 \nThese exceptions are make believe, they do not exist \nin our jurisprudence, and must be rejected. This attempt to \ncreate a harmless error exception to a purely structural \nerror matter must fail because under Ratliff, one is either \nlicensed or permitted to practice law in Washington or not. \nUnder Ratliff, and consistent with Solina, Mr. Van Idour \nwas not authorized to practice law in Washington. This was \nstructural error. \nD. CONCLUSION \nMr. Lewis and Mr. Ayerst respectfully request this \nCourt find they were denied their constitutional right to \ncounsel, vacate their convictions and remand for a new \ntrial.   \n \n \n \n \n \n\n--- Page 24 ---\n \n19 \nDATED this 16th day of February 2022 \n \n \nI, Lise Ellner, in accordance with RAP 18.7, certify that this \ndocument is properly formatted and contains 2733 words. \n \nRespectfully submitted, \n \n \n \n \n \n___________________________ \nLISE ELLNER \nWSBA No. 20955 \nAttorney for Respondent \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n--- Page 25 ---\n \n20 \nI, Lise Ellner, a person over the age of 18 years of age, \nserved \nthe \nAsotin \nCounty \nProsecutor’s \nOffice \nbnichols@co.asotin.wa.us \nand \nRobert \nAyerst/DOC#291791, Airway Heights Corrections Center, \nPO Box 2049, Airway Heights, WA 99001; Justin Lewis \nDOC#403565, Washington State Penitentiary, 1313 North \n13th Avenue, Walla Walla, WA 99362  a true copy of the \ndocument to which this certificate is affixed on February 16, \n2022. Service was made by electronically to the prosecutor \nand to Mr. Ayerst by depositing in the mails of the United \nStates of America, properly stamped \nand addressed. \n \n________________________Signature \n \n \n\n--- Page 26 ---\nLAW OFFICES OF LISE ELLNER\nFebruary 16, 2022 - 9:18 AM\nTransmittal Information\nFiled with Court:\nSupreme Court\nAppellate Court Case Number:   99939-2\nAppellate Court Case Title:\nPersonal Restraint Petition of Justin C. Lewis\nSuperior Court Case Number:\n17-1-00053-3\nThe following documents have been uploaded:\n999392_Briefs_20220216091813SC383280_8650.pdf \n    This File Contains: \n     Briefs - Petitioners Supplemental \n     The Original File Name was Ayerst and Lewis Supp PFR.pdf\nA copy of the uploaded files will be sent to:\nJYoung@co.asotin.wa.us\nbnichols@co.asotin.wa.us\nboruchor@seattleu.edu\ncliedkie@co.asotin.wa.us\njaimehawk@hotmail.com\njeffrey.even@atg.wa.gov\njhawk@aclu-wa.org\njmidgley@aclu-wa.org\npleadings@aclu-wa.org\nrcboru@aol.com\nsgoolyef@atg.wa.gov\ntalner@aclu-wa.org\nComments:\nSender Name: Lise Ellner - Email: liseellnerlaw@comcast.net \nAddress: \nPO BOX 2711 \nVASHON, WA, 98070-2711 \nPhone: 206-930-1090\nNote: The Filing Id is 20220216091813SC383280\n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n", 'source': 'text_input'}
