--- Page 9 ---\n \n \nviii \nWash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations,  \nProclamation 20-07, https://www.governor.wa.gov/ \nsites/default/files/proclamations/20-\n07%20Coronavirus%20%28tmp%29.pdf  \n(last visited Jan. 10, 2022) .................................... 2, 31, 34, 35 \nWash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations,  \nProclamation 20-11, https://www.governor.wa.gov/ \nsites/default/files/proclamations/20-11%20Coronavirus\n%20Gatherings%20Amendment%20%28 \ntmp%29.pdf (last visited Jan. 10, 2022) ........................... 2, 31 \nWash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations,  \nProclamation 20-19.6, https://www.governor.wa.gov/ \nsites/default/files/proclamations/proc_20-19.6.pdf  \n(last visited Jan. 10, 2022) ........................................ 20, 23, 26 \nWash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations,  \nProclamation 20-25, https://www.governor.wa.gov/ \nsites/default/files/proclamations/20-25%20Coronovirus\n%20Stay%20Safe-Stay%20Healthy%20%28tmp% \n29%20%28002%29.pdf  \n(last visited Jan. 10, 2021) ............................................ 6, 7, 27 \nWash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations,  \nProclamation 20-25.3, https://www.governor.wa.gov/ \nsites/default/files/proclamations/20-25.3%20-%20 \nCOVID-19%20Stay%20Home%20Stay%20Healthy \n%20-%20Reopening%20%28tmp%29.pdf  \n(last visited Jan. 10, 2022) ...................................................... 7 \nWash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations,  \nProclamation 20-25.4, https://www.governor.wa.gov/ \nsites/default/files/proclamations/20-25.4%20-%20 \nCOVID-19%20Safe%20Start.pdf  \n(last visited Jan. 10, 2022) ...................................................... 7 \n\n--- Page 10 ---\n \n \nix \nWash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations,  \nProclamation 20-25.8, https://www.governor.wa.gov/ \nsites/default/files/proclamations/proc_20-25.8.pdf  \n(last visited Jan. 10, 2022) ...................................................... 8 \nWash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations,  \nProclamation 20-25.9, https://www.governor.wa.gov/ \nsites/default/files/proclamations/proc_20-25.9.pdf  \n(last visited Jan. 10, 2022) ....................................................... 9 \nWash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations,  \nProclamation 20-25.10, https://www.governor.wa.gov/ \nsites/default/files/proclamations/proc_20-25.10.pdf  \n(last visited Jan. 10, 2022) ......................................................... 9 \nWash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations,  \nProclamation 20-25.11, https://www.governor.wa.gov/ \nsites/default/files/proclamations/proc_20-25.11.pdf  \n(last visited Jan. 10, 2022) .......................................................... 9 \nWash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations,  \nProclamation 20-25.12, https://www.governor.wa.gov/ \nsites/default/files/proclamations/proc_20-25.12.pdf  \n(last visited Jan. 10, 2022) ...................................................... 9 \nWash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations,  \nProclamation 20-25.13, https://www.governor.wa.gov/ \nsites/default/files/proclamations/proc_20-25.13.pdf  \n(last visited Jan. 10, 2022) ...................................................... 9 \nWash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations,  \nProclamation 20-25.14, https://www.governor.wa.gov/ \nsites/default/files/proclamations/proc_20-25.14.pdf  \n(last visited Jan. 10, 2022) .................................................... 10 \n\n--- Page 11 ---\n \n \nx \nWash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations,  \nProclamation 20-28, https://www.governor.wa.gov/ \nsites/default/files/proclamations/20-28%20-%20 \nCOVID-19%20Open%20Govt%20Laws%20 \nWaivers%20%28tmp%291.pdf  \n(last visited Jan. 10, 2022) .................................... 2, 28, 29, 30 \nOther Authorities \nDisaster, Cambridge Dictionary,  \nhttps://dictionary.cambridge.org/us/dictionary/ \nenglish/disaster (last visited Jan. 10, 2022) .......................... 43 \nDisaster, Merriam-Webster.com,  \nhttps://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/ \ndisaster (last visited Jan. 10, 2022) ....................................... 42 \nSenate Concurrent Resolution 8402,  \n67th Leg., Reg. Sess. (Wash. 2021), \nhttp://lawfilesext.leg.wa.gov/biennium/2021-22/ \nPdf/Bills/Senate%20Passed%20Legislature/8402. \nPL.pdf?q=20210121215135  \n(last visited Jan. 10, 2021) .................................. 15, 16, 28, 45  \nWash. Dep’t of Health, COVID-19 Data Dashboard, \nhttps://www.doh.wa.gov/Emergencies/COVID19/ \nDataDashboard (last visited Jan. 10, 2022) ............................ 5 \n \n\n--- Page 12 ---\n \n \n1 \nI. \nINTRODUCTION AND SUMMARY OF \nARGUMENT \n \nElected officials are subject to recall by Washington voters \nonly upon a showing of sufficient cause. Appellant C. Davis fails \nto allege or demonstrate substantial conduct by the Governor \nconstituting misfeasance, malfeasance, or the violation of the \noath of office. Mr. Davis alleges that various Proclamations the \nGovernor issued to combat the deadly COVID-19 virus should \nform the basis for this recall.1 This Court should affirm the \ndecision of the superior court dismissing all of Mr. Davis’ \ncharges as factually and legally insufficient.  \nII. \nISSUES \n \n1. \nDid Mr. Davis allege a factually and legally \nsufficient recall charge by contending that Governor Inslee \ncommitted an act of misfeasance, malfeasance, or violation of his \n                                          \n1 All of the Governor’s proclamations are available \nat https://www.governor.wa.gov/office-governor/official-\nactions/proclamations (last visited Jan. 10, 2022).   \n\n--- Page 13 ---\n \n \n2 \noath of office on the basis that Proclamation 20-19 on residential \nevictions violated the constitutional separation of powers? \n \n2. \nDid Mr. Davis allege a factually and legally \nsufficient recall charge by contending that Governor Inslee \ncommitted an act of misfeasance, malfeasance, or violation of his \noath of office on the basis that Proclamation 20-28 prohibited \npublic agencies from conducting in-person meetings and \nsuspended certain provisions of the Open Public Meetings Act? \n \n3. \nDid Mr. Davis allege a factually and legally \nsufficient recall charge by contending that Governor Inslee \ncommitted an act of misfeasance, malfeasance, or violation of his \noath of office on the basis that Proclamations 20-05, 20-06,  \n20-07, and 20-11, and subsequent proclamations, violated the \nconstitutional right to peaceably assemble by limiting the size of \nin-person gatherings? \n \n4. \nDid Mr. Davis allege a factually and legally \nsufficient recall charge by contending that Governor Inslee \ncommitted an act of misfeasance, malfeasance, or violation of his \n\n--- Page 14 ---\n \n \n3 \noath of office on the basis that Governor Inslee issued emergency \nproclamations related to the COVID-19 pandemic without finding \nthat a public disorder, disaster, energy emergency, or riot existed \nin Washington State due to COVID-19? \nIII. \nSTATEMENT OF THE CASE \nA. \nThe Statement of Charges \n \nMr. Davis, along with several others who are no longer \nparticipating in this case, commenced this matter by filing a \nstatement of charges with the Secretary of State alleging five \ncharges on which to recall Governor Inslee from office. CP \nat 6-67. Mr. Davis expressly abandons the first of his five \ncharges (alleging a violation of religious liberty) on appeal, \nleaving only four charges for consideration. Opening Br. at 15. \nThe Ballot Synopsis summarized those four charges as follows: \n[1]. \nGovernor Inslee violated the constitutional \nseparation of powers by issuing Proclamation \n20-19, \n“Evictions,” \nwhich \nprohibited \nlandlords from initiating judicial actions for \nwrits of restitution involving dwellings for \nfailure of tenants to timely pay rent. \n \n\n--- Page 15 ---\n \n \n4 \n[2]. \nGovernor Inslee violated constitutional rights \nto petition the government for redress of \ngrievances and to peaceably assemble by \nissuing Proclamation 20-28, which prohibited \npublic agencies from conducting in-person \nmeetings and suspended certain provisions of \nthe Open Public Meetings Act. \n \n[3]. \nGovernor Inslee violated the constitutional \nright to peaceably assemble by limiting the \nsize \nof \nin-person \ngatherings \nthrough \nProclamations 20-05, 20-06, 20-07, and 20-11, \nand subsequent proclamations. \n \n[4]. \nGovernor \nInslee \nissued \nemergency \nproclamations related to the COVID-19 \npandemic without finding that a public \ndisorder, disaster, energy emergency, or riot \nexisted in Washington State due to COVID-\n19. \n \nCP at 68. \nB. \nFactual Background \n \nTo place into context the proclamations that form the basis \nof the recall charges, the Governor placed into the record below \nsubstantial evidence of the underlying state of emergency as it \nexisted at the time. CP at 79-595; see also CP at 599-603. Those \nfacts chronicle the development of COVID-19 as a global \n\n--- Page 16 ---\n \n \n5 \npandemic that, as of January 10, 2022, has infected over 820,000 \nWashingtonians, resulting in over 10,000 deaths.2  \n \nCOVID-19 is highly contagious and sometimes fatal. \nCP at 85-86. An infected person or asymptomatic carrier may \nspread COVID-19 without knowing it. CP at 85.  \n \nWhat was then believed to be the first case in the United \nStates occurred in January 2020, in Washington. CP at 84-85. A \nfew days later, the World Health Organization and the U.S. \nDepartment of Health and Human Services declared the outbreak \nto be a public health emergency. CP at 85. On February 29, 2020, \nWashington health officials announced what was then believed \nto be the first COVID-19 death in the United States. CP at 84. \n \nThe Governor issued Proclamation 20-05, declaring a \nstatewide emergency due to COVID-19, on February 29, 2020.3 \n                                          \n2 Wash. Dep’t of Health, COVID-19 Data Dashboard, \nhttps://www.doh.wa.gov/Emergencies/COVID19/DataDashboa\nrd (last visited Jan. 10, 2022).  \n3 Wash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations, Proclamation \n20-05, https://www.governor.wa.gov/sites/default/files/proclam\nations/proc_20-20.5.pdf (last visited Jan. 10, 2022).  \n\n--- Page 17 ---\n \n \n6 \nDespite Washington’s early and aggressive mitigation strategies, \nby mid-March 2020, the state had the nation’s highest absolute \nnumber of COVID-19 cases, and the highest or among the \nhighest per capita. CP at 88-89.  \n \nIn response, the Governor issued a series of proclamations \ndesigned to combat the spread of the virus using the only tools \navailable before the development of vaccines and effective \ntreatments. Early on this included Proclamation 20-25 (Stay \nHome, Stay Healthy Proclamation) on March 23, 2020.4 CP \nat 89. The Governor declared that “the worldwide COVID-19 \npandemic and its progression in Washington State continues to \nthreaten the life and health of our people . . . and remains a public \ndisaster affecting life, health, property or the public peace.” \nProclamation 20-25 at 1. It noted the danger of continued spread, \n                                          \n4 \nWash. \nGov. \nJay \nInslee, \nProclamations,  \nProclamation 20-25, https://www.governor.wa.gov/sites/default\n/files/proclamations/20-25%20Coronovirus%20Stay%20Safe-\nStay%20Healthy%20%28tmp%29%20%28002%29.pdf \n(last \nvisited Jan. 10, 2021).  \n\n--- Page 18 ---\n \n \n7 \nwith the potential for overwhelming the healthcare system. Id. \nThese steps were “medically and scientifically necessary” to \nstem the outbreak in Washington. CP at 88-90. After a month, \nthe Proclamation’s mitigation measures had begun to yield \npositive results, as measured by case rates and hospitalizations. \nCP at 90-91.  \n \nIn May 2020, the Governor shifted to a strategy of phased \nreopening. Proclamation 20-25.3.5 A four-phased “Safe Start” \nPlan followed, under which individual counties could advance \nfrom one phase to another based on the extent to which the spread \nof the virus was contained. Proclamation 20-25.4.6  \n                                          \n5 Wash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations, Proclamation \n20-25.3, https://www.governor.wa.gov/sites/default/files/procla\nmations/20-25.3%20-%20COVID-19%20Stay%20Home%20\nStay%20Healthy%20-%20Reopening%20%28tmp%29.pdf (last \nvisited Jan. 10, 2022).  \n6 Wash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations, Proclamation \n20-25.4, https://www.governor.wa.gov/sites/default/files/procla\nmations/20-25.4%20-%20COVID-19%20Safe%20Start.pdf \n(last visited Jan. 10, 2022).  \n\n--- Page 19 ---\n \n \n8 \n \nUnfortunately, the obstinate nature of the pandemic \ncaused this phased reopening to pause later in 2020. Medical \nnecessity supported additional actions to address the rise in \nCOVID-19 activity, including hospitalizations and deaths. CP \nat 93. These temporary restrictions were originally to last for four \nweeks, from November 17, 2020, through December 14, 2020. \nProclamation 20-25.8.7 However, by December 8, 2020, the \noccupancy of Washington hospitals had grown dangerously \nhigh, with nearly 80 percent of intensive care beds occupied and \nthe effects of Thanksgiving gatherings on infection and hospital \nnumbers still unknown. CP at 98. The Governor extended \n                                          \n7 Wash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations, Proclamation 20-\n25.8, https://www.governor.wa.gov/sites/default/files/proclamat\nions/proc_20-25.8.pdf (last visited Jan. 10, 2022).  \n\n--- Page 20 ---\n \n \n9 \nrestrictions several times before turning to a new phased \nreopening plan. Proclamations 20-25.9,8 20-25.10,9 20-25.11.10 \n \nOn January 11, 2021, the Governor issued Proclamation \n20-25.12,11 which implemented a new phased reopening plan \ncalled “Healthy Washington—Roadmap to Recovery.” That plan \nreduced temporary restrictions, dividing the state into regions \nand reopening each region based on progress on a number of \npublic health metrics. Id. Finally, on May 21, 2021, in \nProclamation 20-25.13,12 the Governor set the state on a path \n                                          \n8 Available at https://www.governor.wa.gov/sites/default/\nfiles/proclamations/proc_20-25.9.pdf (last visited Jan. 10, 2022). \n9 Available at https://www.governor.wa.gov/sites/default/\nfiles/proclamations/proc_20-25.10.pdf (last visited Jan. 10, 2022). \n10 Available at https://www.governor.wa.gov/sites/default\n/files/proclamations/proc_20-25.11.pdf (last visited Jan. 10, 2022). \n11 Wash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations, Proclamation \n20-25.12, https://www.governor.wa.gov/sites/default/files/procl\namations/proc_20-25.12.pdf (last visited Jan. 10, 2022). \n12 Wash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations, Proclamation \n20-25.13, https://www.governor.wa.gov/sites/default/files/procl\namations/proc_20-25.13.pdf (last visited Jan. 10, 2022). \n\n--- Page 21 ---\n \n \n10 \ntoward a statewide reopening on July 1, 2021, and reopened the \nstate through Proclamation 20-25.14.13  \n \nThe facts as recited place into context the Governor’s \nactions that form the basis for Mr. Davis’ recall charges at issue \nhere. Since then, the course of the virus in Washington and \nthroughout the world has continued to evolve, of course, and the \nstate of emergency continues as scientific knowledge and \nmedical circumstances develop. \nC. \nProcedural Background \n \nThe Thurston County Superior Court held a hearing on the \nsufficiency of the recall charges on June 16, 2021. CP at 638. \nAfter reviewing briefing and argument by counsel, the court \nfound all of the charges to be legally and factually insufficient. \nCP at 639. \n \nTwo points Mr. Davis raises in his Opening Brief about \nthe proceedings below merit brief correction. Mr. Davis asserts \n                                          \n13 Wash. Gov. Jay Inslee, Proclamations, Proclamation \n20-25.14, https://www.governor.wa.gov/sites/default/files/procl\namations/proc_20-25.14.pdf (last visited Jan. 10, 2022). \n\n--- Page 22 ---\n \n \n11 \nthat in the trial court the Attorney General represented both the \nGovernor and the proponents of the recall demand. Opening Br. \nat 7-8. The Attorney General did not represent the proponents of \nthe recall below. Rather, the Attorney General drafted a ballot \nsynopsis and petitioned the superior court pursuant to \nRCW 29A.56.130 to determine the sufficiency of the recall \ncharges. CP at 3, 69-70. And, through separately screened \nattorneys, the Attorney General also represented the Governor. \nCP at 596-620. At no time did the Attorney General ever \nrepresent Mr. Davis or the other recall proponents. CP at 622-29. \n \nThe second point for brief response is Mr. Davis’ \nsuggestion of bias on the part of the superior court judge. \nOpening Br. at 8-9. Mr. Davis offers no basis for that suggestion \nbeyond rank speculation, and he has not properly raised such a \nnotion on appeal. It merits no further mention. \n \nTwo notices of appeal to this Court followed the superior \ncourt ruling. Events concerning those appeals determine the \nparties presently before this Court. The recall charges at issue \n\n--- Page 23 ---\n \n \n12 \nwere initially filed in the names of five recall proponents, \nC Davis, Alexis Wallace, Rebecca Faust, Simon Peter Serrano, \nand William Bisson. CP at 6. Mr. Serrano, who is an attorney, \nfiled a notice of appeal solely on behalf of himself and \nMr. Bisson. CP at 640. Mr. Davis, on the next day, filed pro se \nhis own notice of appeal, solely on his own behalf. Notice of \nAppeal (July 1, 2021).14 \n \nMr. Serrano, on behalf of himself and Mr. Bisson, \nrequested to voluntarily withdraw their appeal. Notice of Request \nof Dismissal of Court Review (Oct. 12, 2021). This Court \ngranted that request, leaving Mr. Davis as the sole Appellant. \nLetter Ruling (Oct. 14, 2021). \n                                          \n14 Mr. Davis’ notice of appeal is not included in the clerk’s \npapers. This appears to be the result of an error in compiling the \nclerk’s papers, perhaps because of two separate designations by \nMr. Serrano and Mr. Davis. CP at 644-45 (Mr. Serrano); \nDesignation of Clerk’s Papers (July 30, 2021) (Mr. Davis).  \n\n--- Page 24 ---\n \n \n13 \nIV. \nARGUMENT \nA. \nStandard of Review \n \nThis Court reviews de novo the superior court’s initial \ndetermination of the sufficiency of recall charges. In re Recall of \nWest, 155 Wn.2d 659, 663, 121 P.3d 1190 (2005). The charges \nas a whole must give the elected official enough information to \nrespond and the voters enough information to evaluate the \ncharges. Id. “Although the court does not evaluate the \ntruthfulness or falsity of the allegations, it stands as a gatekeeper \nto ensure that elected officials are not subject to recall for \nfrivolous reasons.” In re Recall of Sun, 177 Wn.2d 251, 255, 299 \nP.3d 651 (2013). “This requires the court to determine that the \nrecall petitioner ha[s] knowledge of the acts complained of, \nRCW 29A.56.110, and that the allegations are both factually and \nlegally sufficient.” Id. (internal quotation marks omitted). \nB. \nThe COVID-19 Pandemic Provides Necessary Context \n \nThe recall charges can be evaluated only in light of the \nemergency authority that state law grants to the Governor. See \n\n--- Page 25 ---\n \n \n14 \nRCW 43.06.220. An emergency “unlocks ‘the powers granted \nthe governor during a state of emergency.’” Colvin v. Inslee, 195 \nWn.2d 879, 895, 467 P.3d 953 (2020) (quoting Cougar Bus. \nOwners Ass’n v. State, 97 Wn.2d 466, 476, 647 P.2d 481 (1982), \noverruled in part on other grounds by Yim v. City of Seattle, 194 \nWn.2d 682, 451 P.3d 694 (2019)).  \n \nState law specifically authorizes the Governor to \n“proclaim a state of emergency” if he “find[s] that a  \npublic disorder” or “disaster . . . exists within this state . . . which  \naffects life, health, property, or the public peace[.]” \nRCW 43.06.010(12). The Governor did so with respect to \nCOVID-19—an action plainly justified under the statute. See \nSlidewaters LLC v. Dep’t of Lab. & Indus., 4 F.4th 747, 755 (9th \nCir. 2021) (“[T]he governor had the lawful authority under \n[RCW] 43.06.010(12) to issue Proclamation 20-05 [declaring \nstate of emergency], as the pandemic is both a ‘public disorder’ \nand a ‘disaster’ affecting ‘life [and] health’ in Washington.”); \nColvin, 195 Wn.2d at 896 (“[t]he governor’s response to an \n\n--- Page 26 ---\n \n \n15 \nemergency ‘is clearly one of those discretionary acts that are ‘in \ntheir nature political, or which are, by the constitution and laws, \nsubmitted to the executive’”) (quoting SEIU Healthcare 775NW \nv. Gregoire, 168 Wn.2d 593, 600, 229 P.3d 774 (2010)). The \nexecutive branch historically has led Washington’s response to \nemergencies. Colvin, 195 Wn.2d at 895. \n \nThe Legislature has itself recently reaffirmed “that the \ngovernor has broad authority to proclaim a state of emergency \n. . . and to exercise emergency powers during the emergency.” \nLaws of 2019, ch. 472, § 1 (emphasis added). In January 2021, \nthe Legislature adopted a concurrent resolution that further \nratifies the Governor’s authority to respond to COVID-19 using \ndelegated emergency authority. Senate Concurrent Resolution \n8402, 67th Leg., Reg. Sess. (Wash. 2021).15 The Legislature \nrecognized the Governor’s emergency authority and extended \n                                          \n15 \nSee \nhttp://lawfilesext.leg.wa.gov/biennium/2021-\n22/Pdf/Bills/Senate%20Passed%20Legislature/8402.PL.pdf?q=\n20210121215135 (last visited Jan. 10, 2021). \n\n--- Page 27 ---\n \n \n16 \nnumerous proclamations that waived or extended statutory \nobligations “until the termination of the state of emergency \npursuant to RCW 43.06.210, or until rescinded by gubernatorial \nor legislative action[.]” Id. This action demonstrates general \nlegislative support for Governor Inslee’s emergency response.   \nC. \nRecall Charges Must be Both Factually and Legally \nSufficient to Proceed \n \nWashington requires that a recall be justified “for cause”; \nit is the only state, among those that provide a process for recall, \nto impose that requirement. In re Recall of Estey v. Dempsey, 104 \nWn.2d 597, 600, 707 P.2d 1338 (1985). By requiring cause, \nWashington does not allow for purely political recalls. Cole v. \nWebster, 103 Wn.2d 280, 285-86, 692 P.2d 799 (1984). To the \ncontrary, Washington law limits recall, “to allow recall for cause \nyet free public officials from the harassment of recall elections \ngrounded on frivolous charges or mere insinuations.” Chandler \nv. Otto, 103 Wn.2d 268, 274, 693 P.2d 71 (1984). These limits \nare well-established and consistent with the intent of the framers \n\n--- Page 28 ---\n \n \n17 \nof the recall provision. In re Recall of Telford, 166 Wn.2d 148, \n152, 206 P.3d 1248 (2009). \n \nMr. Davis conflates this judicial explanation of the \npurpose of judicial review of recall charges with the legal \nstandard that courts apply. Mr. Davis thus suggests that the \nofficer subject to recall bears a burden to prove that the recall \ncharges are “frivolous and . . . unsubstantiated.” Opening Br.  \nat 8. The burden actually rests on the proponent of the recall to \nbring factually and legally sufficient charges. See In re Recall of \nDurkan, 196 Wn.2d 652, 664, 476 P.3d 1042 (2020) (holding \nthat the “charges before the court, though by no means frivolous, \nare insufficient to support a recall election.”) (emphasis added). \n \nRecall must be based on one or more acts of misfeasance, \nmalfeasance, or violation of the oath of office. Misfeasance and \nmalfeasance both mean “any wrongful conduct that affects, \ninterrupts, or interferes with the performance of official duty.” \nRCW \n29A.56.110(1). \nMisfeasance \nadditionally \nmeans  \n“the performance of a duty in an improper manner.”  \n\n--- Page 29 ---\n \n \n18 \nRCW 29A.56.110(1)(a). Malfeasance also means, “the \ncommission of an unlawful act.” RCW 29A.56.110(1)(b). \n“‘Violation of the oath of office’ means the neglect or knowing \nfailure by an elective public officer to perform faithfully a duty \nimposed by law.” RCW 29A.56.110(2). \n \n“The proponent of the recall petition bears the burden of \nestablishing that the charges alleged in the recall petition are both \nlegally and factually sufficient.” In re Recall of Kelley, 185 \nWn.2d 158, 163, 369 P.3d 494 (2016). Factual sufficiency \nrequires the proponent to concisely state each charge, including \n“‘a detailed description including the approximate date, location, \nand nature of each act’” that if true would constitute \nmisfeasance, malfeasance, or the violation of the oath of office. \nRecall of Sun, 177 Wn.2d at 255. Each charge must demonstrate \nthat the petitioner “knows of identifiable facts that support the \ncharge.” In re Recall of Reed, 156 Wn.2d 53, 58, 124 P.3d 279 \n(2005). “[C]harges are factually sufficient only if they enable the \nvoters and the challenged official to make informed decisions.” \n\n--- Page 30 ---\n \n \n19 \nRecall of Kelley, 185 Wn.2d at 164 (citing In re Recall of \nWasson, 149 Wn.2d 787, 791, 72 P.3d 170 (2003)). \n \nTo be legally sufficient, the petitioner must “state with \nspecificity \nsubstantial \nconduct \nclearly \namounting \nto \nmisfeasance, malfeasance, or violation of the oath of office.” In \nre Recall of Bolt, 177 Wn.2d 168, 174, 298 P.3d 710 (2013) \n(emphasis by the court) (citing Chandler, 103 Wn.2d at 274). \nThe petitioner states legally sufficient charges only if he or she \nidentifies the “standard, law, or rule that would make the \nofficer’s conduct wrongful, improper, or unlawful.” In re Recall \nof Ackerson, 143 Wn.2d 366, 377, 20 P.3d 930 (2001). “If recall \nis sought for acts falling within the elected official’s discretion, \nthe official must have acted with a manifest abuse of discretion” \nfor those acts to constitute legally sufficient charges. Recall of \nSun, 177 Wn.2d at 255 (citing Recall of Bolt, 177 Wn.2d at 298). \nD. \nNone of the Charges are Factually or Legally Sufficient \n1. \nThe charge that the Governor violated the \nconstitutional separation of powers by issuing \n\n--- Page 31 ---\n \n \n20 \nProclamation 20-19, related to evictions, is \nfactually and legally insufficient \n \nMr. Davis contends that the Governor should be recalled \nfrom office based on the argument that Proclamation 20-19 \nviolated the separation of powers. This charge is insufficient for \ntwo reasons. First, RCW 43.06.220(1)(h) authorized the \nGovernor’s action. Second, Mr. Davis offers no basis for \nclaiming that the Governor (who acted legally) intended to act \nillegally.16 \n \nMr. Davis charges that the now-expired moratorium on \nresidential evictions violated the constitutional separation of \npowers by “unilaterally dictating the powers and jurisdiction of \n                                          \n16 The eviction proclamation forming the basis of this \ncharge expired by its own terms and by statute on June 20, 2021. \nProclamation 20-19.6 (in effect “until 11:59 pm on June 30, \n2021”); Laws of 2021, ch. 115, § 4(1) (“The eviction moratorium \ninstituted by the governor of the state of Washington’s \nproclamation 20-19.6 shall end on June 30, 2021.”); available at \nhttps://www.governor.wa.gov/sites/default/files/proclamations/\nproc_20-19.6.pdf (last visited Jan. 10, 2022). The Legislature has \nrevised substantially the statutes governing the eviction process \ngoing forward. See generally, Laws of 2021, ch. 115. The charge \naddresses conduct occurring while the proclamation was in \neffect. \n\n--- Page 32 ---\n \n \n21 \nthe supreme and inferior courts of the State of Washington.” CP \nat 9. He alleges no facts in support of the charge other than \nquoting the language of the proclamation. \n \nThe COVID-19 pandemic has been not only a public \nhealth crisis, but also an economic one. The Governor issued \nProclamation 20-19 to prevent economic hardships caused by the \npandemic from translating into the increased spread of the virus. \nIn March 2020, the Washington Employment Security \nDepartment was receiving over 120,000 unemployment claims \neach week (with more than 1.6 million filed by December 2020). \nCP at 193. Recognizing that the pandemic would leave many \ntenants in financial distress and at risk of eviction, the Governor \nsought to keep people in their homes during this public health \nemergency. Eviction forces people into congregate settings such \nas homeless shelters and shared living quarters, where COVID-\n19 spreads most aggressively. CP at 194-95. Homelessness was \na significant problem even before the pandemic, CP at 192, and \nwithout the moratorium, tens or hundreds of thousands of \n\n--- Page 33 ---\n \n \n22 \nWashingtonians could be at risk of eviction. CP at 195.17 Mass \nevictions on that scale could lead to many additional infections \nand deaths in the state. CP at 195-96. Moreover, housing \ninsecurity and evictions tend to disproportionately affect persons \nof color. CP at 196. To address those and related aspects of the \nCOVID-19 emergency, Proclamation 20-19 was developed in a \nconsultative process with a variety of stakeholder groups. CP \nat 198-99. Additionally, the Legislature adopted new legislation \nproviding landlords an avenue for recovering unpaid rent. Laws \nof 2021, ch. 115, §§ 4-5. \n \nThe temporary moratorium in Proclamation 20-19 was a \nconstitutional exercise of the Governor’s emergency power. See \npages 13-16, above. In issuing the moratorium, the Governor \nexpressly relied on his authority under RCW 43.06.220(1) to \n“prohibit . . . activities related to residential dwellings and \ncommercial rental properties” to “help preserve and maintain \n                                          \n17 An August 2020 study estimated that 789,000 \nWashingtonians were at risk of eviction. CP at 195. \n\n--- Page 34 ---\n \n \n23 \nlife, health, property or the public peace.” Proclamation 20-19.16 \nat 5 (citing RCW 43.06.220(1)(h)). This power is “committed to \nthe sole discretion of the Governor and not amenable to review \nin a civil action[.]” Cougar Bus. Owners Ass’n, 97 Wn.2d at 476. \nThe moratorium is well within the Governor’s “exclusive and \ndiscretionary” authority. Colvin, 195 Wn.2d at 897. Given the \npandemic’s economic dislocations and the public health risks \nposed by thousands of displaced tenants, the Governor \n“reasonably believe[d]” that temporarily prohibiting most \nevictions will “help preserve and maintain life, health, property \nor the public peace.” RCW 43.06.220(1)(h). \n \nMr. Davis acknowledges in passing that the Governor \nacted pursuant to explicit statutory authority. Opening Br. at 19. \nBut he simply asserts that Proclamation 20-19 violates the \nseparation of powers by denying some persons (landlords) access \nto the courts. It does not. Washington courts apply the doctrine \nof separation of powers to reserve to the courts those \n“fundamental functions [that] are within the inherent power of \n\n--- Page 35 ---\n \n \n24 \nthe judicial branch, including the power to promulgate rules for \nits practice.” Putman v. Wenatchee Valley Med. Ctr., P.S., 166 \nWn.2d 974, 980, 216 P.3d 374 (2009). This inquiry distinguishes \nbetween procedural and substantive matters. For example, if a \ncourt rule and a statute cannot be harmonized, “the court rule will \nprevail in procedural matters and the statute will prevail in \nsubstantive matters.” Id. at 984. This is because the right of \naccess to courts is different than “the validity of a theory of \nrecovery.” Doe v. Puget Sound Blood Ctr., 117 Wn.2d 772, 781, \n819 P.2d 370 (1991). “Substantive law ‘creates, defines, and \nregulates primary rights,’ while procedures involve the \n‘operations of the courts by which substantive law, rights, and \nremedies are effectuated.’” Putman, 166 Wn.2d at 984 (quoting \nCity of Fircrest v. Jensen, 158 Wn.2d 384, 394, 143 P.3d 776 \n(2006)); see also Waples v. Yi, 169 Wn.2d 152, 161, 234 P.3d \n187 (2010).18 \n                                          \n18 The court in Putman invalidated a statute that required \nmedical malpractice plaintiffs to obtain a “certificate of merit” \n\n--- Page 36 ---\n \n \n25 \nProclamation 20-19 defines neither a judicial procedure \nnor a substantive cause of action. Rather, it does exactly what \nRCW 43.06.220(1)(h) authorizes the Governor to do in an \nemergency: it prohibits conduct that would otherwise constrain \nefforts to combat a global pandemic. The moratorium does not \naddress, let alone constrain, judicial procedures protected by the \nseparation powers. \nNor does Proclamation 20-19 impermissibly infringe \nanyone’s constitutional right of access to courts. Our Supreme \nCourt has held that the legislative abrogation of a remedy—even \none available at common law—does not violate any right of \naccess to the courts. See Shea v. Olson, 185 Wash. 143, 161, 53 \nP.2d 615 (1936) (“There is, therefore, no express, positive \n                                          \nbefore filing a medical malpractice claim. Putman, 166 Wn.2d \nat 977. Waples concerned a requirement for providing advance \nnotice before filing a medical malpractice claim. Waples, 169 \nWn.2d at 155. The Washington Supreme Court invalidated both \nstatutes on the basis that they conflicted with court rules defining \njudicial procedure. Putman, 166 Wn.2d at 985; Waples, 169 \nWn.2d at 160-61. \n\n--- Page 37 ---\n \n \n26 \nmandate of the Constitution which preserves such rights of action \nfrom abolition by the Legislature, even when acting under its \npolice power.”); see also 1519-1525 Lakeview Blvd. Condo. \nAss’n v. Apt. Sales Corp., 144 Wn.2d 570, 581, 29 P.3d 1249 \n(2001) (“the state constitution does not contain any guaranty that \nthere shall be a remedy through the courts for every legal injury \nsuffered by a plaintiff”).19 \nThe Proclamation did not deprive landlords of all \nremedies. For example, it allowed a landlord to bring a breach of \ncontract claim to obtain a money judgment where a tenant was \nnot paying rent after the tenant refused or failed to comply with \na repayment plan that is based on the individual financial, health, \nand other circumstances of that resident. Proclamation 20-19.6 \nalso contained exceptions, allowing eviction orders to  \n(a) respond to a significant and immediate risk to others’ health, \n                                          \n19 The argument that Proclamation 20-19 impermissibly \ninfringed the right of access to the courts is also at issue in an \nappeal pending before the Court of Appeals. Gonzales v. Inslee, \nNo. 55915-3-II (oral argument scheduled for Jan. 25, 2022). \n\n--- Page 38 ---\n \n \n27 \nsafety, or property; (b) personally occupy the premises as the \nowner’s primary residence; or (c) sell the property.  \nEven if this Court questioned the constitutionality of the \neviction moratorium, that alone would be insufficient to support \nrecall. A difference of opinion as to the legality of the Governor’s \nproclamation in no way suggests that the Governor intended to \nact illegally. Recall of Telford, 166 Wn.2d at 154. \nMr. Davis has not alleged any facts beyond the language \nof Proclamation 20-25 and speculative characterizations of its \neffect on landowners. He has not alleged or offered any facts that, \nif true, would constitute a violation of constitutional separation \nof powers. This charge is factually and legally insufficient. \n2. \nThe \ncharge \nthat \nthe \nGovernor \nviolated \nconstitutional rights to petition the government \nfor redress of grievances and to peaceably \nassemble by issuing Proclamation 20-28 in \nresponse to the COVID-19 pandemic is factually \nand legally insufficient \n \nMr. Davis next charges that the Governor exceeded the \nauthority granted in RCW 43.06.220(2)(g) by issuing \n\n--- Page 39 ---\n \n \n28 \nProclamation 20-28,20 which temporarily prohibited agencies \nsubject to the Open Public Meetings Act (RCW 42.30) from \nconducting in-person meetings. Petitioner also charges that \nProclamation 20-28 violated his First Amendment right to \npeaceably assemble and petition the government. CP at 10-11. \nNotably, this Proclamation is among those extended by \nlegislative action through the end of the COVID-19 emergency. \nS. Con. Res. 8402. \n \nThe Open Public Meetings Act serves an important \npurpose: ensuring that most decisions of multimember governing \nbodies of public agencies are made in sessions open to the public. \nRCW 42.30.010. Proclamation 20-28 was narrowly tailored to \nensure public access even during the COVID-19 pandemic, \nrequiring that any meeting subject to the Act must “provide[] an \noption(s) for the public to attend the proceedings through, at \n                                          \n20 See https://www.governor.wa.gov/sites/default/files/pr\noclamations/20-28%20-%20COVID-19%20Open%20Govt%20\nLaws%20Waivers%20%28tmp%291.pdf (last visited Jan. 10, \n2022).  \n\n--- Page 40 ---\n \n \n29 \nminimum, telephonic access, and may also include other \nelectronic, internet or other means of remote access,” and must \n“provide[] the ability for all persons attending the meeting to \nhear each other at the same time.”21 And so no citizens are barred \nfrom attending and participating in open public meetings \nremotely. \n \nSignificantly, the Act is not a statutory codification of the \nFirst Amendment right to petition government and it cannot be \nconstrued as such. The First Amendment “does not grant to \nmembers of the public generally a right to be heard by public \nbodies making decisions of policy.” Minn. State Bd. for Cmty. \nColls. v. Knight, 465 U.S. 271, 283, 104 S. Ct. 1058, 1065, 79 L. \nEd. 2d 299 (1984).22 “However wise or practicable various levels \nof public participation in various kinds of policy decisions may \n                                          \n21 Id. at 2-3. \n22 Although the Petition does not charge a violation of the \nWashington Constitution, article I, section 4 of the Washington \nConstitution has been interpreted as consistent with the First \nAmendment. Richmond v. Thompson, 130 Wn.2d 368, 383, 922 \nP.2d 1343 (1996) (conducting Gunwall analysis). \n\n--- Page 41 ---\n \n \n30 \nbe, this Court has never held, and nothing in the Constitution \nsuggests it should hold, that government must provide for such \nparticipation.” Id. at 285.  \n \nEven a full repeal of the Open Public Meetings Act thus \nwould not violate the First Amendment. Consequently, a \ntemporary suspension of some of the Act’s provisions in \nresponse to a public emergency does not violate the First \nAmendment. Proclamation 20-28 does not prohibit citizens from \nseeking redress from government. And to the extent \nProclamation 20-28 temporarily prohibited gatherings, it does \nnot prohibit freedom of assembly under the First Amendment for \nthe reasons explained in the following section of this brief. \n \nOther than alleging the issuance of Proclamations 20-05 \nand 20-28, Mr. Davis has stated only two facts: (1) Governor \nInslee signed Senate Bill 5260 in 2019, CP at 10; and (2) the \nnumber of Washington households reported to lack internet \nconnection in 2018. CP at 11. Of course, even if many people \nlack internet access, many others lack the practical ability to \n\n--- Page 42 ---\n \n \n31 \nattend meetings in person due to demands of time and distance. \nHe has not alleged or offered any facts that, if true, would \nconstitute a violation of the right to petition government or the \nright to freedom of assembly under the First Amendment. This \ncharge is factually and legally insufficient. \n3. \nThe charge that the Governor violated the \nconstitutional right to peaceably assemble by \nlimiting the size of in-person gatherings through \nProclamations 20-05, 20-06, 20-07, and 20-11, \nand subsequent proclamations is factually and \nlegally insufficient \nMr. Davis charges that the Governor exceeded the \nauthority \ngranted \nin \nRCW 43.06.220(2)(g) \nby \nissuing \nProclamations 20-05,23 20-06,24 20-07,25 and 20-1126 to \n                                          \n23 Available at https://www.governor.wa.gov/sites/default\n/files/proclamations/20-05%20Coronavirus%20%28final\n%29.pdf (last visited Jan. 10, 2022).  \n24 Available at https://www.governor.wa.gov/sites/default\n/files/proclamations/20-06%20Coronavirus%20%28tmp%29\n.pdf (last visited Jan. 10, 2022).  \n25 Available at https://www.governor.wa.gov/sites/default\n/files/proclamations/20-07%20Coronavirus%20%28tmp%29\n.pdf (last visited Jan. 10, 2022).  \n26 Available at https://www.governor.wa.gov/sites/default\n/files/proclamations/20-11%20Coronavirus%20Gatherings%20\nAmendment%20%28tmp%29.pdf (last visited Jan. 10, 2022).  \n\n--- Page 43 ---\n \n \n32 \ntemporarily limit the number of people attending a single \ngathering. CP at 11-12. This is the only one of the charges for \nwhich Mr. Davis alleges “‘a detailed description including the \napproximate date, location, and nature of each act,’” Recall of \nSun, 177 Wn.2d at 255 (quoting RCW 29A.56.110), to support \nthe charge: a series of applications to hold rallies on the capitol \ncampus that were denied in compliance with those \nproclamations. But even assuming the truth of the facts, they \nwould not constitute misfeasance, malfeasance, or the violation \nof the oath of office.27  \nMr. Davis alleges a violation of the First Amendment \nfreedom of assembly. The correct legal framework governing \n                                          \n27 This charge is based on facts relating to the experience \nof one of the original proponents of recall, Rebecca Faust. CP \nat 16-67. Ms. Faust separately litigated in federal court her claim \nthat the Governor’s restrictions on demonstrations at the Capitol \nCampus violated her First Amendment rights. The federal court \ndismissed her claim on the basis that the expiration of the \nrelevant proclamations (and Ms. Faust holding the demonstration \nshe desired) rendered it moot. Faust v. Inslee, No. C20-5356 \nBHS, 2021 WL 4288587, at *2 (W.D. Wash. Sept. 21, 2021). \n\n--- Page 44 ---\n \n \n33 \nassembly claims tracks the well-established “time, place, and \nmanner” standards for regulating protected speech. See C.L. for \nUrban Believers v. City of Chicago, 342 F.3d 752, 765 (7th \nCir. 2003). The challenged Proclamations are facially valid \nbecause they are (1) “content-neutral,” (2) “designed to serve a \nsubstantial governmental interest,” and (3) “do not unreasonably \nlimit alternative avenues of communication.” City of Renton v. \nPlaytime Theatres, Inc., 475 U.S. 41, 47, 106 S. Ct. 925, 89 L. \nEd. 2d 29 (1986).  \nFirst, the Proclamations are content neutral. They “serve[] \npurposes unrelated to the content of expression.” Ward v. Rock \nAgainst Racism, 491 U.S. 781, 791, 109 S. Ct. 2746, 105 L. Ed. \n2d 661 (1989). The Governor did not adopt them because of any \n“disagreement with the message” at any gatherings, id., but to \ncombat a pandemic. Mr. Davis states no fact indicating or even \nsuggesting that the permit applications cited in the charges were \ndenied because of the message or content of the proposed rallies. \nMr. Davis’ speculation regarding the Governor’s motives to the \n\n--- Page 45 ---\n \n \n34 \ncontrary are just that: speculation. Opening Br. at 41. The \nProclamations and the denials of the permits do not target the \nmessage, but rather the “harmful secondary effects of public \ngathering—the spread of a novel virus for which there currently \nis no cure or effective treatment.” Geller v. de Blasio, No. 20-cv-\n3566 (DLC), 2020 WL 2520711, at *4 (S.D.N.Y. May 18, 2020). \nAs stated in Proclamation 20-07 (March 11, 2020), the first of \nthe challenged Proclamations that specifically limits gatherings, \nCOVID-19 is “a respiratory disease that spreads easily from \nperson to person and may result in serious illness or death”; “to \nreduce spread of COVID-19, the United States Centers for \nDisease Control and Prevention and the Washington State \nDepartment of Health (DOH) recommend implementation of \ncommunity mitigation strategies to increase containment of the \nvirus, including cancellation of large gatherings and social \ndistancing in smaller gatherings”; and “implementation of \nlimitations on large gatherings and use of social distancing \n\n--- Page 46 ---\n \n \n35 \nprevent initial exposure and secondary transmission to our most \nvulnerable populations.” Proclamation 20-07. \nSecond, \nthe \nProclamations \nserve \na \n“substantial \ngovernment interest that would be achieved less effectively \nabsent the regulation.” Ward, 491 U.S. at 799 (citation and \ninternal quotation marks omitted). Protecting public health and \nslowing the spread of a highly contagious pandemic is not only \na “substantial” governmental interest, but a compelling one, as \ndiscussed above. Accord Legacy Church, Inc. v. Kunkel, 455 F. \nSupp. 3d 1100, 1159 (D.N.M. 2020); Givens v. Newsom, 459 F. \nSupp. 3d 1302, 1315 (E.D. Cal. 2020). The physical distancing \nand masking strategies the Proclamations implement serves that \ninterest and, at the time of the challenged Proclamations, were \nthe only known effective way to slow the transmission of \nCOVID-19. See, e.g., Benner v. Wolf, 461 F. Supp. 3d 154, 162 \n(M.D. Pa. 2020) (“public health experts the world over \nproclaimed that social distancing was the only effective way to \ncombat its deadly effects”); see also CP at 89. \n\n--- Page 47 ---\n \n \n36 \nBecause slowing the spread of the virus would be achieved \nless effectively without the Proclamations’ physical distancing \nstrategy, the Proclamations are narrowly tailored. To be narrowly \ntailored to achieve a legitimate state interest, the Proclamations \n“need not be the least restrictive or least intrusive means of doing \nso.” Ward, 491 U.S. at 798. They are narrowly tailored “[s]o long \nas the means chosen are not substantially broader than necessary \nto achieve the government’s interest.” Id. at 800. Because \nCOVID-19 spreads easily and because asymptomatic individuals \nmay spread the virus, prohibiting gatherings or limiting their size \n“promotes a substantial government interest that would be \nachieved less effectively absent the regulation.” Antietam \nBattlefield KOA v. Hogan, 461 F. Supp. 3d 214, 235 (D. \nMd. 2020). \nThird, while a Proclamation restricting public gatherings \nmight not be found to be narrowly tailored in ordinary times, \n“these times are extraordinary [and] . . . the scientific and \nmedical communities believe that preventing in-person \n\n--- Page 48 ---\n \n \n37 \ngatherings is crucial to any strategy of containment” of \nCOVID-19. Geller, 2020 WL 2520711, at *4.  \nMoreover, the challenged Proclamations did not prevent \nWashingtonians from using online tools, electronic media, \ntelephones, and other such means of communicating with one \nanother or organizing political protests or other messages to the \ncommunity or to government. These means have been held to be \nacceptable alternatives to public group protests while restrictions \non public gathering are necessary to contain the spread of \nCOVID-19. Id.; accord Givens, 459 F. Supp. 3d at 1314 \n(observing that these alternatives may be a more effective way of \ncommunicating their messages during the pandemic, since \n“much of their intended audience is presently at home”). See also \nPackingham v. North Carolina, 137 S. Ct. 1730, 1735, 198 L. \nEd. 2d 273 (2017) (describing cyberspace as the most important \nplace for the exchange of views today, and specifically noting \nthe access to elected officials). The gathering restrictions in the \n\n--- Page 49 ---\n \n \n38 \nProclamations are reasonable limitations to prevent the spread of \nthe virus. \nThe facts Mr. Davis relies on, even if true, do not \nconstitute misfeasance, malfeasance, or a violation of the oath of \noffice. See Recall of Sun, 177 Wn.2d at 255. Each act complained \nof was taken in compliance with Proclamations that were legally \njustified by the need to protect the life and health of persons in \nWashington by stopping or slowing the transmission of COVID-\n19. Because the Proclamations and the acts identified by the \nPetitioner were legally justified, this charge is legally \ninsufficient. In re Recall of Olsen, 154 Wn.2d 606, 610, 116 P.3d \n378 (2005). Because the Governor acted with legal justification, \nthe Proclamations did not violate the First Amendment, and there \nis no factual basis on which to find that the Governor intended to \nviolate the Constitution.28 Because there is no evidence of intent \n                                          \n28 Mr. Davis contends that if an act was illegal, this is proof \nthat the Governor intended to violate the law. As described, the \nacts at issue were legal. But even aside from that point, adoption \n\n--- Page 50 ---\n \n \n39 \nto act unlawfully and there is legal justification for the acts \ncomplained of, Petitioner’s charge is both legally and factually \ninsufficient and should be dismissed. See Recall of Telford, 166 \nWn.2d at 158. \n4. \nThe charge that the Governor issued emergency \nproclamations \nrelated \nto \nthe \nCOVID-19 \npandemic without finding that a public disorder, \ndisaster, energy emergency, or riot existed in \nWashington State due to COVID-19 is factually \nand legally insufficient \nMr. Davis’ final charge is that, beginning on February 29, \n2020, every Proclamation the Governor issued to address the \nCOVID-19 pandemic exceeded his authority under RCW 43.06 \nbecause he “failed to make a finding that a ‘public disorder, \ndisaster, energy emergency, or riot’” existed in Washington \nbecause of COVID-19. CP at 12-14. This charge rests not on any \nalleged facts, but entirely on the meaning assigned to terms used \nin RCW 43.06.010(12). Mr. Davis contends that the COVID-19 \npandemic is not a public disorder, disaster, energy emergency, or \n                                          \nof Mr. Davis’ argument would essentially eliminate the concept \nof intent from the analysis of recall charges. \n\n--- Page 51 ---\n \n \n40 \nriot. The Ninth Circuit has already rejected this argument, \nSlidewaters, 4 F.4th at 755, and Mr. Davis’ overly narrow \ninterpretation of “disaster” is inconsistent with statutory \nlanguage and purpose, and with this Court’s precedent. \nRCW 43.06.010(12) gives the Governor authority to \ndeclare a state of emergency. Once an emergency has been \ndeclared, RCW 43.06.220 “specifically authorize[s] the \nGovernor to issue orders with operative effect” to respond to the \nemergency. Fischer-McReynolds v. Quasim, 101 Wn. App. 801, \n813, 6 P.3d 30 (2000). The Governor may issue orders \nprohibiting various activities, or waiving or suspending statutory \nobligations. RCW 43.06.220. \nThis Court has held that these statutes “evidence a clear \nintent by the [L]egislature to delegate requisite police power to \nthe governor in times of emergency.” Cougar Bus. Owners \nAss’n, 97 Wn.2d at 474. The Legislature confirmed that intent \nwhen it recently amended RCW 43.06.220, stating “that the \ngovernor has broad authority to proclaim a state of emergency in \n\n--- Page 52 ---\n \n \n41 \nany area of the state under RCW 43.06.010(12), and to exercise \nemergency powers during the emergency.” Laws of 2019, \nch. 472, § 1; see note following RCW 43.06.220. \nWhere the Legislature declares an emergency, that \ndeclaration is given every favorable presumption and deferred to \nunless it is obvious that the declaration is false. CLEAN v. State, \n130 Wn.2d 782, 807, 928 P.2d 1054 (1996), as amended Jan. 13, \n1997). Because the Legislature has given the Governor broad \nauthority to proclaim an emergency, the same deference should \nbe given the Governor’s declaration of an emergency.  \nAs explained above, the unique dangers posed by \nCOVID-19 are widely recognized by the nation’s leading health \nofficials and experts, and they led both the World Health \nOrganization and U.S. Department of Health and Human \nServices to declare in March 2020, that the COVID-19 outbreak \nis a public health emergency. CP at 85.  \nIn Proclamation 20-05, the first Proclamation responding \nto the COVID-19 outbreak, the Governor explicitly found that \n\n--- Page 53 ---\n \n \n42 \n“[t]he worldwide outbreak of COVID-19 and the effects of its \nextreme risk of person-to-person transmission throughout the \nUnited States and Washington State significantly impacts the life \nand health of our people, as well as the economy of Washington \nState, and is a public disaster that affects life, health, property or \nthe public peace.” Under Washington case law, “[a] nontechnical \nstatutory term [like “disaster”] may be given its dictionary \nmeaning” and “statutes should be construed to effect their \npurpose, and unlikely, absurd, or strained consequences should \nbe avoided.” State v. Smith, 189 Wn.2d 655, 662, 405 P.3d 997 \n(2017). Webster’s dictionary defines a disaster in relevant part as \n“a sudden calamitous event bringing great damage, loss, or \ndestruction.”29 Similarly, the leading definition of “disaster” in \nthe Cambridge Dictionary is “(an event that results in) great \n                                          \n29 Disaster, Merriam-Webster.com, https://www.merriam\n-webster.com/dictionary/disaster (last visited Jan. 10, 2022). \n\n--- Page 54 ---\n \n \n43 \nharm, damage, or death, or serious difficulty.”30 The COVID-19 \npandemic readily falls within both definitions. See Slidewaters, \n4 F.4th at 755. \nThis Court upheld a broad definition of “disaster” in \nCougar. The plaintiffs in that case, involving the Mt. St. Helens \neruption, sought to define “disaster” as “a specifically definable \nevent causing harm or injury[.]” Cougar Bus. Owners Ass’n, 97 \nWn.2d at 475. But the Court rejected this crabbed definition as \n“unduly narrow” and overly restrictive in light of the clear \n“legislative intent to empower the governor to respond to \nemergencies.” Id. This Court recognized that the “disaster” was \nnot simply the eruption but the entire “reactivation of a dormant \nvolcano.” Id. Similarly, the COVID-19 pandemic meets this \nbroad definition of a “disaster.” \n                                          \n30 Disaster, Cambridge Dictionary, https://dictionary.cam\nbridge.org/us/dictionary/english/disaster (last visited Jan. 10, \n2022). \n\n--- Page 55 ---\n \n \n44 \nThe definition in a related statute concerning emergency \nmanagement also illuminates the meaning of “disaster” in \nRCW 43.06.010(12). RCW 38.52.010(9)(a) defines the phrase \n“[e]mergency \nor \ndisaster” \nwith \nspecific \nreference \nto \nRCW 43.06.010: \n“Emergency or disaster” as used in all sections of \nthis chapter [Chapter 38.52] except RCW 38.52.430 \nmeans an event or set of circumstances which: (i) \nDemands immediate action to preserve public \nhealth, protect life, protect public property, or to \nprovide relief to any stricken community overtaken \nby such occurrences; or (ii) reaches such a \ndimension or degree of destructiveness as to \nwarrant the governor proclaiming a state of \nemergency pursuant to RCW 43.06.010. \n \nThis broad definition expressly recognizes that the \nGovernor may declare an emergency under RCW 43.06.010 \nbased on the criterion of “destructiveness.” As applied here, a \nglobal pandemic that has cost 800,000 American lives, caused \nsevere economic and social disruptions, and which has required \nmassive public health interventions, possesses the requisite \n“dimension or degree of destructiveness.” RCW 52.10.010(9)(a). \n\n--- Page 56 ---\n \n \n45 \nThe Legislature vested this decision in the Governor, not in the \ncourts, and not in groups of citizens looking to different \ndictionaries. \nFinally, in January 2021, the Washington Legislature \nadopted S. Con. Res. 8402, which further ratifies the Governor’s \nauthority to respond to COVID-19 using delegated emergency \nauthority. The Legislature recognized the Governor’s emergency \nauthority and extended numerous proclamations that waived or \nextended statutory obligations “until the termination of the state \nof emergency pursuant to RCW 43.06.210, or until rescinded by \ngubernatorial or legislative action[.]” S. Con. Res. 8402. If the \nLegislature did not consider the Governor’s proclamation of an \nemergency to be valid, it presumably would not have extended \nsubsequent proclamations that depended upon it. \nThe Governor’s emergency proclamation was proper \nbecause COVID-19 is a disaster requiring government action to \nprotect life and public safety. \n\n--- Page 57 ---\n \n \n46 \nThe only articulated basis for this charge against the \nGovernor is Mr. Davis’ attempt to find alternative meanings for \nstatutory terms. But under the meanings assigned by courts \ninterpreting RCW 43.06.010(12) and informed by definitions in \nrelated statutes, it is clear that the COVID-19 pandemic readily \nfalls within at least one term used in RCW 43.06.010(12). \nMr. Davis presents no factual or legal basis to support this \ncharge. This final charge is factually and legal insufficient. \nV. \nCONCLUSION \n \nFor these reasons, this Court should affirm the decision of \nthe superior court holding that all of the charges are factually and \nlegally insufficient to support recall. \n \n \nThis document contains x,xxx words, excluding the parts \nof the document exempted from the word count by RAP 18.17. \n \nRESPECTFULLY SUBMITTED this 11th day of \nJanuary, 2022.   \nROBERT W. FERGUSON  \n   Attorney General  \n \n\n--- Page 58 ---\n \n \n47 \n s/ Jeffrey T. Even  \nJEFFREY T. EVEN, WSBA 20367  \nPETER GONICK, WSBA 25616 \n   Deputy Solicitors General  \nOffice ID 91087  \nPO Box 40100  \nOlympia, WA 98504-0100  \n360-753-6200 \njeffrey.even@atg.wa.gov \npeter.gonick@atg.wa.gov \n \nAttorneys for Respondent Jay Inslee \n \n \n\n--- Page 59 ---\n \n \n48 \nCERTIFICATE OF SERVICE \nI certify that on this date, I electronically filed this \ndocument with the Clerk of the Court using the Washington State \nAppellate Courts’ e-file portal, which will send notification of \nsuch filing to all counsel of record at the following: \n \nC. Davis \nwas2016@hotmail.com; recallinslee@gmail.com \n \n \nDATED this 11th day of January 2022, at Olympia, \nWashington. \n s/ Leena Vanderwood \nLEENA VANDERWOOD \n   Legal Assistant \n \n\n--- Page 60 ---\nSOLICITOR GENERAL OFFICE\nJanuary 11, 2022 - 1:45 PM\nTransmittal Information\nFiled with Court:\nSupreme Court\nAppellate Court Case Number:   99948-1\nAppellate Court Case Title:\nIn the Matter of the Recall of Jay Inslee\nSuperior Court Case Number:\n21-2-00968-2\nThe following documents have been uploaded:\n999481_Briefs_20220111134440SC131673_8251.pdf \n    This File Contains: \n     Briefs - Respondents \n     The Original File Name was InsleeResponseBr_Final.pdf\nA copy of the uploaded files will be sent to:\npeter.gonick@atg.wa.gov\nrecallinslee@gmail.com\nwas2016@hotmail.com\nComments:\nSender Name: Kristin Jensen - Email: kristinj@atg.wa.gov \n    Filing on Behalf of: Jeffrey Todd Even - Email: jeffrey.even@atg.wa.gov (Alternate Email:\nSGOOlyEF@atg.wa.gov)\nAddress: \nPO Box 40100\n1125 Washington St SE \nOlympia, WA, 98504-0100 \nPhone: (360) 753-4111\nNote: The Filing Id is 20220111134440SC131673\n• \n• \n• \n• \n', 'source': 'text_input'}
