.......... . ..... . .. .. ...• . , . . . . . . . . . . . 7 \nU.S. Court of Appeals Cases \nCole v. United States, 162 F.3d 957 {7th Cir. 1998) . . .. . 9 \nDerringerv. United States, 441 F.2d 1140 (8th Cir. 1971). 9 \nHardamon v. United States, 319 F.3d 943 (7th Cir. 2003). 8 \nKieser v. New York, 56 F.3d 16 (2nd Cir. 1995) .. .... .. 9 \nUnited States v. Costanzo, 740 F.2d 251 (3rd Cir. 1984) . 9 \nUnited States v. Maria-Martinez, 143 F.3d 914 (5th Cir. \n1998) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 8-10 \nUnited States v. Mitchell, 216 F.3d 1126 (D.C. Cir. 2000) . 8 \nUnited States v. Watson, 479 F.3d 607 (8th Cir.) .... . .. 8 \nState Supreme Court Cases \nCity of Seattle v. Ratliff, 100 Wn.2d 212 (1983) ... . passim \nIn re Coats, 173 Wn.2d 123 (2011) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 5 \nIn re Personal Restraint Petition of Crace, 174 Wn.2d 835 \n(2012) ....................................... • 7 \nIn re Personal Restraint of Davis, 152 Wn.2d 647 (2004) . 7 \nIn re Personal Restraint Petition of Finstad, 177 Wn.2d 501 \n(2013) •• . .... .. . . . .. .... ....... ... ....•.... . ; . 7 \nii \n\n--- Page 4 ---\nState Court of Appeals Cases \nMatter of Ayerst, 17 W n .App. 356 (Div. Ill, 2021 ) . . . . . . . 9 \nMatter of Lewis, 37284-7-111, 2021 WL 1750935 (Div. 111, May \n4, 2021) (unpublished) . .. . .......... . .......... 5, 1 O \nState v. Edison, 61 Wn.App. 530 (1991) . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 9 \nState v. Justin C. Lewis, 35775-9-:111, 2019 WL 1568058 (Div. \n111, Apr. 11, 2019)(unpublished) ... .. . . . ... . . . ..... . 4 \nState v. Medlock, 86 Wn.App. 89 (1997) ...... .... .. .. 7 \nOther Cases \nCheatham v. State, 364 So.2d 83 (Fla.Dist.Ct.App. 1978} 13 \nFerrara v. Keane, 806 F.Supp.472 (S.D.N.Y. 1992) .... . 8 \nIn re Moore, 63 III.App.3d 899 (1978) ... _ . . . . .. ..... . 13 \nPeople v. Felder, 47 N.Y.2d 287 (1979) .. . ..... ... .. 13 \nPeople v. Masonis. 58 Mich.App. 615 (1975) . . ... . . . , 13 \nCourt Rules \nRAP 13.4 .. . . .. . .. . .............. , . . . . . . . . . • . . . 5 \nOther Authorities \nConst. amend. VI ... .. .... .... . . . . • .. . . ..... .. . . . 7 \nConst. Art. 1 , § 22 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .. .. , . . . . . 7 \nAPR 8 . . ... . .... . . .. . .. . . . . . .. .. . . . 2-4, 10, 15, 18 \nAPR9 . .. . . . .. . . . , •... . . .. .. ... ... .. . . .. .. 10-14 \niii \n\n--- Page 5 ---\nI. \nSUMMARY OF ISSUES \n1. \n2. \nDOES THE COURT OF APPEALS\' DECISION IN \nMATTER OF LEWIS CONFLICT WITH THIS \nCOURT\'S DECISION IN CITY OF SEATTLE VS. \nRATLIFF? \nDOES THE COURT OF APPEALS\' DECISION, IN \nWHICH THE COURT APPLIED EXISTING LAW TO \nDEFENDANT\'S CASE, INVOLVE AN ISSUE OF \nSUBSTANTIAL PUBLIC INTEREST? \nII. \nSUMMARY OF ARGUMENT \n1. \n2. \nTHE COURT\'S DECISION DOES NOT CONFLICT \nWITH RA TL/FF BECAUSE RA TUFF IS SPECIFIC \nTO APR 9 LEGAL INTERNS AND FITS WITHIN THE \nLEGAL FRAMEWORK USED BY THE COURT IN \nTHIS CASE \nTHE COURT\'S DECISION DOES NOT INVOLVE AN \nISSUE OF SUBSTANTIAL PUBLICATION BECAUSE \nIT DOES NOT CHANGE THE PUBLIC\'S \nCONSTITUTIONAL RIGHT TO COUNSEL. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n1 \n\n--- Page 6 ---\nIll. \nSTATEMENT OF THE CASE \nThe Defendant was convicted after jury trial of Assault in the \nFirst Degree, Robbery in the First Degree, Possession of a \nControlled Substance (Heroin), and Possession of Drug \nParaphernalia. See State v. Justin C. Lewis, 35775-9-111, 2019 WL \n1568058 (Div. 111, Apr. 11, 2019)(Unpublished).1 The underlying \nfacts of the case are immaterial and warrant no discussion. \nThe Defendant was represented by attorney Robert Van \nldour throughout the proceeding from its origin. Bond Order \n(04/05/17) (see attached State\'s Response to Personal Restraint \nPetition and attached documents, attached hereto). At the time, Mr. \nVan I dour was a licensed member of the Idaho State Bar \nAssociation, in good standing, and was assigned to provide public \ndefense, on contract under APR 8(c). Declaration of Robert J. Van \nldour (see attached State\'s Response to Personal Restraint \nPetition and attached documents, attached hereto). Mr. Van ldour \nhad contracted with Asotin County and had arranged to practice \nunder APR B(c), which authorizes attorneys who are licensed by \nother states to appear for the purposes of public defense while they \napply for a formal license to practice in Washington. Declaration of \n1 The State does not cite the unpublished decision for \nprecedent, but rather as establishing the procedural and factual \nbackground of the case. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n2 \n\n--- Page 7 ---\nRobert J. Van ldour, Declaration of Vivian Bly, (see attached \nState\'s Response to Personal Restraint Petition and attached \ndocuments, attached hereto). Prior to contracting with Mr. Van \n!dour, the Board of County Commissioners inquired of the Superior \nCourt Judge, Scott Gallina, regarding Mr. Van ldour\'s qualifications \nto contract for public defense without being licensed in \nWashington. Declaration of Vivian Bly. Judge Gallina confirmed \nthat Mr. Van ldour would be eligible through APR 8(c) process to \nperform duties under a contract while he applied for admission to \nthe Washington State Bar. Throughout the process, the Superior \nCourt Administrator made periodic efforts to contact the \nWashington State Bar Association to confirm that Mr. Van ldour\'s \napplication was pending and processing. Declaration of Tammy \nTenny,(see attached State\'s Response to Personal Restraint \nPetition and attached documents, attached hereto). On October 26, \n2017, the Court was notified via email that Mr. Van ldour\'s \napplication for admission had been approved. Declaration of \nTammy Tenny. \nIn October of 2017, Mr. Van ldour indicated he needed a \nformal order from the Court which was provided, memorializing his \nacceptance by the Court as indigent defense counsel dated \nOctober 6, 2017, nunc pro tune to February 1, 2017. Declaration of \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n3 \n\n--- Page 8 ---\nTammy Tenny. Not until January 31, 2018, did the Court receive \nany information that there was any problem with Mr. Van ldour\'s \napplication to practice under APR 8(c).2 The Court immediately \ninstructed Mr. Van ldour to arrange to transfer his cases to other \nattorneys. Declaration of Tammy Tenny. Subsequently, the Court \nreceived a letter dated February 13, 2018, indicating Mr. Van ldour \nwas not authorized to practice in Washington courts. Declaration of \nTammy Tenny. \nThe Defendant, who was convicted on October 31, 2017 \nand sentenced on November 7, 2017, appealed his conviction, \nand it was affirmed by this Court in an unpublished opinion. \nSee State v. Lewis, supra. The Defendant then filed this personal \nrestraint petition, alleging only that he had been deprived of his \nright to counsel, relying solely on the fact that Mr. Van ldour was \nnot licensed to practice in Washington. The Defendant neither \nargued nor demonstrated that Mr. Van ldour committed any act of \nmisfeasance or nonfeasance, nor did he otherwise claim any \nprejudice therefrom. In an unpublished decision, the Division Three \nof the Court of Appeals dismissed the Defendant\'s PRP, finding \n2 For what it\'s worth, the Asotin County Prosecutor\'s office \ndid not become aware of any irregularities concerning Mr. Van \nldour\'s eligibility to represent indigent defendants until the Court \nassigned new counsel to Mr. Van ldour\'s cases in February of \n2018. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n4 \n\n--- Page 9 ---\nthat 1) the Defendant failed to allege he was prejudiced by his \nattorney\'s representation and 2) the facts set forth in the \nDefendant\'s petition failed to establish that he should be excused \nfrom showing prejudice based on a theory of structural error. Matter \nof Lewis, 37284-7-111, 2021 WL 1750935 (Div. Ill, May 4, 2021) \n( unpublished). 3 \nIV. \nDISCUSSION \nThe decision to review a Court of Appeals decision \nterminating review is discretionary. RAP 13.4(a). A petition for \nreview will be accepted by the Supreme Court only if at least one of \nfour criteria are met. RAP 13.4(b); In re Coats, 173 Wn.2d 123, \n132-33 (2011 ). Two criteria relevant to this petition are a) the \ndecision of the Court of Appeals is in conflict with a decision by the \nSupreme Court and b) the petition involves a substantial public \ninterest that should be determined by the Supreme Court. Id. \nThe Defendant argues that the Court of Appeals\' decision \nconflicts with this Court\'s decision in City of Seattle v. Ratliff. 100 \nWn.2d 212 (1983). However, the Court of Appeals\' decision here \nthis Court\'s decision in Ratliff are based on a similar legal \n3The State does not cite the unpublished decision for \nprecedent, but rather as establishing the procedural and factual \nbackground of the case. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n5 \n\n--- Page 10 ---\nframework, with the difference in outcome dictated by the \ndistinguishing facts of each case. Furthermore, because the \ndecision in this petition does not change the right to counsel, it \ndoes not represent a matter of substantial public interest. \nI. \nTHE COURT OF APPEALS DECISION DOES NOT \nCONFLICT WITH THIS COURT\'S HOLDING IN CITY OF \nSEATTLE V. RA TL/FF BECAUSE THE FACTS OF THE \nDEFENDANT\'S CASE ARE DISTINGUISHABLE IN THAT \nCOUNSEL FOR THE DEFENDANT WAS LICENSED IN AT \nLEAST ONE JURISDICTION. \nGenerally, a defendant challenging his conviction through a \nPersonal Restraint Petition (PRP) must show that the error raised \nimpacted the outcome of the trial. See, e.g., In re Personal \nRestraint of Crace, 174 Wn.2d 835 (2012); In re Pers. Restraint of \nFinstad, 177 Wn.2d 501, 506 (2013). If the defendant cannot show \nthat they were prejudiced by the error, then their petition is \ndismissed. Crace, 174 Wn.2d 847-48. An exception to the \nprejudice requirement exists where there is a structural error, i.e. \nan error which impacts the framework of the trial process. Weaver \nv. Massachusetts, -\nU.S.--, 137 S. Ct. 1899(2017). \nBoth the Sixth Amendment of the United States Constitution \nand article 1, section 22 of the state constitution guarantee the right \nto counsel in a criminal case. Const. amend VI; Const. art. 1, § 22; \nSee, e.g., In re Personal Restraint of Davis, 152 Wn.2d 647, 672, \n101 P.3d 1 (2004). With regard to this right to counsel, the state \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n6 \n\n--- Page 11 ---\nconstitution provides no more protection than the Sixth \nAmendment. State v. Medlock, 86 Wn.App. 89, 99, review denied, \n133 Wn.2d 1012 (1997). Absolute denial of counsel, such as when \nthe "attorney" was not licensed in any state at the time of \nrepresentation, does not require a showing of prejudice.4 See \nFerrara v. Keane, 806 F.Supp. 472, 474 (S.D.N.Y. 1992), affd, 2 \nF.3d 403 (2d Cir. 1993). \nHowever, lesser violations of the right to counsel do not \nrequire automatic dismissal. A defendant who is represented by a \nperson who has been licensed to practice law in any jurisdiction \nhas not suffered from a per se Sixth Amendment violation. See, \ne.g.,United States v. Mitchell, 216 F.3d 1126, 1132 (D.C. Cir. 2000) \n("We decline appellant\'s invitation to extend the per se \nineffectiveness rule beyond those cases in which a defendant is \nrepresented by a person never properly admitted to any bar.\'); \nUnited States v. Maria-Martinez, 143 F.3d 914, 917 (5th Cir. 1998) \n("the key to adequate representation is not technical license to \npractice in the jurisdiction involved, but a credential from some \nforum demonstrating the specialized knowledge of a lawyer.\'). \nA defendant who was represented by a lawyer whose \n4See, e.g.,Brecht v. Abrahamson, 507 U.S. 619, 629-30 \n(1993), citing Gideon v. Wainwright, 372 U.S. 335 (1963). \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n7 \n\n--- Page 12 ---\nlicense has been suspended must demonstrate actual prejudice in \norder to have her conviction vacated. See, e.g., United States v. \nWatson, 479 F.3d 607, 611 (8th Cir. 2007) (suspension of a state \nlicense not per se ineffective assistance of counsel); Hardamon v. \nUnited States, 319 F.3d 943,951 (7th Cir. 2003) (suspension of an \nattorney does not amount to per se ineffective assistance); State v. \nEdison, 61 Wn. App. 530, 811 P.2d 958 (1991) (defendant must \ndemonstrate actual prejudice when represented by a lawyer who \nhas been licensed but whose license was suspended). \nA defendant Who was represented by an out-of-state \nattorney who has not been admitted pro hac vice must demonstrate \nactual prejudice in order to have her conviction vacated. See, e.g., \nCole v. United States, 162 F.3d 957, 959 (7th Cir. 1998); Kieser v. \nNew York, 56 F.3d 16, 17 (2d Cir. 1995) (lawyer suspended in New \nJersey and not admitted pro hac vice in New York when case was \ntried); United States v. Costanzo, 740 F.2d 251, 258-59 (3d Cir. \n1984); Derringer v. United States, 441 F.2d 1140, 1141 (8th Cir. \n1971 ). \nThese violations are considered less serious because they \ndo not thwart the purposes of the right to counsel. As the Court of \nAppeals explained in Matter of Ayerst, \nThe limited allowance for structural error is based on \ntwo considerations. "First, courts are concerned that a \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n8 \n\n--- Page 13 ---\ndefendant have a counselor who has legal training \nand demonstrated the specialized knowledge and \nability of a lawyer." Maria-Martinez, 143 F.3d 914, \n916. This concern was emphasized in the \nWashington Supreme Court\'s decision in Ratliff, \nwhere the defendant was represented by a law \nstudent who operated outside the parameters of the \nadmission to practice rules applicable to law students. \n100 Wash.2d at 217, 667 P.2d 630. Second, there is \nconcern "an undisclosed lack of credentials may \ncreate a conflict of interest." Maria-Martinez, 143 F.3d \nat 916. This consideration is based on Judge \nFriendly\'s analysis in Solina. \n17 Wn.App.2d 356. \nUnder these applicable standards, the Court of Appeals \ncorrectly determined that, while the Defendant had been deprived \nof counsel, the deprivation was not severe enough to constitute a \nstructural error. Matter of Lewis, No. 37284-7-111, 2-3 (2021) \n(unpublished). As the Court noted, Mr. Van ldour "was and is an \nattorney, subject to state licensing regulations," and could have \nqualified to provide indigent defense representation in Washington \n[under APR 8]." Id. at 3. Thus, the Defendant was required to \ndemonstrate prejudice and, because he failed to do so, the Court \nof Appeals dismissed his PRP. Id. \nThe Court of Appeals decision neither conflicts with Ratliff \nnor undermines this Court\'s authority to regulate the practice of \nlaw. In Ratliff, this Court held that representation by a legal intern \nlicensed under APR 9 ("rule 9 intern") who has been prevented \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n9 \n\n--- Page 14 ---\nfrom complying with that rule is a per se violation of the right to \ncounsel. 100 Wn.2d at 221. There, the defendant was scheduled to \nbe tried on three charges, but his court-appointed counsel did not \nappear on the morning of trial. Id. at 213. Upon questioning the \ndefendant, the court learned that he was being represented by a \nrule 9 intern, who had not completed law school and who had \nnever been licensed to practice law in Washington or any other \njurisdiction, in a different.case. Id. at 213-14; see APR 9(8). \nDespite the fact that the rule 9 intern had no involvement with the \nmatters set for trial, the court summoned him and, after allowing \nthe him to speak with the defendant "U]ust a little bit," attempted to \nproceed with the trial. Id. at 214. When the intern objected, the. \ncourt required him over objection to proceed, but allowed the \ndefense to delay the presentation of its case until the next business \nday. Id. at 214. The court did not allow the intern time to consult his \nsupervising attorney prior to trial, as required by APR 9. Id. at 215, \n220; see APR 9(0). \nBy the time that case reached this Court, two separate \nissues had been raised. The defendant argued that he was denied \neffective assistance of counsel because the intern was given \ninsufficient time to prepare his case and was prevented from \nconsulting his supervising attorney as required by APR 9. Id. at \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n10 \n\n--- Page 15 ---\n215. In addition, the Court of Appeals, sua sponte, questioned \nwhether representation of an indigent defendant by a law student, \nwithout the presence of a supervising attorney in court, constituted \na per se violation of the right to counsel. Id. \nThis Court first addressed the constitutional challenge to \nrepresentation by rule 9 interns. In doing so, it reminded the legal \ncommunity that it has the constitutionally granted power to \ndetermine who may appear before the bar, and that this power is \nboth "well established" and "involate." Id. at 215. Pursuant to this \npower, this Court promulgated and adopted the Admission to \nPractice Rules including APR 9, which allows "lesser qualified \npersons to engage in limited practice." Id. at 216. Next, this Court \nexplained that rule 9 has several requirements, including \ncompletion of at least two-thirds of the prescribed study at an \napproved law school, maintenance of good academic standing, and \nsupervision by a qualified licensed attorney. Id. Additionally, rule 9 \ncontains specific rules outlining the conditions under which an \nintern may try cases without a supervisor present. Id. Thus, this \nCourt concluded, \n[a]lthough a legal intern is not an attorney, this court \nhas established safeguards sufficient to assure that \npersons attaining rule 9 status will be competent to \nserve the needs of their clients. In light of this court\'s \nauthority to determine which persons may practice in \nWashington state courts, and in light of the rules this \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n11 \n\n--- Page 16 ---\ncourt has promulgated to assure that persons so \npracticing are qualified, we have held that there is no \nper se impropriety in allowing legal interns to appear \nin criminal proceedings. We now hold that these \nsame safeguards are sufficient to meet the \nconstitutional requirements of counsel. \nId. at 216-217.Ultimately, this Court concluded that when rule 9 is \ncomplied with, a criminal defendant receives legal assistance \n"sufficient to meet with both state and federal constitutions." Id. at \n218. \nThis Court then turned to the facts of the defendant\'s case. \nWhereas a law student acting in compliance with rule 9 does \nqualify as counsel for sixth amendment purposes, this Court held, \n"representation by a law student intern who fails to comply with the \nconditions placed upon his or her practice does constitute an \nabsolute denial of the right to counsel which requires reversal." Id. \nat 19 (emphasis added), citing Cheatham v. State, 364 So.2d 83, \n84 {Fla.Dist.Ct.App.1978) (failure to comply with requirements that \nsupervising attorney be present and defendant be informed of \nstudent status); In re Moore, 63 III.App.3d 899, 904 (1978) (failure \nto inform court of student status and failure to obtain defendant\'s \nwritten consent); cf. People v. Masonis, 58 Mich.App. 615, 619 \n(1975) (/aw student representation did not violate right to counsel \nwhere defendant was aware of student status, general effective \nassistance of counsel standards were met, and there was no \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n12 \n\n--- Page 17 ---\nviolation of student practice rules). Additionally, the Court held that \nwhere counsel is outright denied, no prejudice need be shown. Id. \nat 220, citing People v. Felder,4 7 N.Y.2d 287, 294 (1979) \n( defendants were denied effective assistance of counsel where \neach defendant was represented in his underlying criminal action \nby a person who had not completed law school and who was not, \nand had never been, admitted to the Bar of the State or of any \nother jurisdiction). Because the record demonstrated that the law \nstudent representing the defendant was prevented from complying \nwith the requirements of APR 9 which would have otherwise \nqualified him as counsel, this Court held that there was a denial of \nthe "in the circumstances of this case." Id. at 213. \nThe Court of Appeals decision in this case does not conflict \nwith Ratliff because the holding of Ratliff is restricted to law \nstudents practicing under APR 9 or, at the very least, \nrepresentation by individuals who have never completed law school \nor been licensed as an attorney in any jurisdiction. In Ratliff, the \nquestion before this Court was whether a rule 9 intern, who had \nnever been admitted to practice in any jurisdiction, could ever \nqualify as counsel for constitutional purposes and, if so, the \nconditions required for doing so. This reading is supported by the \ncases cited by this Court in reaching its conclusions, a majority of \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n13 \n\n--- Page 18 ---\nwhich discuss the practice of law by student interns in other states. \nFurthermore, the Defendant\'s argument that the Court of \nAppeal\'s decision in his case interfere\'s with this Court\'s ability to \nregulate the practice of law in Washington state is misplaced. \nRatliff\'s discussion of this Court\'s power to regulate the practice of \nlaw is oriented towards this Court\'s constitutional power to decide \nwhen an individual may affirmatively be admitted to practice in \nWashington state. It does not necessarily follow that, in every case \nwhere an individuaf is represented by counsel out of compliance \nwith the APRs, there is an per se denial of counsel. Absent the \nrequirements of rule 9, a legal intern is comparable to a lay person \nbecause they have never been licensed to practice law in any \njurisdiction and, in many cases, have not yet completed their legal \nstudies. By contrast, an attorney admissible pro hac vice under \nAPR 8, while not licensed in Washington state, has completed their \nlegal studies and qualified as an attorney in at least one \njurisdiction. See APR 8(b).5 Even if that attorney were to later fail to \ncomply with the requirements of APR 8, he would still have legal \ntraining and specialized knowledge necessary to ensure a fair trial. \n5The irony is inescapable that this issue has been raised in \nan era where this Court suspended the bar exam and admitted \nover 500 law school graduates under diploma privilege. See \nhttps://news. bloomberglaw .com/business-and-practice/over-1-000-\nnew-lawyers-get-licenses-without-taking-bar-exam. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n14 \n\n--- Page 19 ---\nThe issue raised here is not whether Mr. Van !dour was admitted to \npractice in Washington, but rather what the remedy should be \nwhen he failed to comply with the APRs but had the legal \nknowledge and skills necessary to provide competent \nrepresentation. 6 \nBecause the holding of Ratliff is so restricted, it fits within the \nsame legal framework applied in Defendant\'s case. As discussed \nabove, courts draw a distinction between deprivation of counsel \nclaims where the defendant\'s representative is not qualified to \npractice law in any jurisdiction, and those where the representative \nis qualified to practice in at least one jurisdiction, but not the \njurisdiction where the representation occurred. In Ratliff, the \notherwise unlicensed law student intern was prevented from \ncomplying with the requirements of rule 9, and therefore was not \nqualified to practice in any jurisdiction. Thus, this Court \nappropriately declared the law student\'s representation a per se \nviolation of the right to counsel. In the Petitoner\'s matter, the Court \nof Appeals correctly identified a distinguishing fact-that Mr. Van \nldour, at the time of his representation, was qualified to practice in \nat least one jurisdiction- and appropriately required the Defendant \n6lt is worth noting that, outside the per se violation of the \nright to counsel argument, the Defendant does not argue that Mr. \nVan ldour\'s representation prejudiced him in any way. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n15 \n\n--- Page 20 ---\nto demonstrate prejudice. \nII. \nTHE COURT OF APPEALS\' DECISION DOES NOT \nREPRESENT A MATTER OF PUBLIC INTEREST \nBECAUSE IT DOES NOT CHANGE THE PUBLIC\'S RIGHT \nTO COUNSEL IN CRIMINAL PROCEEDINGS. \nAs discussed above, the Court of Appeals merely applied \n. existing law to reach its decision in the Defendant\'s matter. \nFurthermore, it did so in a manner not inconsistent with this Court\'s \nholding in Ratliff. As such, the Court of Appeals decision does not \nalter the public\'s right to counsel. Because of this, the Defendant\'s \ncase does not represent a matter of public interest and review on \nthat ground should be denied. \nIll. \nTHE RECORD DOES NOT SUPPORT DEFENDANT AND \nAMICUS\'S CLAIMS THAT EITHER THE COURT OR THE \nPROSECUTOR\'S OFFICE WERE COMPLICIT IN MR. VAN \nIDOUR\'S FAILURE TO COMPLY WITH THE APR\'S. \nIn his brief, Defendant claims that the presiding judge in his \ncase became aware that Mr. Van !dour had not been admitted to \npractice in Washington and attempted to cover up this fact. Brief of \nPetitioner at 2. He later includes a section in his brief questioning \nwhy the prosecutor\'s office did not suspect Mr. Van ldour\'s \nqualifications and suggesting that the State breached its duty as a \nrepresentative of the people. Id. at 11-13. Amicus ACLU joins in \nthis argument, alleging that the trial court and prosecutor\'s office \n"facilitated" Mr. Van ldour\'s actions. Memorandum of Amicus \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n16 \n\n--- Page 21 ---\nCuriae ACLU of Washington at 7. \nBoth parties claims that then-judge Galina covered up Mr. \nVan ldour\'s actions are unsupported by the record. Similarly, the \nclaims regarding the prosecutor\'s office are unfounded. The State \nwas not privy to Mr. Van ldour\'s licensing status, and believed that \nMr. Van ldour was practicing under APR 8 per the trial court\'s \norder. It was not until Mr. Van ldour\'s cases were reassigned that \nthe State became aware of the issue. \nV. \nCONCLUSION \nThe Court of Appeals decision in the Defendant\'s case \nneither conflicts with this Court\'s decision in Ratliff nor involves an \nissue of substantial public interest. Because no basis for review \nexists, the State respectfully requests that the Defendant\'s Petition \nfor Review be denied. \nDated this l\'fl~ day of August, 2021 . \nRespectfully submitted, \n~~~~ \n.Attorney for Responde~ \nDeputy Prosecuting Attorney for Asotin County \nP.O. Box 220 \nAsotin, Washington 99402 \n(509) 243-2061 \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n17 \n\n--- Page 22 ---\nNo. 37284-7-1 11 \nIN THE COURT OF THE APPEALS \nOF THE STATE OF WASHINGTON \nIn Re Personal Restraint Petition of: \nJUSTIN C. LEWIS, \nPetitioner. \nRESPONSE TO PERSONAL RESTRAINT PETITION \nP. 0. Box 220 \nAsotin, Washington 99402 \n(509) 243-2061 \nCURT L. LIEDKIE \nAsotin County Chief Deputy \nProsecuting Attorney \nWSBA#30371 \n\n--- Page 23 ---\nTABLE OF CONTENTS \nPage \nTABLE OF AUTHORITIES . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . ii \nI. \nSUMMARY OF ISSUES . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 1 \nII. \nSUMMARY OF ARGUMENT . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 1 \nIll. \nSTATEMENT OF THE CASE . . .... . .. . .... . . . . . . .. 2 \nIV. \nDISCUSSION . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 4 \nA. \nDESPITE IRREGULARITIES WITH REGARD TO \nTRIAL COUNSEL\'S LICENSURE, THE PETITIONER \nWAS NOT DENIED HIS RIGHT TO COUNSEL \nWHERE TRIAL COUNSEL WAS A LICENSED \nATTORNEY IN GOOD STANDING WITH THE IDAHO \nSTATE BAR.. . .. . .. . .... . . .. . .... . . . . . . .. . 6 \nV. \nCONCLUSION .. .. ...... . ....... , . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 9 \n\n--- Page 24 ---\nTABLE OF AUTHORITIES \nU.S. Supreme Court Cases \nStrickland v. Washington, 466 U.S. 668, 104 S.Ct. 2052, \n80 L.Ed.2d 674 (1984) . .... . . ....... . •. .. ... ...... 8 \nOther Federal Cases \nFerrara v. Keane, 806 F.Supp. 472 (S.D.N.Y. 1992) .. . 7, 8 \nSolina v. United States, 709 F.2d 160 (2d Cir.1983) . . . . . 7 \nState Supreme Court Cases \nIn re Pers. Restraint of Cross, 180 Wn.2d 664, \n327 P. 3d 660 (2014) ......... . .......... ... . ... 4, 5 \nIn re Pers. Restraint of Davis, 152 Wn.2d 647, \n101 P.3d 1 (2004) ...................... . . .... . . . 7 \nIn re Pers. Restraint of Elmore, 162 Wn.2d 236, \n172 P.3d 335 (2007) .................... . . .. . .. .. 6 \nIn re Pers. Restraint of Hagler, 97 Wn.2d 818, \n650 P.2d 1103 (1982) ................... ....... 4, 5 \nIn re Pers. Restraint of Music, 104 Wn.2d 189, \n704 P.2d 144 ( 1985) .............. . .... . .. . .. . ... 6 \nIn re Pers. Restraint of Rice, 118 Wn.2d 876, \n828 P.2d 1086 (1992) . .... . . . ... . .. . . . . . . . . .... . . 5 \nState Court of Appeals Cases \nIn re Pers. Restraint of Monschke, 160 Wn. App. 479, \n490, 251 P. 3d 884 (Div. II, 2010) . . . .. . . . ... ... . .... . 5 \nCourt Rules \nRAP 16.11(b) ... . . ... • . •. ..... . ...... . . . ...... 4, 9 \nii \n\n--- Page 25 ---\nI. \nISSUES \nA. \nWAS THE PETITIONER DENIED HIS RIGHT TO \nCOUNSEL WHERE TRIAL COUNSEL WAS AN \nATTORNEY IN GOOD STANDING WITH THE IDAHO \nSTATE BAR? \nII. \nARGUMENT \nA. \nDESPITE IRREGULARITIES WITH REGARD TO \nTRIAL COUNSEL\'S LICENSURE, THE PETITIONER \nWAS NOT DENIED HIS RIGHT TO COUNSEL \nWHERE TRIAL COUNSEL WAS A LICENSED \nATTORNEY IN GOOD STANDING WITH THE IDAHO \nSTATE BAR. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n1 \n\n--- Page 26 ---\nIll. STATEMENT OF THE CASE \nThe Petitioner was convicted after jury trial of Assault in the \nFirst Degree, Robbery in the First Degree, Possession of a Controlled \nSubstance (Heroin), and Possession of Drug Paraphernalia. See \nState v. Justin C. Lewis, 35775-9-111, 2019 WL 1568058 (Div. 111, Apr. \n11, 2019)(Unpublished)(attached hereto).1 The underlying facts of \nthe case are largely immaterial and need no discussion herein. \nThose facts pertinent to the issues raised, which this Court specifically \nreque·sted that the State address, are recited herein. \nThe Petitioner was represented by attorney Robert Van ldour \nthroughout the proceeding from its origin. Bond Order (04/05/17) \n(attached hereto). Mr. Van ldour was, and is still currently, a licensed \nmember of the Idaho State Bar Association, in good standing, and \nwas assigned, at that time, to provide public defense, on contract \nunder APR 8( c ). Declaration of Robert J. Van I dour ( attached hereto). \nMr. Van ldour had contracted with Asotin County and had \narranged to practice under APR 8(c), which authorizes attorneys who \nare li~ensed by other states to appear for the purposes of public \ndefense while they apply for a formal license to practice in \nWashington. DeclarationofRobertJ. Van ldour, DeclarationofVivian \n.eJy, (attached hereto). Prior to contracting with Mr. Van ldour, the \n1The State does not cite the unpublished decision for precedent, but \nrather as establishing the procedural and factual background of the case. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n2 \n\n--- Page 27 ---\nBoard of County Commissioners inquired of the Superior Court \nJudge, Scott Gallina, regarding Mr. Van ldour\'s qualifications to \ncontract for public defense without being licensed in Washington. \nDeclaration of Vivian Bly. Judge Gallina confirmed that Mr. Van ldour \nwould be eligible through APR 8(c) process to perform duties under \na contract while he applied for admission to the Washington State \nBar. Throughout the process, the Superior Court Administrator made \nperiodic efforts to contact the Washington State Bar Association to \nconfirm that Mr. Van ldour\'s application was pending and processing. \nDeclaration ofTammyTenny.(attached hereto). On October 26, 2017, \nthe Court was notified via email that Mr. Van ldour\'s application for \nadmission had been approved. Declaration of Tammy Tenny. \nIn October of 2017, Mr. Van ldour indicated he needed a \nformal order from the Court which was provided, memorializing his \nacceptance by the Court as indigent defense counsel dated October \n6, 2017, nuncpro tune to February 1, 201 7. Declaration of Tammy \nTenny. \nNot until January 31 , 2018, did the Court receive any \ninformation that there was any problem with Mr. Van ldour\'s \napplication to practice under APR 8(c).2 The Court immediately \ninstructed Mr. Van ldour to arrange to transfer his cases to other \n2 For what it\'s worth, the Asotin County Prosecutor\'s office did not \nbecome aware of any irregularities concerning Mr. Van ldour\'s eligibility to \nrepresent indigent defendants until the Court assigned new counsel to Mr. Van \nldour\'s cases in February of 2018. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n3 \n\n--- Page 28 ---\nattorneys. Declaration of Tammy Tenny. Subsequently, the Court \nreceived a letter dated February 13, 2018, indicating Mr. Van ldour \nwas not authorized to practice in Washington courts. Declaration of \nTammy Tenny. \nThe Petitioner, who was convicted on October 31, 2017 and \nsentenced on November 7, 2017, appealed his conviction, and it was \naffirmed by this Court in an unpublished opinion. See State v. Lewis, \nsupra. \nThe Petitioner then filed this personal restraint petition, \nalleging only that he had been deprived of his right to counsel, relying \nsolely on the fact that Mr. Van ldour was not licensed to practice in \nWashington. The Defendant does not argue, nor does he make any \nshowing that Mr. Van ldour committed any act of misfeasance or \nnonfeasance, nor does he otherwise claim any prejudice therefrom. \nIV. DISCUSSION \nRAP 16.11(b) authorizes dismissal of the petition if the petition \nis clearly without merit. The Respondent submits that the issues \nraised by the Petitioner are without merit and lack any basis in fact or \nin the law. \nA PRP is not a substitute for a direct appeal. In re Pers. \nRestraint of Hagler, 97 Wn.2d 818, 824, 650 P.2d 1103 (1982). \nCourts are reluctant to overturn a settled judicial decision where the \nPetitioner has had an opportunity for direct appellate review. In re \nPers. Restraint of Cross, 180 Wn.2d 664, 676, 327 P. 3d 660 (2014 ). \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n4 \n\n--- Page 29 ---\nTherefore, there are limitations on the use of a PRP to collaterally \nattack a conviction. Hagler, 97 Wn.2d at 824. A personal restraint \npetition must state with particularity the factual allegations which, if \nproven, would entitle the petitioner to relief. In re Pers. Restraint of \nRice, 118 Wn.2d 876, 886, 828 P.2d 1086 (1992). Factual allegations \nmust be supported by evidence. 118 Wn.2d at 886. If the record does \nnot support the factual allegations, then the petitioner must show \nthrough affidavits or other forms of corroboration that competent and \nadmissible evidence will establish the factual allegations. 118 Wn.2d \nat 886. Bald assertions and conclusory allegations are not sufficient. \nId. \nTo be entitled to collateral relief through a PRP the Petitioner \nmust prove the existence of error by a preponderance of the \nevidence. In re Pers. Restraint of Monschke, 160 Wn. App. 479, 490, \n251 P. 3d 884 (Div. II, 2010). Assuming error can be shown, he is \nalso required to prove prejudice. Cross, 180 Wn.2d at 676. The \nsignificance of the level of prejudice depends on the type of error \nshown. \nId. \nTo be entitled to relief for a constitutional error, a \npetitioner must also prove by a preponderance of the evidence that \nthe error caused "actual and substantial prejudice." Id. (Emphasis \nadded). This prejudice "must be determined in light of the totality of \ncircumstances," and exists only if the error "so infected petitioner\' s \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n5 \n\n--- Page 30 ---\nentire trial that the resulting conviction violates due process." In re \nPers. Restraint of Music, 104 Wn.2d 189, 191, 704 P.2d 144 (\'1985). \nTo be entitled to relief for a nonconstitutional error, a petitioner \nmust prove by a preponderance of the evidence that the error caused \n"a fundamental defect resulting in a complete miscarriage of \njustice." In re Pers. Restraint of Elmore, 162 Wn.2d 236, 251, 172 \nP.3d 335 (2007) (emphasis added). With this as the backdrop, the \nState will respond to the issue raised. \nA. \nDESPITE IRREGULARITIES. WITH REGARD TO TRIAL \nCOUNSEL\'S LICENSURE, THE PETITIONER WAS NOT \nDENIED HIS RIGHT TO COUNSEL WHERE TRIAL \nCOUNSEL WAS A LICENSED ATTORNEY IN GOOD \nSTANDING WITH THE IDAHO STATE BAR. \nThe Petitioner bases his entire claim on the fact that his trial \ncounsel, Robert Van ldour, was not licensed in Washington State. It \nis beyond dispute that Mr. Van ldour was not licenced with the \nWashington State Bar. It is, however, important to note that much of \nthe information now contained in the Bar Association\'s complaint \nagainst Mr. Van ldour was unknown to either Asotin County or the trial \ncourt. On advice from the Superior Court Judge, the County entered \ninto a contract with Mr. Van ldour, who had decades of experience \nrepresenting criminal defendants. The picture painted by the \nPetitioner of a system which was cavalier with regard to the \nPetitioner\'s right to counsel is not accurate. The Petitioner was \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n6 \n\n--- Page 31 ---\nrepresented by a veteran and capable attorney throughout the \nprocess, despite the subsequent revelations regarding counsel\'s \nfailure to obtain licensure. \nThere can be no question that a criminal defendant has the \nright to effective assistance of trial counsel under the Sixth \nAmendment to the United States Constitution and article I, section 22 \nof the Washington State Constitution. See, e.g., In re Pers. Restraint \nof Davis, 152 Wn.2d 647, 672, 101 P.3d 1 (2004). \nWhere a \ndefendant is denied counsel because the "attorney" was not licensed \nin any state, prejudice may be presumed. See Ferrara v. Keane, 806 \nF.Supp. 472, 474 {S.D.N.Y. 1992), affd, 2 F.3d 403 {2d Cir. 1993). \nHowever, this per se rule does not apply where the attorney is \nlicensed to practice but merely lacks a license in the jurisdiction where \nthe case occurs. Id. The Petitioner\'s reliance on Solina is misplaced. \nAs explained in Ferrara: \nIn support of his position, petitioner looks to the holding of \nSolina v. United States, 709 F.2d 160 (2d Cir.1983), where the \ncourt found that a per se violation of the right to counsel had \noccurred where the defendant\'s counsel had repeatedly failed \nthe New York bar examination and was not a member of any \nbar. Here, however, [trial counsel] had been a member of the \nNew Jersey bar for more than 30 years and was well \nfamiliarized with general criminal procedure because of his \nyears spent as a county prosecutor. \nFerrara, at 47 4. Here, Mr. Van ldour was licensed (for more than 30 \nyears) in Idaho, having handled hundreds of criminal defense cases. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n7 \n\n--- Page 32 ---\nEven assuming that his qualifications under APR 8(c) were lacking, \nhe was an attorney licensed to practice. As a result, there was no per \nse deprivation of counsel because Mr. Van ldour was licensed to \npractice in Idaho, and the Petitioner must therefore make a further \nshowing under Strickland v. Washington, 466 U.S. 668, 104 S.Ct. \n2052, 80 L.Ed.2d 674 (1984). \nTo establish that the right to effective assistance of counsel \nhas been violated, the Petitioner must make two showings: that \ncounsel\'s representation was deficient and that counsel\'s deficient \nrepresentation caused prejudice. Ferrara, at 474 (citing Strickland v. \nWashington, 466 U.S. at 687). The Petitioner makes no showing that \nMr. Van ldour was ineffective or that but for any deficient \nperformance, the outcome would have been different. First, the \nPetitioner points to no action or inaction on the part of Mr. Van ldour \nthat would constitute deficient performance. Further, the Petitioner, \nas evidenced by his failure to aver otherwise, cannot point to any \nscintilla of prejudice suffered as a result of any act or omission on the \npart of Mr. Van ldour. The Petitioner\'s sole claim rests on the lack of \na license to practice law in Washington. Because Mr. Van ldour was \nan attorney and licensed by another state, the Petitioner\'s per se \nclaim of deprivation of the right to an attorney fails. He further fails to \ndemonstrate His claim therefore necessarily fails. \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n8 \n\n--- Page 33 ---\nV. CONCLUSION \nIn summary, the petition filed in this matter, based upon the \nrecord, is without merit. In the best interests of justice this petition \nshould be dismissed. The Respondent respectfully requests that this \nCourt enter an order dismissing Petitioner\'s Personal Restraint \nPetition pursuant to RAP 16.11 (b ). \nDated this_ day of August, 2021. \nRespectfully submitted, \nCURT L. LIEDKIE, WSBA #30371 \nAttorney for Respondent \nDeputy Prosecuting Attorney for Asotin County \nP.O. Box 220 \nAsotin, Washington 99402 \n(509) 243~2061 \nBRIEF OF RESPONDENT \n9 \n\n--- Page 34 ---\n_, .. \nJ \n· r /~i~. · . \n. ~ -~ \n·~: \n28.11 \'APR ~ S · P . 3r •. , 1 \n. M8~5ff~£tltf (f \nSUPERIOR COURT OF WASHINGTON \nFOR ASOTIN COUNTY \nTHE STATE OF WASHINGTON, \nPlaintiff, \nV. \nJUSTIN C. LEWIS, \nDefendant. \nNO: 17-1.:.00053--02 \nBOND ORDER \n~emporary \nASOTIN COUHT%\\A \nIT IS HEREBY ORDERED that the abpve-named Defendant shall be released from the Asotin County Jail \npending trial on the following conditions pursuant to CrR 3.2: \n• \nOn personal recognizance. \n~ Bond In the amount of$ :ZJ;QGD • \nto be made by execution of: \n• \nAn unsecured appearance/perfor~ance bond. · \n• \nAn appearance.bond/perform.ance or a deposit of cash with the Clerk of the Court or other \napproved security which is ten percent (10%) of said bond. \nA surety bond or posting of cash, assuring appearance and performance of conditions. \nContact your attorney each week. AJJ V+,. ;:t"\'J,.. c \nIs hereby appointed to represent the \nDefendant \n·Make all Court appearances concerning this case. NEXT COURT APPEARANCE IS ON MONDAY, \nApril 171 201;1\' at 9:00 a.m.. \n. \n. \n~ Do not violate any of the criminal laws of this state, any other state or the United states, or any \npolitical subdivlsion thereof. \n~ Residence is restricted to: \n9?4 •ff SQiet ft Gla•ftste. ·: W.A. \n~ ,-. \'- ftA ,.,u ~,i-i ~-k..~ \n~ \nDo not associate with anyane on probation or parole: Have no contact with the complaining witness, \nother witnesses, SPECIFICALLY No Contact,· Direct, Indirect or Through Third Parties with: \nMarv \nRickman, David Rickman. Lori Evans. Michael Evans, Dons Bevins. Codie French, Robert Bevins, \nTracey Lewis, Erica Evans \n, or any other person who may be named as a witness. \n~ Do not possess or consume any \'1--beverages c:o.ntainlng alcohol, and/or Id. controlled substances \nllittftcluding marijuana, without a valid prescription from a llcensed physician. \nBOND \nORQER \nPage 1 of2 \nBenjamin C. Nichols, Prosecuting Attorney \nP. 0 . Box 220, Asotin, WA 99402 \n(509) 243-2061 \n\n--- Page 35 ---\n~ \nD \n• \nDo not possess any firearms, _knives or other dangerous weapons during-the pendency of this action. \nShall be released to the supervision of Asotin County District Court Probation Officer Nate Uhlhom, \nand comply with all directives of the Probation Office. \nAdditional conditions of release: ____________________ _ \nThe above-named Defendant is hereby committed to the custody of the sheriff to be detained. by \nsame until the above stated conditions of release have been met. \nI have read the above conditions of release and fully understand them. I agree to follow the these conditions \nand I understand that a violation of any of these conditions will lead to my arrest, may be punishable by \ncon\'.einpt of court, and-may result In my being charged Wfth the crime of bail Jumping, RCW 9A.76.170. I have \nbeen Informed and understand that the Asotin County jail charges a booking fee of fifty dollars ($50.00) which \nI must pay prior to my release if I am convicted of any of the charges herein. \nI ag~ee that during the pendency of this matter, es an absolute requirement of being released on bond, I shall \nkec;> my attorney fully advised of my current address and telephone number (if any) or any changes in same, \nand further, no more than thirty (30) days and no less·than twenty-fi\'I(! (25) days before trial date I shall make \ncontact with my attQmey of record to review the status of this matter. My failure to do any of the above shall \nresult in the immediate iS$uance of a bench warrant for my arrest after proof to the Court establishing such \nfailure. \n\' \nDATEDthis ~~ dayof\'_A..__,.,,. \n........ •l __ \n_.,20_n_. \n~~. \nOefenan \n· . \n2017-04-05T14:24:01-07:00 \n~ \n. \n. \nApproved this S \nday of ___ ~_ \n.. 1 ___ _,. 20 I ( . \nBOND \nORDER \n• t • • \nPage2of2 \nAttorney for Defendant \nWSBA# \n. \nCURT L. LIEDKIE, WSBM# 30371 \nDeputy Prosecuting Attorney \nBenjamin C. Nfchola, Proaecutfng Altomey \nP. 0. Box 220, Asotin, WA 99402 \n(509) 243-2061 \n\n--- Page 36 ---\nCOURT OF AFPE..41.S OF TriE S\'l"ATF. or: \nIn the Matter of Personal Restraint of \nROBERT L. AYERST \nPetitioner, \nWASHINGTOt-:- 01VISICN UI \n) \n) \n) \n) \n) \n) \nCourt Of Appeal No.: 369650 \nDECLARATION OF ROBERT J. VAN IDOUR \nDECLAM TION \nr have been licensed to practice law in the State of Idaho sine~ my .;dmission on S:!ptember 2S, \n1980 and have remained so until the present. In my nearly 40 years of practic~, I have handled hundreds \nof criminal defense cases. In 2017 I began re~resenting clients as a contract public defundcr in the \nCounty of Asotin, Washington pursuant to an application under APR 8 undar the supervision of il!eil P. \nCox, WSBA# 24040 and represented indigent defendants under that public defense oontract in Asotin \nCounty. I applied for admission by motion and under APRS. However my admission under APR 8 was not \ngranted under APR8. I was notified in late 2017 that my admission by motion requirements were met. \nI declare under the penatty of perjury under the laws of the State of Washington the foregoing \nstatement is true and correct \nSigned at Lewiston, Idaho on November 13, 2019. \n~CJtk~r uJL-\nDECLARATION OF \nROBERT J. VAN IDOUR \nRoi>ert J. Van uour, ISBA 2644 \nBenjamin c. Nichols, Pros~~uting A\'itomey \nPO Box 220, Asotin WA 99402; 509-243-2061 \n.. \n\n--- Page 37 ---\nIN THE COURT OF APPEALS OF THE STATE OF WASHINGTON \nDIVISION Ill \nIN RE THE PRP OF: \nJUSTIN LEWIS \nCOA NO. 37284-7-111 \nDECLARATION OF VIVIAN BLY \nCLERK OF THE BOARD \nDEC LARA Tl()N \nI am the Clerk of the Board of County Commissioners responsible .for maintaining the records and \ncommunications within the Board. \n· \nAttached are true and correct copies of emails received by the Board of County Commissioners \nconcerning Mr. RobertVan ldour\'s proposal to provide indigent public defense, including inquiries \nby the Board to Superior Court Judge Scott Gallina concerning Mr. Van ldour\'s qualifications and \nJudge_ Gallina\'s responses. \nI declare under penalty of perjury under the laws of the State of Washington the foregoing statement \nis true and correct. \nSigned at Asotin, Washington on Q\\a.,,cdJ\'-:;;.. ),.Jo::;uj \n(Date) \nDECLARATION \nOF VIVIAN BLY, CLERK OF THE BOARD \nPage 1 of 1 \nVivian Bly \nClerk of Board \nBenjamin C. Nichols, Prosecuting Attomey \nP. 0. Box 220, Asotin, WA 99402 \n(509) 243-2061 \n\n--- Page 38 ---\nIN THE COURT OF APPEALS OF THE STATE OF WASHINGTON \nDIVISION Ill \nIN RE THE PRP OF: \nJUSTIN LEWIS \nCOANO. 37284-7-111 \nDECLARATION OF TAMMY TENNY \nCOURT ADMINISTRATOR \nDECLARATION \nAsotin County Superior Court contacted Robert Vanldour via e-mail to inquire of the status of his \nlicensure, (see attached e-mail dated March 2, 2017 and follow-up e-mail dated March 17, 2017). Mr. \nVanldour\'s response was that the Admissions Department was currently processing his application. \nAfter several weeks, I called the Washington State Bar Association and spoke with Barbara Ochota \nof the Admissions Department and inquired status regarding Mr. Vanldour\'s application. Ms. Ochota stated \nthat the application was submitted and could take up to 6 months to process. \nOn September 5, 2017, I contacted Ms. Ochota via e-mail for·follow-up regarding status of Mr. \nVanldour\'s application. Ms. Ochota\'s response was that it was being reviewed by r~gulatory service counsel. \nOn October 26, 2017, Mr. Vanldour forwarded an e-mail from the WSBA stating that the application \nhad been approved and that the admissions process needed to be completed. \n· \nOn January 31, 2018, Judge Gallina received an e-mail from Mr. Vanldour advising that he had \nreceived an e-mail from Ms. Biestek of the WSBA stating that the Order of Conditional Admission was not \nsufficient basis for allowing him to practice in Superior Court. \nCommunications regarding my inquiries of the Status of Mr. Vanldour\'s application are attached as \nwell as a letter from the WSBA to Judge Gallina and Judge Gaflina\'s response. \nDECLARATION \nOF TAMMY TENNY COURT ADMINISTRATOR \nPage 1 of 1 \nBenjamin C. Nichol$, Prosecuting Attorney \nP. 0. Box 220, Asotin, WA 99402 \n(509) 243-2061 \n\n--- Page 39 ---\nIN THE SUPREME COURT OF THE STATE OF \nWASHINGTON \nIn Re Personal Restraint Petition of: \nJUSTIN C. LEWIS \nPetitioner. \nSupreme Court No: 99939-2 \nCourt of Appeal: 37284-7-111 \nDECLARATION OF SERVICE \nDECLARATION \nOn August 2, 20211 electronically mailed, through the portal, a copy of the RESPONSE TO PETITION \nFOR REVIEW in this matter to: \nLise Ellner \nliseelinerlaw@comcast.net \nI declare under penalty of perjury under the laws of the State of Washington the foregoing statement \nis true and correct. \nSigned at Asotin, Washington on August 2, 2021. \nDECLARATION \nOF SERVICE \nOffice Assistant 111 \nPage 1 of 1 \nBenjamin C. Nichols, Prosecuting Attorney \nP. 0 . Box 220, Asotin, WA 99402 \n(509) 243-2061 \n\n--- Page 40 ---\nASOTIN COUNTY PROSECUTOR\'S OFFICE\nAugust 02, 2021 - 5:55 PM\nTransmittal Information\nFiled with Court:\nSupreme Court\nAppellate Court Case Number:   99939-2\nAppellate Court Case Title:\nPersonal Restraint Petition of Justin C. Lewis\nSuperior Court Case Number:\n17-1-00053-3\nThe following documents have been uploaded:\n999392_Briefs_20210802174517SC643939_4413.pdf \n    This File Contains: \n     Briefs - Respondents \n     The Original File Name was SKMBT_36321080217590.pdf\nA copy of the uploaded files will be sent to:\nLiseellnerlaw@comcast.net\nbnichols@co.asotin.wa.us\njaimehawk@hotmail.com\njhawk@aclu-wa.org\njmidgley@aclu-wa.org\npleadings@aclu-wa.org\ntalner@aclu-wa.org\nComments:\nSender Name: Sharlene Tiller - Email: stiller@co.asotin.wa.us \n    Filing on Behalf of: Curtis Lane Liedkie - Email: cliedkie@co.asotin.wa.us (Alternate Email: )\nAddress: \n135 2nd Street\nP.O. Box 220 \nAsotin, WA, 99402 \nPhone: (509) 243-2061\nNote: The Filing Id is 20210802174517SC643939\n', 'source': 'text_input'}
