2025-07-29 22:02:59 - src.vue_api_endpoints - INFO - JSON data received: {'text': '\n--- Page 1 ---\nNO. 99939-2 \n(consol. with No. 99941-1)  \nSUPREME COURT OF THE STATE OF WASHINGTON  \nIN THE MATTER OF: \n THE PERSONAL RESTRAINT PETITION  \nOF JUSTIN C. LEWIS AND ROBERT L. AYERST  \nBRIEF OF AMICUS CURIAE WASHINGTON STATE \nOFFICE OF PUBLIC DEFENSE  \nROBERT W. FERGUSON \n   Attorney General \nJEFFREY T. EVEN, WSBA 20367 \n   Deputy Solicitor General \nOffice ID 91087 \n1125 Washington Street SE \nPO Box 40100 \nOlympia, WA 98504-0100 \n(360) 753-6200\njeffrey.even@atg.wa.gov\nFILED \nSUPREME COURT \nSTATE OF WASHINGTON \n4111/2022 8:44 AM \nBY ERIN L. LENNON \nCLERK \n\n--- Page 2 ---\n \n \ni\nTABLE OF CONTENTS \n \nI. \nINTRODUCTION ............................................................. 1 \nII. \nINTEREST OF AMICUS ................................................. 2 \nIII. \nISSUE ADDRESSED BY AMICUS ................................ 3 \nIV. STATEMENT OF THE CASE ......................................... 4 \nV. \nARGUMENT .................................................................... 4 \nA. Washington’s Rules Governing the Practice of \nLaw Determine the Outcome of This Case ................ 4 \nB. Representation by an Out of State Attorney \nRequires Reversal Without a Showing of \nPrejudice ..................................................................... 7 \nC. The Posture of These Cases as Personal Restraint \nPetitions Does Not Preclude Relief .......................... 14 \nVI. CONCLUSION ............................................................... 18 \n \n \n \n\n--- Page 3 ---\n \n \nii \nTABLE OF AUTHORITIES \n \nCases \nCity of Seattle v. Ratliff \n100 Wn.2d 212, 667 P.2d 630 (1983) ...... 1-2, 5-6, 8-11, 13-14 \nDavison v. State \n196 Wn.2d 285, 466 P.3d 231 (2020) ................................... 17 \nGideon v. Wainwright \n372 U.S. 335, 83 S. Ct. 792, 9 L. Ed. 2d 799 (1963) ............. 1 \nIn re Pers. Restraint Petition of Ayerst \n17 Wn. App. 2d 356, 486 P.3d 943 (2021) .... 4, 6-7, 11-12, 14 \nIn re Pers. Restraint Petition of Cross \n180 Wn.2d 664, 327 P.3d 660 (2014) .............................. 15-16 \nIn re Pers. Restraint Petition of Lewis \nNo. 37284-7-III, 2021 WL 1750935  \n(Wash. Ct. App. May 4, 2021) (unpublished) ........................ 4 \nSolina v. United States \n709 F.2d 160 (2d Cir. 1983) ........................................... 11, 13 \nState v. A.N.J. \n168 Wn.2d 91, 225 P.3d 956 (2010) ..................................... 17 \nState v. Jackson \n66 Wn.2d 24, 400 P.2d 774 (1965) ......................................... 1 \nState v. Sublett \n176 Wn.2d 58, 292 P.3d 715 (2012) ..................................... 14 \nState v. Yishmael \n195 Wn.2d 155, 456 P.3d 1172 (2020) ................................. 12 \n\n--- Page 4 ---\n \n \niii \nYim v. City of Seattle \n194 Wn.2d 651, 451 P.3d 675 (2019) ..................................... 5 \nRules \nAdmission to Practice Rules \nAPR 8 ................................................................................ 10 \nAPR 8(c) ......................................................................... 9-10 \nAPR 8(c)(2) ......................................................................... 9 \nRule 9 ............................................................................. 8, 10 \nRAP 16.11 ................................................................................ 15 \nRAP 16.4(c)(2) ......................................................................... 17 \nConstitutional Provisions \nConst. art. I, § 22 ........................................................................ 2 \nConst. art. IV, § 1 ....................................................................... 6 \nU.S. Const. amend. VI ............................................................ 2, 7 \nStatutes \nRCW 10.73.150 ........................................................................ 14 \nRCW 2.70.005 ............................................................................ 2 \n \n\n--- Page 5 ---\n \n \n1\nI. \nINTRODUCTION \nPetitioners Justin C. Lewis and Robert L. Ayerst both \npossessed a federal and state constitutional right to counsel at the \noutset of the cases against them. Gideon v. Wainwright, 372 U.S. \n335, 339-45, 83 S. Ct. 792, 9 L. Ed. 2d 799 (1963); State v. \nJackson, 66 Wn.2d 24, 28, 400 P.2d 774 (1965). All parties to \nthis action agree, as they must, that Mr. Lewis and Mr. Ayerst \nwere entitled to representation by a person authorized by this \nCourt to practice law. City of Seattle v. Ratliff, 100 Wn.2d 212, \n217, 667 P.2d 630 (1983). They never waived that right, but the \nAsotin County Superior Court chose to assign Robert Van Idour \nto their cases. Unknown to either Mr. Lewis or Mr. Ayerst until \nlong after their convictions, Mr. Van Idour was at no point \nauthorized to practice law in Washington. \nThese cases therefore begin with the premise that Lewis \nand Ayerst were both denied their right to counsel. The meaning  \n \n \n \n\n--- Page 6 ---\n \n \n2\nand construction of neither the Sixth Amendment to the United \nStates Constitution nor of article I, section § 22 of the \nWashington Constitution are at issue. The sole question before \nthe court, rather, is whether the denial of the right to counsel \ninvalidates the convictions entered by the Asotin County \nSuperior Court. There can be only one answer to that question, \nand this Court provided it almost 40 years ago: the absolute \ndenial of the right to counsel by appointing a person not \nauthorized to practice law in this state invalidates the criminal \nconviction. Ratliff, 100 Wn.2d at 219. \nII. \nINTEREST OF AMICUS \nThis brief is filed on behalf of the Washington State Office \nof Public Defense. The Office of Public Defense is an \nindependent judicial branch agency with a statutory duty to \n“implement the constitutional and statutory guarantees of \ncounsel and to ensure effective and efficient delivery of indigent \ndefense services funded by the state of Washington[.]”  \nRCW 2.70.005. \n\n--- Page 7 ---\n \n \n3\nThis case raises the question of whether a client who was \ndenied the representation of counsel duly authorized to practice \nlaw in Washington is entitled to relief from personal restraint on \nthat basis. The Office of Public Defense has a systemic role in \nimplementing the constitutional and statutory right to counsel in \nWashington. While the Office of Public Defense does not have \ndirect supervisory authority over trial-level public defense in \ncriminal cases, the agency has an ongoing role providing training \nand technical assistance to both public defense systems and to \nadministrators of public defense systems at the county and \nmunicipal levels. \nIII. \nISSUE ADDRESSED BY AMICUS \nDoes the assignment as a public defender of an out-of-state \nattorney who is not admitted to practice in Washington constitute \na complete denial of the constitutional right to counsel, \ninvalidating an indigent defendant’s felony conviction? \n \n \n\n--- Page 8 ---\n \n \n4\nIV. \nSTATEMENT OF THE CASE \nThe Office of Public Defense adopts the statements of the \ncase offered by the parties and by the Court of Appeals in the \ndecisions below. See In re Pers. Restraint Petition of Ayerst, 17 \nWn. App. 2d 356, 358-59, 486 P.3d 943 (2021); In re Pers. \nRestraint Petition of Lewis, No. 37284-7-III, 2021 WL 1750935, \nslip. op. at 1 (Wash. Ct. App. May 4, 2021) (unpublished).1 \nV. \nARGUMENT \nA. \nWashington’s Rules Governing the Practice of Law \nDetermine the Outcome of This Case \nLegal analysis must begin in the right place in order to \nreach the right result. It is undisputed that the attorney who \nrepresented Mr. Lewis and Mr. Ayerst was at no point authorized \nto practice law in Washington. The question presented is whether \n                                          \n1 Cited as procedural history to this case. Although citable \nas persuasive authority pursuant to CR 14.1, the opinion has no \nprecedential value, is not binding on any court, and is cited only \nas one of the decisions reviewed in this consolidated appeal. The \nreasoning employed by the Court of Appeals in the two cases \nunder review is essentially the same, and so further citations \nreference only the published opinion in In re PRP of Ayerst.  \n\n--- Page 9 ---\n \n \n5\nthis denial of their right to counsel results in the invalidation of \ntheir convictions.  \nThe State’s supplemental brief thus asks and answers the \nwrong question. The State begins from the premise that the \nWashington Constitution provides the same protections as the \nfederal constitution as to the right to counsel. Supplemental Brief \nof the State of Washington (State Br.) at 2. But it cannot be \ndenied that the right to counsel entails the right to representation \nby a person duly authorized to practice law in this state. Ratliff, \n100 Wn.2d at 219-20; see also id. at 221 (focusing on whether \nthe defendant was provided with an advocate who has attained \n“status as counsel,” which is based on compliance with the \nAdmission to Practice Rules). The State agrees. State Br. at 11. \nWhere the scope of the right is the same under state and federal \nlaw, federal cases may guide this Court’s application of that law. \nSee, e.g., Yim v. City of Seattle, 194 Wn.2d 651, 658-59, 451 P.3d \n675 (2019) (equating in substance the right against an  \n \n\n--- Page 10 ---\n \n \n6\nunconstitutional taking of property under federal and state law). \nBut that does not mean that federal cases control the outcome of \nrelated but different issues of state law. The question here is not \nwhether federal jurisprudence would excuse what happened here \nhad the cases been in federal court; the question is whether this \nCourt, as the body entrusted with oversight of the practice of law, \nwill do so. Const. art. IV, § 1. \nThis is a question of the remedy, not of the right. The State, \nlike the Court of Appeals, relies on federal authority for the \nproposition that so long as the person appointed to serve as \ncounsel was admitted to the practice of law somewhere, the \nremedy for their appointment here does not include invalidation \nof the conviction absent a showing of prejudice. State Br.  \nat 13-17; In re PRP of Ayerst, 17 Wn. App. at 360-62. \nThis reliance on federal authority fails at the outset \nbecause this Court, not the federal courts, “determines who may \nor may not appear before the bar.” Ratliff, 100 Wn.2d at 215 \n(citing Const. art. IV, § 1). The role of this Court to oversee the \n\n--- Page 11 ---\n \n \n7\npractice of law thus provides an independent and adequate state \nground for invalidating the convictions of Mr. Lewis and Ayerst \nthat goes unaddressed by the State or the court below. Beyond \ndispute, Mr. Van Idour was not authorized by this Court to \npractice law in Washington. In re PRP of Ayerst, 17 Wn. App. \n2d at 359. The legal consequence of that failure to comply with \nWashington law therefore turns upon Washington law and not \nupon federal law defining the scope of the right to counsel under \nthe Sixth Amendment.  \nThis court should reject the State’s invitation to rely on \nfederal case law and decide this case under Washington \nprecedent, which unambiguously requires reversal of Mr. Ayerst \nand Lewis’s convictions. \nB. \nRepresentation by an Out of State Attorney Requires \nReversal Without a Showing of Prejudice \nThe Court of Appeals recognized that the uncontroverted \nfacts concerning Mr. Van Idour’s appointment demonstrated a \ncomplete deprivation of the right to counsel. In re PRP of Ayerst, \n17 Wn. App. 2d at 360 (noting that Mr. Ayerst did not have \n\n--- Page 12 ---\n \n \n8\n“counsel” as that term is defined for constitutional purposes).  \nThe court below erred, however, when it failed to follow this \nCourt’s precedent as to the remedy for a failure to appoint \ncounsel who was legally authorized to practice in Washington. \nThe lower court should have followed the rule this Court \nestablished in Ratliff: The appointment of a non-Washington \nlawyer constitutes a complete denial of counsel unless that \nperson fully complies with the APRs. Ratliff, 100 Wn.2d at 219. \nThe Ratliff case is analogous to those of Mr. Lewis and \nMr. Ayerst, and required that their personal restraint petitions be \ngranted. There, this Court found a complete denial of the right to \ncounsel where the trial judge effectively precluded a Rule 9 \nintern from complying with the requirements of Admission to \nPractice Rule (APR) 9. Id. at 220. Contrary to APR 9, the intern \nwas working without the supervision of a licensed attorney with \nat least three years of experience. Id. at 218-21. This Court \nconsequently held that representation by the intern was a total \ndeprivation of the right to counsel: “[a]lthough one who is \n\n--- Page 13 ---\n \n \n9\nauthorized to practice only under certain conditions . . . may be \nconsidered ‘counsel’ for constitutional purposes, this is so only \nwhen he or she complies with those conditions.” Ratliff, 100 \nWn.2d at 218. This Court accordingly reversed Mr. Ratliff’s \nconviction, despite there being nothing in the record to suggest \nthe intern provided sub-par representation. See id. at 221. \nHere, Mr. Van Idour was an Idaho attorney without a \nlicense to practice Washington, whose practice in Washington \nwas therefore unauthorized. APR 8(c) permits attorneys licensed \nout of state to represent indigent clients “while rendering service \nin either a bar association or governmentally sponsored legal \nservices organization or in a public defender’s office or similar \nprogram providing legal services to indigents[.]” Such attorneys \nmay, “upon application and approval,” practice law in \nWashington. APR 8(c) (emphasis added). This authorization is \nconditioned on the attorney associating with an active member \nof the Bar in Washington, who “shall be the lawyer of record and \nresponsible for the conduct of the matter[.]” APR 8(c)(2). There \n\n--- Page 14 ---\n \n \n10\nis no evidence in this case Mr. Van Idour was part of any sort of \norganization responsible for providing public defense services; \nhe was practicing as an independent contractor. And no licensed \nWashington attorney served as counsel of record as APR 8(c) \nrequired. It is thus undisputed that Mr. Van Idour was practicing \nin violation of APR 8. \nWhile Mr. Van Idour violated APR 8 rather than APR 9,  \nhe was just as unauthorized to practice in Washington as the \nintern in Ratliff. In fact, Mr. Van Idour’s conduct was more \negregious than that of the intern in Ratliff. The intern in Ratliff \nrepresented the defendant under protest, Ratliff, 100 Wn.2d  \nat 214, while Mr. Van Idour accepted an indigent defense \ncontract from Asotin County Superior Court in full knowledge \nthat he was not admitted to practice in Washington. Thus, while \nthe specific APR rule at issue might have differed from Ratliff, \nthe total deprivation of counsel was the same, and the culpability  \n \n\n--- Page 15 ---\n \n \n11\nof the unauthorized attorney was greater. The Court of Appeals \nshould have applied Ratliff and granted Mr. Ayerst and  \nMr. Lewis’s personal restraint petitions. \nTo escape this result, the Court of Appeals attempted to \ndistinguish Ratliff based on Solina v. United States, 709 F.2d 160, \n164 (2d Cir. 1983), even though the Ratliff court never cited \nSolina. In re PRP of Ayerst, 17 Wn. App. 2d at 360. Solina held \nthat dismissal without a showing of prejudice is required when \nan unauthorized attorney represents a defendant because the \nunauthorized practice of law creates a conflict of interest \nbetween the attorney and client. Solina, 709 F.2d at 164. The \nconflict of interest arises because the unauthorized practitioner, \nwho is committing a crime by practicing without authorization, \n“cannot be wholly free from fear of what might happen if a \nvigorous defense should lead the prosecutor or the trial judge to \ninquire into his background and discover his lack of credentials.” \nId. Relying on Solina, the Court of Appeals held that Mr. Lewis  \n \n\n--- Page 16 ---\n \n \n12\nand Mr. Ayerst had failed to “allege[] facts suggesting Mr. Van \nIdour’s licensing problems were sufficiently severe to fall in line \nwith” the rationale for dismissal without a showing of prejudice \nin Solina. In re PRP of Ayerst, 17 Wn. App. 2d at 363. \nAs noted above, it was improper for the lower court to rely \non a federal case to distinguish controlling authority of this \nCourt. Even so, under the reasoning the Court of Appeals cited \nfrom Solina, Mr. Ayerst and Mr. Lewis are still entitled to relief. \nThe unauthorized practice of law is a strict liability crime. State \nv. Yishmael, 195 Wn.2d 155, 172, 456 P.3d 1172 (2020). As it is \nundisputed that Mr. Van Idour was practicing without \nauthorization, it is likewise undisputed that he was engaging in \ncriminal conduct while representing Mr. Ayerst and Mr. Lewis. \nIt therefore defies logic to suggest that Mr. Van Idour’s licensing \nproblems may not have been “sufficiently severe” under Solina \nto justify relief. Mr. Van Idour possessed the exact conflict of \ninterest that justified reversal without a showing of prejudice in \n\n--- Page 17 ---\n \n \n13\nSolina. The lower court cited Solina, but reached the opposite of \nthe conclusion that Solina demands. \nThis Court, not federal case law, governs who may \npractice before Washington courts, and by extension the \nconsequences when somebody does so without authorization. \nSee Ratliff, 100 Wn.2d at 215. The federal court decision in \nSolina, as well as the other federal cases the State cites, are thus \ninapplicable when the question is the consequence under state \nlaw for the representation of a criminal defendant by a person \nunauthorized under state law to practice law. \nThe rule urged by the State would render this Court’s \nsupervision of the practice of law in Washington a mere \nformality so far as indigent defense in criminal cases is \nconcerned. Admission to practice in some other jurisdiction is \nnot the same as authorization to practice here. Laws vary from \nstate to state, including in ways that may be important to a  \n \n\n--- Page 18 ---\n \n \n14\nparticular client’s case. See, e.g., State v. Sublett, 176 Wn.2d 58, \n88-89, 292 P.3d 715 (2012) (contrasting the elements of robbery \nin Washington from those in California).  \nAs such, the Court of Appeals doubly erred, first by \napplying federal precedent to distinguish controlling Washington \nprecedent in Ratliff, and second by misapplying that federal \nprecedent to reach the opposite of the conclusion it required. \nGiven the undisputed facts, the convictions of Mr. Lewis and  \nMr. Ayerst should be invalidated. Ratliff, 100 Wn.2d at 219. \nC. \nThe Posture of These Cases as Personal Restraint \nPetitions Does Not Preclude Relief \nThis case illustrates the fact that a criminal defendant may \nhave no way of knowing until long after the fact that a court has \nappointed an unqualified person to act as their counsel. In this \ncase, Mr. Van Idour’s status did not come to light until after the \ntrial. In re PRP of Ayerst, 17 Wn. App. 2d at 358-59. This means \nthat often, as here, the only vehicle for seeking relief when the \nlawyer is unauthorized to practice law in Washington could be a \nPRP. RCW 10.73.150 (describing the circumstances under \n\n--- Page 19 ---\n \n \n15\nwhich counsel is appointed); RAP 16.11 (describing the PRP \nprocess). This reality would undercut the right to counsel if an \naggrieved defendant were required to show not just unauthorized \ncounsel, but prejudice in order to show a meritorious claim. \nThe State contends that Mr. Lewis and Mr. Ayerst cannot \nenforce their right to counsel because of the procedural posture \nof these cases. This argument ignores the fact that Mr. Lewis and \nMr. Ayerst not only did not, but reasonably could not, know  \nMr. Van Idour’s unauthorized status until after their direct \nappeals. \nThe State says that on a personal restraint petition,  \nMr. Lewis and Mr. Ayerst bear the burden of showing that the \nerror below resulted in “actual and substantial prejudice.” State \nBr. 8 (quoting In re Pers. Restraint Petition of Cross, 180 Wn.2d \n664, 671, 327 P.3d 660 (2014)).2 If the State were correct as \napplied here, criminal defendants would be left without a remedy \n                                          \n2 The State’s citation to In re PRP of Cross is incorrect. \nThe State likely intended to cite page 676. \n\n--- Page 20 ---\n \n \n16\nunder these circumstances to enforce their undoubted right to \ncounsel. Fortunately for the sake of enforcing the right to \ncounsel, this Court in In re PRP of Cross went on to explain after \nthe passage the State cited that “where the petitioner has not had \na prior opportunity for judicial review, the petitioner need show \nonly that he is restrained under RAP 16.4(b) and that the restraint \nis unlawful under RAP 16.4(c).” In re PRP of Cross, 180 Wn.2d \nat 677.  \nThe context in which the unauthorized practice occurred \nalso matters when considering the remedy. The parties before the \ntrial court included, of course, the prosecutor and the defendants, \nand a superior court judge presided. Both the prosecutor and the \njudge were well positioned to inquire as to Mr. Van Idour’s \nstatus. See Pet’r’s Suppl. Br. at 2 (noting that Mr. Van Idour \nnever showed a bar number on his pleadings). Mr. Lewis and Mr. \nAyerst, in contrast, reasonably relied on none other than Mr. Van \nIdour himself to protect their interests. Given this imbalance, rote  \n \n\n--- Page 21 ---\n \n \n17\nreliance on the burden in a personal restraint petition fails to \nacknowledge the important rights at stake for criminal \ndefendants. \nThe right to counsel is too important to surrender to these \nprocedural vicissitudes. “The constitutional right to effective \nassistance of counsel is a cornerstone of ‘any meaningful modern \nconcept of ordered liberty.’” Davison v. State, 196 Wn.2d 285, \n303-04, 466 P.3d 231 (2020) (Gonzalez, J., concurring) (quoting \nState v. A.N.J., 168 Wn.2d 91, 96, 225 P.3d 956 (2010)). It \nfollows that Mr. Lewis and Mr. Ayerst were unlawfully \nrestrained within the meaning of RAP 16.4(c)(2) because their \nconvictions were obtained in violation of this constitutional right. \nWhere, as here, the facts demonstrating the constitutional \nviolation were not reasonably knowable until after the original \ncriminal case was over, such an important constitutional right \ncries out for recognition rather than for procedural obstacles. \n \n \n\n--- Page 22 ---\n \n \n18\nVI. \nCONCLUSION \nFor these reasons, Amicus Curiae the Washington State \nOffice of Public Defense respectfully asks this Court to grant the \npersonal restraint petitions of Justin C. Lewis and Robert L. \nAyerst. \n \nThis document contains 2,950 words, excluding the parts \nof the document exempted from the word count by RAP 18.17. \n \nRESPECTFULLY SUBMITTED this 11th day of April, \n2022.   \nROBERT W. FERGUSON \n   Attorney General \n \n s/ Jeffrey T. Even \nJEFFREY T. EVEN, WSBA 20367 \n   Deputy Solicitor General \nOffice ID 91087 \n1125 Washington Street SE \nPO Box 40100 \nOlympia, WA 98504-0100 \n(360) 753-6200 \njeffrey.even@atg.wa.gov \n \n \n\n--- Page 23 ---\n \n \n19\nCERTIFICATE OF SERVICE \n \nI certify, under penalty of perjury under the laws of the \nState of Washington, that the foregoing was electronically filed \nin the Washington State Supreme Court and electronically served \non the following parties, according to the Court’s protocols for \nelectronic filing and service: \nLise Ellner, WSBA 20955 \nLaw Offices of Lise Ellner \nliseellnerlaw@comcase.net \n \nBenjamin Curler Nichols \nCurtis Lane Liedkie \nJamie Kiona Young \nAsotin County Prosecutor’s \nOffice \nbnichols@co.asotin.wa.us \ncliedkie@co.asotin.wa.us \njyoung@co.asotin.wa.us \n \nJohn Midgley, WSBA 6511 \nNancy Talner, WSBA 11196 \nJaime Hawk, WSBA 35632 \nACLU of Washington Found. \njmidgley@aclu-wa.org \ntalner@aclu-wa.org \njhawk@aclu-wa.org \nRobert C. Boruchowitz, \nWSBA 4563 \nThe Defender Initiative \nrcboru@aol.com \nboruchor@seattleu.edu \n \nRobert Ayerst, DOC #291791 \nAirway Heights Corrections \nCenter \nPO Box 2049 \nAirway Heights, WA  99001 \n \nJustin Lewis, DOC #403565 \nWashington State Penitentiary \n1313 N. 13th Avenue \nWalla Walla, WA  99362 \n \n \n \n \n \nDATED this 11th day of April 2022, at Olympia, \nWashington. \n s/ Leena Vanderwood \nLEENA VANDERWOOD \n   Legal Assistant \n\n--- Page 24 ---\nSOLICITOR GENERAL OFFICE\nApril 11, 2022 - 8:44 AM\nTransmittal Information\nFiled with Court:\nSupreme Court\nAppellate Court Case Number:   99939-2\nAppellate Court Case Title:\nPersonal Restraint Petition of Justin C. Lewis\nSuperior Court Case Number:\n17-1-00053-3\nThe following documents have been uploaded:\n999392_Briefs_20220411084020SC538384_6627.pdf \n    This File Contains: \n     Briefs - Amicus Curiae \n     The Original File Name was 220411AyerstAmBr.pdf\n999392_Motion_20220411084020SC538384_6001.pdf \n    This File Contains: \n     Motion 1 - Amicus Curiae Brief \n     The Original File Name was 220411AyerstMotAmBr.pdf\nA copy of the uploaded files will be sent to:\nJYoung@co.asotin.wa.us\nLiseellnerlaw@comcast.net\nali@defensenet.org\nbnichols@co.asotin.wa.us\nboruchor@seattleu.edu\ncliedkie@co.asotin.wa.us\njaimehawk@hotmail.com\njhawk@aclu-wa.org\njmidgley@aclu-wa.org\npleadings@aclu-wa.org\nrcboru@aol.com\ntalner@aclu-wa.org\nComments:\nSender Name: Kristin Jensen - Email: kristinj@atg.wa.gov \n    Filing on Behalf of: Jeffrey Todd Even - Email: jeffrey.even@atg.wa.gov (Alternate Email:\nSGOOlyEF@atg.wa.gov)\nAddress: \nPO Box 40100\n1125 Washington St SE \nOlympia, WA, 98504-0100 \nPhone: (360) 753-4111\nNote: The Filing Id is 20220411084020SC538384\n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n• \n', 'source': 'text_input'}
